he Government has implemented any sche me to set up vocational training \ncentres to provide alternative employment to the un employed persons in the country, \nparticularly in the backward and rural regions of Maharashtra;  \n(b) if so, the State-wise details thereof, particul arly in the backward and rural regions of \nMaharashtra;  \n(c) whether the Government proposes to open more su ch training centres in the country, \nparticularly in the backward and rural regions of Maharashtra during the current year; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(DR. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the  House.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) of LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. †*279 TO BE ANSWERED ON 15.03.2021 RAI SED BY SHRI \nPRATAPRAO JADHAV REGARDING ‘VOCATIONAL TRAINING CEN TRES IN \nMAHARASHTRA’ \n \n(a) to  (d):  The Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneur ship (MSDE) is \nimplementing several schemes for imparting employab le skills through short-duration and \nlong-duration skill training. The details of vocati onal training centres in these schemes are \ngiven below: \n \n(1) MSDE is implementing the flagship scheme, Pradh an \nMantriKaushalVikasYojana(PMKVY), under which short- duration training is provided through \nthree routes, namely Short-Term Training (STT) for freshskilling of school / college drop outs \nand unemployed youth; Recognition of Prior Learning  (RPL) to recognize the existingskills \nthrough orientation; and Special Projects to addres s skilling requirements of the vulnerable \ngroups. Training under PMKVY is provided through em panelledTraining Providers / Centres . \nThe State / Union Territory (UT ) - wise number of empanelledTraining Centres under PMK VY \n2.0 (2016 -2020 ), as of 19 .01 .2021, is given at Annexure-I. \n \nMSDE has also taken the initiative to create Pradha n MantriKaushalKendras (PMKKs ) as \nvisible and aspirational model training centres in every district of India. As of 19 .01 .2021, 738 \nPMKKs have been established. The State/ UT - wise n umber of PMKKs set up (as of \n19.01.2021) are given at Annexure-II . \n \n(2) Under the scheme, Jan ShikshanSansthan (JSS ), skill training is imparted to the socio -\neconomically backward and educationally disadvantag ed groups of urban / rural population \nsuch as neo -literates, semi -literates, Scheduled Castes, Scheduled Tribes, wome n and girls, \nslum dwellers, migrant workers, etc . The number of districts of sanctioned JSS, State / UT – \nwise, is given at Annexure-III. \n \n(3) Under Craftsmen Training Scheme, long-duration skill training is imparted through \nIndustrial Training Institutes (ITIs) in 137 trades . The State/ UT – wise number of ITIs is given \nat Annexure-IV .  \n \nIn addition, the Government has approved the settin g up of Indian Institute of Skills (IISs) in \nPublic Private Partnership (PPP) mode in three loca tions in the country namely Mumbai, \nKanpur and Ahmedabad. The first batch at IIS Mumbai  has commenced training in December \n2020.  \n  \n\nAnnexure I  \n \nAnnexure-I referred to in reply to LS SQ No. †*279 to be answered on 15.03.2021 \nregarding ‘VOCATIONAL TRAINING CENTRES IN MAHARASHTRA’ \n \nState/ UT - wise number of empanelled Training Cent res(TCs) under PMKVY 2.0 (2016-\n2020) (as of 19.01.2021) \n \nSl . \nNo. \nState / UT  No . of empanelled TCs  \n1. Andaman and Nicobar Islands 8 \n2. Andhra Pradesh 471 \n3. Arunachal Pradesh 92 \n4. Assam 335 \n5. Bihar 454 \n6. Chandigarh 55 \n7. Chhattisgarh 166 \n8. Dadra and Nagar Haveli 6 \n9. Daman and Diu 12 \n10. Delhi 376 \n11. Goa 24 \n12. Gujarat 414 \n13. Haryana 1,045 \n14. Himachal Pradesh 307 \n15. Jammu and Kashmir 355 \n16. Jharkhand 210 \n17. Karnataka 273 \n18. Kerala 322 \n19. Lakshadweep 1 \n20. Madhya Pradesh 1,079 \n21 . Maharashtra  794  \n22. Manipur 