> � \u0015?> � @= �\n\u0015� \u0018\u001a\u000b\b\b\u0002\u0002\u0005 � \u0019@\u0017 � \u0015\u0014\u0019 � \u0017\u0014A � \u0015= �\n=� \u0018\u0010\f\f\r\u0012\r\u001a � \u0019\u00177 � \u0017A\u0015 � AA � \u0015? �\n?� \u0004\r\u000e\b\t\u0001\u0002\f\r\u0003\r\u0005\u000b � 7\u0014\u0017 � \u0017A? � \u0015? � \u0019@ �\n\u0019� \u0001\u0010\u0006\b\u0010\u0005 � \u0019\u00197 � =\u0019\u0015 � @7 � \u0015\u0015\u0015 �\nA� \u001b\u0005\u000b\u000e\u001a\u0006\r � =>@ � \u0019> � \u0017\u0015 � \u0017A �\n>� \u001b\u0010\u0005\u0006\u0002\u0002\u0011 � A?\u0019 � \u0017\u0017\u0015 � >@ � \u0015A �\n@� \u0006\u0004\u0011\u0011\u0002\u0005\u0004 � \u0019\u0017\u0015 � >A � \u0015? � \u0015@ �\n7� \u0012\u0005\r\u001b\r\u000e\r\u0007 � @\u00197 � \u0017\u0017A � A\u0014 � =\u0014 �\n\u0017\u0014 � \u000e\u0005\u000b\u001b\r\u001b\u0010\u0011\r\u0007 � >A? � A\u0017 � \u0015\u0017 � \u00177 �\n\u0017\u0017 � \u0003\u000b\u000e\r\u001b\u001a\r\u0012\r\b\r\u0006\r\u0007 � @7A � \u0015A? � \u0015\u0019\u0014 � \u0017= �\n\u0017\u0015 � \u0003\u000bB\u000b\r\u0006\r\u0001\r\u0005\r\u0007 � A@? � \u0017=7 � \u0015? � \u0015A �\n\u0017= � \u0013\u0004\u000e\b\t\u0001\u0002\f\r\u0003\r\u0005\u000b � ?=\u0017 � \u0017\u00197 � \u00177 � ?@ �\n�� ����� � ���� � ���� � ���� � ��� �\n\t\t\n\t\n�"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |206", "t": "Penalty for Animal Cruelty", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 206 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2ND FEBRUARY, 2021 \n \nPENALTY FOR ANIMAL CRUELTY \n206. SHRI GAUTAM GAMBHIR: \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMALHUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING       \n�������������������������������������������������������������\n be pleased to state: \n(a) whether an increase in the penalty for animal cruelty is being considered by \namending existing laws which stands at Rs. 50; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \n(DR. SANJEEV KUMAR BALYAN) \n \n(a)& (b) The Department of Animal Husbandry and Dairying, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal \nHusbandry and Dairying has been receiving number of representations from various animal \nwelfare organizations, animal lovers and persons regarding increase in current penal ty \nprescribed in the Prevention of Cruelty to Animal Act, 1960. The Department is examining \nthose representation regarding possibility of increasing the existing penalty. \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |207", "t": "Implementation of Assam Accord", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, \nCOOPERATION\n \n& FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n207\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n \n9\nTH\n \nMARCH, 2021\n \n \nSPECIAL INCENTIVES TO FARMERS FOR BUMPER CROP\n \n \n*207. \n  \nSHRI SUKHBIR SINGH BADAL: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS \nWELFARE\n  \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state\n:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether\n \nthe Government recognizes that despite GDP growth touching new lows, \nfarmers in the country have ensured record crops even during the pandemic and if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether\n \nthe Government has given any special incentives to the farmers for growing \nthe bumper crop during pandemic and saving the nation in the times of crisis and if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to give special incentives to th\ne States which have \nbeen producing foodgrains for the Central kitty like Punjab and Haryana; and \n \n \n(d)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n \n  \n(SHRI \nNARENDRA  SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n \n(a) to (\nd\n):\n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (\nd\n) OF L\nOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n  \n207\n    \nDUE FOR REPLY ON \n9\nTH\n \nMARCH, 2021\n.\n \n(a):\n \nYes sir. As per the second advance estimate for 2020\n-\n21, the country has harvested a \nrecord food grain production at 303.34 million tons during 2020\n-\n21. The record production of \nfood grains is attributed to increase an area under kharif crops during 2020 a\nt 110.65 million \nhectare against 108.90 million hectare in kharif 2019. Similarly, the area under Rabi crop \n2020\n-\n21 also increased to 64.84 million hectare as compared to 63.99 million hectare during \nRabi 2019\n-\n20. This also includes area of summer crop whi\nch has increase from 5.10 million \nhectare in 2019\n-\n20 to 6.9 million hectare in 2020\n-\n21. \n \n(b):\n \nYes sir. In view of the global pandemic, the Ministry of Home Affairs issued notification \nfor nation\n-\nwide lockdown from 23\nrd\n \nMarch, 2020. In order to ensure timely and smooth \nfunctioning of agriculture and allied sector activities during the lockdown period due to \npandemic Covid\n-\n19 on 25\nth\n \nMarch, 2020 onwards the government allowed opening of shops \nfor seeds, pesticides, manufa\ncturing and packaging units of seeds, fertilizers & pesticides & \nprocurement under Minimum Support Price Operations. On 27\nth\n \nMarch, 2020 Government \nhad allowed farmers and farm workers were allowed to do farming operations in the field, \nMandies were allowe\nd to start operating and procurement of agricultural produce was \nstarted.  Besides, the Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare (MoA&FW) had issued the \nStandard Operations procedures(SOPs) dated 18.04.2020 for farmers for their safety and \nkeeping social \ndistancing while undertaking farm related activities. These measures includes \nsensitization of field functionaries, facilitate movement of agriculture labour, agriculture \nmachinery, tools and implements for harvesting of Rabi/Summer and sowing of Kharif cr\nops \nbesides availability of Custom Hiring Centres(CHCs) machinery and services, Intra and inter\n-\nstate\n  \n \nmovement of farm \n   \nmachines besides opening of Shops of Agriculture Machinery. To \n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n\n-\n2\n-\n \nmake\n \navailability of fertilisers, authorisation to wholesalers and retailer was extended by 3 \nmonths (whose validity was expiring during Covid time).Special parcel trains were initiated for \nsmooth movement of seeds, fertilizers and horticulture produce. To fac\nilitate farmers during \nlockdown, more than 2,067 agriculture markets were made functional.\n \nKisan\n \nRath was \nlaunched in April, 2020 to facilitate farmers & traders for transport and vehicle movement of \nAgriculture/Horticulture produce. The government also en\nsured early release of financial \nbenefits to the farmers under PM\n-\nKisan Scheme. During the period of April\n-\nAugust 2020, Rs. \n40,466 Crores were released benefitting 9.36 Crores farmer families under PM\n-\nKisan \nScheme. The Government also ensured adequate cred\nit facility to the farmers, Kisan Credit \nCard drive started in February, 2020 was taken forward which helped in issuing Rs. 1.96 \nCrore KCCs. Under PMFBY, to the tune of Rs. 8,741.3 Crore were paid to 69.7 lakh farmers \nduring Lockdown period (24.03.2020 to \n07.06.2020). To build the confidence of the farmers, \nGovernment declared Minimum Support Price for Kharif & Rabi during 2020\n-\n21 before \nsowing seasons ensuring atleast 1.5 times of remunerative price as per the government \npolicy.\n \n(c) & (d):\n \nThe Department o\nf Agriculture & Farmers Welfare is already providing \nincentives to all states & UTs for producing food grains under various scheme viz. National \nFood Security Mission (NFSM), Bringing Green Revaluatio"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |208", "t": "Pharma Products in Jan Aushadhi Stores", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 208 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02nd February, 2021 \n \nPharma Products in Jan Aushadhi Stores  \n \n208.  SHRI PRATHAP SIMHA:  \nSHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM SHRANGARE:  \nSHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA:  \nSHRI SUMEDHANAND SARASWATI:  \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the total sales of pharma products through Jan Aushadhi stores during the last three years, year-\nwise;  \n(b) the total number of Jan Aushadhi stores across the country, State-wise particularly in Karnataka;  \n(c) the fresh steps taken by the Government for increasing awareness of people regarding efficacy \nand quality of Jan Aushadhi medicines, increas ing coverage with a focus on remote and rural areas \nand for ensuring the adequate availability of medicines at each of the Jan Aushadhi stores? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS  \n(SHRI D. V. SADANANDA GOWDA) \n \n(a) The total sales of pharma products through Jan Aushadhi stores during last three financial years \nare as under: \n \nSl. No. Financial Year Sales at MRP (Value in Rs. Crore) \n1. 2017-18 140.84 \n2. 2018-19 315.70 \n3. 2019-20 433.61 \n \n(b) As on 22.01.2021, 7167 Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Kendras (PMBJKs) are opened \nacross the country. Out of this, 815 PMBJKs are opened in the State of Karnataka. State/UT - wise \nlist of PMBJKs is enclosed as Annexure. \n(c)  The steps taken to increase awareness on Jan Aushadhi generic medicines include advertisements \non TV, FM Radio and Cinema, extensive use of social media platforms like Facebook, Twitter etc., \ndisplay through Auto wrappings, Bus Brandings, State Transport Bus Stands, Audio and Di gital \nScreen Advertisements at Railway Stations and by organizing workshops in various parts of the \ncountry. In order to ensure the adequate availability of medicines at the Jan Aushadhi Stores, the \nlogistics system is being strengthened. At present three  warehouses are functional a t Gurugram, \nChennai and Guwahati and f ourth one is under construction at Surat. Further, 37 distributors have \nbeen appointed across the country to support the supply of medicines to remote and rural areas. \nFurther, additional one-time incentive is given to store owners for opening of stores in aspirational \ndistricts, Himalayan, Island territories and North-Eastern States. \n \n\nAnnexure \n    \nThe statement referred to in  part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Q. No. 208   for 02.02.2021 raised \nby SHRI PRATHAP SIMHA, SHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM SHRANGARE, SHRI \nBHAGWANTH KHUBA and SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARASWATI regarding Pharma \nProducts in Jan Aushadhi Stores \n \nState / Union Territory-wise list of Janaushadhi Kendras functional across the country as \non 22.01.2021 \nSl. No. Name of the State/Union Territory \nNumber of Janaushadhi Kendras \nfunctional in the State/Union \nTerritory \n1 Andaman & Nicobar 2 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 177 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 28 \n4 Assam 76 \n5 Bihar 215 \n6 Chandigarh 7 \n7 Chhattisgarh 226 \n8 Delhi 271 \n9 Goa 9 \n10 Gujarat 479 \n11 Haryana 187 \n12 Himachal Pradesh 57 \n13 Jammu And Kashmir 89 \n14 Jharkhand 69 \n15 Karnataka 815 \n16 Kerala 688 \n17 Ladakh 3 \n18 Lakshadweep* 0 \n19 Madhya Pradesh 211 \n20 Maharashtra 552 \n21 Manipur 32 \n22 Meghalaya 13 \n23 Mizoram 22 \n24 Nagaland 15 \n25 Odisha 240 \n26 Puducherry 16 \n27 Punjab 263 \n28 Rajasthan 107 \n29 Sikkim 3 \n30 Tamil Nadu 733 \n31 Telangana 140 \n32 Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu 30 \n33 Tripura 24 \n34 Uttar Pradesh 1021 \n35 Uttarakhand 199 \n36 West Bengal 148 \nGrand Total 7167 \n* Medicines are directly supplied to administration of Union Territory of Lakshadweep. "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |209", "t": "Panchayati Raj Grants", "head": "��\n������������������� �\n�������������������������� �\n��������� �\n�������������������������� �\n���������������������� �\n��\n��������������������� �\n��������������������������� �\n��\n���������������������������������������������������������\n��\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������\n��������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������\n��\n������ �\n������������������������������ �\n��������������������������� �\n��\n���� ��� ���� ���� ��������� ������ ���� ���������� ������� ��� ��������� ��������������������\n���������������������� ��������� ������������ ������� ���� ����� �������� �� �������� ����\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������� �\n��� �\n����� ��������������\n�������� ���������������\n�������� ���������������\n�������������������������������������������� ����������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������\n��\n������������������ ��� ����� ��������� �� ��������� ��� ���� ������ ��� ������������ ��� �����\n�������������������������������\n����� ������������������������������ ������ ����������\n�����\n��������������\n�������� ��������������� ���������������� ���������������� ����������������\n�������� ���������������� ��������������� ���������������� ����������������\n�������� ��������������� ���������������� ���������������� ��\n��\n������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |21", "t": "Jobs Under DDU-GKY", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 21 \n  ANSWERED ON 02.02.2021 \n \nJOBS UNDER DDU-GKY \n \n21.  SHRI NAYAB SINGH: \nSHRI JOHN BARLA: \nSHRI HEMANT TUKARAM GODSE: \nDR. RAM SHANKAR KATHERIA: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is running the Deen Day al Upadhyay Grameen Kaushalya \nYojana (DDU-GKY) in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  whether the rural youths are being imparted tr aining under this scheme in the \ncountry including districts of Maharashtra especially in Nashik, districts of Haryana \nespecially in Kurukshetra, districts in West Bengal  especially in Alipurduar and \ndistricts of Uttar Pradesh especially Etawa; \n(c)  if so, the details of youths provided training  till date and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(d)  whether jobs/placements have been provided to these trained youths and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and \n(e)  the percentage of trained youths who have been  provided job opportunities \nalongwith the details thereof?  \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n \n(a)  Yes, Sir. Ministry of Rural Development is imp lementing Deen Dayal Upadhyaya Grameen \nKaushalyaYojana (DDU-GKY), a placement linked skill  development program for rural poor \nyouth under National Rural Livelihoods Mission (NRL M) since September, 2014. Rural Youth \nin the age group of 15-35 Years are covered under this scheme. There is a mandatory coverage of \nsocially disadvantaged groups, i.e for SC/ST-50%, M inorities- 15%, and Women-33% under \nDDU-GKY. \n       DDU-GKY follows a 3-tier implementation arch itecture in PPP mode, with the National \nUnit responsible for policy, funding of central sha re and technical support, State Rural \nLivelihood Missions (SRLMs)/ State Skills Missions responsible for funding of state share, \nimplementation and monitoring controls, and Project  Implementing Agencies (PIAs), mostly \nprivate training partners, responsible for mobilization, training and placements. \n\n \nUnder DDU-GKY, two special programs are being implemented: \ni.  ROSHNI program is being implemented for 27 left wing extremist affected areas of 9 States  \nwith mandatory residential course with 40% coverage to women candidates  \nii.  Himayat- All youth of the UT of Jammu & Kashmir an d Ladakh are covered under this \nscheme. \n       DDU-GKY follows a funding pattern of 60:40 b etween Centre and State generally. Funding \npattern in case 8 North Eastern States, Uttarakhand  and Himachal Pradesh is 90:10 between \nCentre and State. All UTs including UT of J&K and L adakh, receives 100% funding from \nCentre.   \n      DDU-GKY is working with 760 PIAs having 1638 Projects with 1792 Training centres \nacross the country. \nUnder DDU-GKY, a total of 10.63 lakh candidates hav e been trained and \n6.80 Lakh candidates have been placed since incepti on (September, 2014) till December, 2020.  \nYear-wise break-up of the same is given below:- \n \nYear  Total no. of candidates \ntrained under DDU-GKY  \nTotal no. of candidates placed \nunder DDU-GKY  \n2014-15 43,038 21,446  \n2015-16 2,36,471 1,09,512  \n2016-17 1,62,586 1,47,883  \n2017-18 1,31,527 75,787  \n2018-19 2,41,080 1,38,248  \n2019-20  2,38,336 1,50,199  \n2020-21(Till \nDecember 2020) 9,986 36,933  \nTotal  10,63,024 6,80,008  \n \n \n(b) to (e)  Yes Sir. DDU-GKY is being implemented i n various districts of Maharashtra \nincluding Nashik, districts of Haryana including in  Kurukshetra, districts in West Bengal  \nincluding Alipurduar and districts of Uttar Pradesh  including Etawa. The district-wise number of \ncandidates trained, placed and percentage of placem ent since 2014-15 till December, 2020 in \nMaharashtra, Haryana, West Bengal and Uttar Pradesh  is given at Annexure-1, Annexure-2, \nAnnexure-3 and Annexure-4 respectively. \n***** \n \n  \n \n\nAnnexure-1 \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY OF UNSTARRED QUESTION  NO 21 FOR \nREPLY IN LOK SABHA ON 02.02.2020   \nDistrict wise Implementation of DDU-GKY in Maharashtra \n \n S. No.  District Trained Placed Percentage of placed candid ates \n1 Ahmednagar 1460  774  53.01 \n2 Akola 798  364  45.61 \n3 Amaravati 3285  1863  56.71 \n4 Aurangabad 1224  602  49.18 \n5 Beed 1101  370  33.61 \n6 Bhandara 1983  1010  50.93 \n7 Buldhana 1116  575  51.52 \n8 Chandrapur 2123  963  45.36 \n9 Dhule 798  290  36.34 \n10 Gadchiroli 2682  1597  59.55 \n11 Gondia 2093  1050  50.17 \n12 Hingoli 844  394  46.68 \n13 Jalgaon 1039  442  42.54 \n14 Jalna 1680  781  46.49 \n15 Kolhapur 551  263  47.73 \n16 Latur 672  317  47.17 \n17 Nagpur 1953  1157  59.24 \n18 Nanded 1052  754  71.67 \n19 Nandurbar 1842  813  44.14 \n20 Nashik 2424  1115  46.00 \n21 Osmanabad 1080  374  34.63 \n22 Palghar 998  373  37.37 \n23 Parbhani 699  283  40.49 \n24 Pune 1352  683  50.52 \n25 Raigad 429  233  54.31 \n26 Ratnagiri 914  408  44.64 \n27 Sangli 294  124  42.18 \n28 Satara 1154  672  58.23 \n29 Sindhudurg 636  368  57.86 \n30 Solapur 21"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |210", "t": "Incentives for Plantation Crops", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, \nCOOPERATION\n \n& FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n210\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n \n9\nTH\n \nMARCH, 2021\n \n \nKCC UNDER ATMA NIRBHAR BHARAT PACKAGE\n \n \n*210. \n   \nSHRI DUSHYANT SINGH: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS \nWELFARE\n  \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state\n:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government plans to provide Kisan Credit Card (KCC) to 2.5 crore \nfarmers under Atma Nirbhar Bharat package; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details of the number of farmers who have been given these credit cards \nunder Atma Nirbhar Bharat package, State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(c) \n \nthe details of the amount of loan availed by farmers who have been given these cards \nunder Atma Nirbhar Bharat pac\nkage, State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(d)\n \n \nthe size of land\n-\nholding of the farmers to whom these cards have been distributed \nunder Atma Nirbhar Bharat package; \n \n \n(e) \n \nthe number of agriculture credit agencies available in districts in which more than 50 of \nthese cards have \nbeen distributed under Atma Nirbhar Bharat package; and \n \n \n(f) \n \nwhether the target of 2.5 crore cards include the 1.5 crore dairy farmers covered under \nthe Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying and if so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER\n \nOF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n \n  \n(SHRI NARENDRA  SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n \n(a) to (\nf\n):\n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (\nf\n) OF L\nOK SABHA \nSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.\n  \n210\n    \nDUE FOR REPLY ON \n9\nTH\n \nMARCH, 2021\n.\n \n \n(a) to (c): \n \nYes Sir. \nUnder the Atma\n \nNirbhar\n \nBharat\n \n(ANB)\n \npackage, the target is to provide \nKCC to 2.5 crore new farmers. \nAround 1.96 crore \nKCC \nhave been \nsanctioned \nto farmers \nunder ANB package\n. The \nstate\n-\nwise \ndetails of the same are \nat\n \nAnnexure.\n  \nThe\n \ndetails of the \namount of loan availed by farmers who have been given these cards under Atma\n \nNirb\nhar \nBharat package, State\n-\nwise is not available. However, loans are availed by the farmers as \nper the KCC limit sancti\noned to them based on scale of finance for the crop under cultivation, \npost harvest/household/consumption requirements, maintenance expenses of farm assets, \nas also working capital requirement for animal husbandry, dairy and fisheries. Total amount \nof Rs. \n1.87 lakh crore credit limits has been sanctioned which works out to an average \nsanctioned limit of around 0.95 lakh per farmer.\n \n(d)\n: \n \nAll farmers irrespective of the size of land,\n \npracticing Crop Husbandry\n \n(including \nsharecroppers and tenant farmers)\n \nAnim\nal Husbandry, Dairying and Fisheries\n \nare eligible for \nKCC. \n \n(e): \n \nAll the Scheduled Commercial Banks\n \n(SCBs), Cooperative Banks\n \nand \nRegional Rural \nBanks can issue KCC to the eligible farmers. Farmers can \nalso \napply through\n \nCommon \nService Centers (CSCs)\n \nfor KCC\n.\n \n(f): \n \nThe target set under Atma\n \nNirbhar Bharat package includes farmers practicing Crop \nHusbandry, Animal Husbandry, Dairying& Fisheries. \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure\n \n \nKCC issued under Atma\n \nNirbhar Bharat Package as on 26\n-\n02\n-\n2021\n \nSl. No.\n \nState/UT\n \nNumber\n \n1\n \nANDAMAN AND NICOBAR ISLANDS\n \n3,237\n \n2\n \nANDHRA PRADESH\n \n1,296,853\n \n3\n \nARUNACHAL PRADESH\n \n2,204\n \n4\n \nASSAM\n \n257,555\n \n5\n \nBIHAR\n \n395,718\n \n6\n \nCHANDIGARH\n \n6,411\n \n7\n \nCHHATTISGARH\n \n223,975\n \n8\n \nDADRA AND NAGAR HAVELI\n \n208\n \n9\n \nDAMAN AND DIU\n \n789\n \n10\n \nDELHI\n \n3,364\n \n11\n \nGOA\n \n7,901\n \n12\n \nGUJARAT\n \n1,109,583\n \n13\n \nHARYANA\n \n275,219\n \n14\n \nHIMACHAL PRADESH\n \n106,863\n \n15\n \nJAMMU AND KASHMIR\n \n657,068\n \n16\n \nJHARKHAND\n \n180,231\n \n17\n \nKARNATAKA\n \n1,330,817\n \n18\n \nKERALA\n \n745,706\n \n19\n \nLADAKH\n \n5,973\n \n20\n \nLAKSHADWEEP\n \n56\n \n21\n \nMADHYA PRADESH\n \n1,356,915\n \n22\n \nMAHARASHTRA\n \n2,956,591\n \n23\n \nMANIPUR\n \n4,485\n \n24\n \nMEGHALAYA\n \n13,543\n \n25\n \nMIZORAM\n \n14,013\n \n26\n \nNAGALAND\n \n10,067\n \n27\n \nODISHA\n \n481,364\n \n28\n \nPUDUCHERRY UT\n \n7,125\n \n29\n \nPUNJAB\n \n367,681\n \n30\n \nRAJASTHAN\n \n1,693,410\n \n31\n \nSIKKIM\n \n2,546\n \n32\n \nTAMIL NADU\n \n825,191\n \n33\n \nTELENGANA\n \n860,150\n \n34\n \nTRIPURA\n \n148,764\n \n35\n \nUTTAR \nPRADESH\n \n2,402,