 thereof and the current status thereof; \n \n(e) whether the Railways has stopped the said work suddenly through an \norder by the Board; and \n \n(f) if so, the details thereof and views of the Government to restart the \nworks? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMERCE & INDUSTRY AND CONSUMER \nAFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n                                      \n                                    (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n \n(a) & (b) A work of setting up of coach periodical overhauling and \nrefurbishment workshop at Jhansi was sanctioned in the year 2018-19. \n(c) & (d) The work was entrusted to RVNL.  \n(e) & (f) Based on developments in technology and rolling stock, the \nrequirement is under review. \n*****    "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |455", "t": "Clothings for Army and FDI in Defence", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE  \nDEPARTMENT OF MILITARY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.455 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 3rd February, 2021 \n \nCLOTHINGS FOR ARMY AND FDI IN DEFENCE \n455. SHRI RAJAN VICHARE: \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE j{kk ea=h  \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the expenditure incurred by the Government  during 2019-20 on purchase of clothes, boots \nand bulletproof jackets for soldiers; \n(b) whether sufficient quantity of ammunition is in  stock to face any war eventuality and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has any scheme for FDI in defence sector; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; \n(e) whether the Government has any plan to install  fencing along Indo-Pakistan border and if so, \nthe details thereof? \n \nA    N    S    W    E    R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE     (SHRI SHRIPAD NAIK) \nIN THE MINISTRY OF DEFENCE    \n \n(a)  The expenditure incurred by the Government during the Financial Year 2019 -20 on purchase \nof clothing and boots for soldiers  is Rs. 611.69 crore. N o procurement has been done with \nregard to Bullet Proof Jackets in the Financial Year 2019-20. \n(b)  The authorisation  and holding of ammunition is as per operational requirement of Indian \nArmy.  There is adequate stock of ammunition with the Army and it is well equipped for meeting \nany operational requirements. \n(c) & (d):  Department for Policy of Industry and Internal Trade, Ministry of Commerce & \nIndustry has allowed FDI, in defence sector, upto 74% under automatic route and above 74% \nthrough Government r oute, wherever it is likely to result in access to modern technology or for \nother reasons to be recorded.  \n(e)  Fencing on the Indo -Pakistan border exists both along the Line of Control and International \nBorder. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |456", "t": "Slag of Iron-ore", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.456 \nANSWERED ON 03.02.2021 \n  \nSLAG OF IRON-ORE \n  \n†456. DR. RAMAPATI RAM TRIPATHI: \n  \nWill the Minister of MINES be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether a large amount of slag of iron -ore is produced during mining in the \ncountry which is not suitable for use in furnace without its processing; \n \n(b) if so, whether most of the mine -holders are unwilling to establish processing \nplants for the purpose in order to save the capital expenditure and this o re-slag is \nseparated as waste resulting in loss of natural resource; \n \n(c) if so, the steps being taken to make the establishment of said plants mandatory; \nand \n \n(d) the state/UT-wise status of the compliance/implementation in this regard? \n  \n  \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF MINES, COAL AND PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n  \n(a):  No, Sir.   No slag is generated during the course of iron ore mining.  Slag is \nproduced during smelting/steel making. However, low/sub -grade ore are generated \nduring mining and the same is dumped in designated areas.  \n(b): The low/sub -grade ore contains very l ow percentage of iron which cannot be \nused directly for iron and steel making. \n(c) & (d): The present technology does not support processing of low/sub -grade iron \nore. However, the res earch & development is being carried out in \nbeneficiation/mineral processing for effective utilization of the low/sub -grade ores.  \n \n     \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |457", "t": "Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 457 TO BE ANSWERED ON 03 rd FEBRUARY, 2021 \nREGIONAL COMPREHENSIVE ECONOMIC PARTNERSHIP 457. SHRI SISIR KUMAR ADHIKARI: DR. AMAR SINGH: SHRI VINCENT H. PALA: Will the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वा�ण�य एवं उ�योग मं�ी ) be pleased to state: (a) whether the Government has estimated the potential impact of rejecting the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership (RCEP) on Indian economy and GDP ;Comprehensive Economic Partnership (RCEP) on Indian economy and GDP ;(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; (c) whether India’s rejection of RCEP will strengthen the Chinese economy by not providing a regional counter balance; and (d) if so, the reaction of the Government thereon? \nANSWER okf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h ¼Jh gjnhi flag iqjh ½ THE MINISTER OF STA TE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY (SH RI HARDEEP SINGH PURI ) (SH RI HARDEEP SINGH PURI ) \n(a) to (d): The Government held extensive consultations with the stakeholders such as the domestic industry, exporters, Export Promotion Councils, trade experts, various Ministries/Departments, academicians etc. and received inputs, which were taken into consideration while formulating India’s position in the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership (RCEP) negotiations. Accordingly, during the 3rd RCEP Leaders’ Summit held on 4 November, 2019 in Bangkok, India made it clear that the current structure of RCEP did not reflect the RCEP Guiding Principles or address the outstanding issues and concerns of India, in the light of which India did not join consensus. India’s position in RCEP was formulated to did not join consensus. India’s position in RCEP was formulated to achieve equitable outcom es, balanced ambitions and addressing domestic sensitivities of its stakeholders including small entrepreneurs. Further, India had also expressed its stated position that the Act East Policy was the bedrock of India’s economic policy and India’s engagement with ASEAN countries and other trading partners would continue. ***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |458", "t": "COVID-19 positive Defence Personnel", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE  \nDEPARTMENT OF DEFENCE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.458 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 3rd February, 2021 \n \nCOVID-19 POSITIVE DEFENCE PERSONNEL \n458. SHRI V.K.SREEKANDAN: \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE j{kk ea=h  \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether many soldiers who travelled to Delhi to take part in the Republic Day and Army Day \nparades have tested positive for COVID-19; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether protocols have been put in place for  ensuring safety of  the soldiers and conducting \nthe parade safely; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nA    N    S    W    E    R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE     (SHRI SHRIPAD NAIK) \nIN THE MINISTRY OF DEFENCE    \n \n \n(a) to (d):  As part of the Covid -19 prevention protocol, prior Covid -19 testing was done for the \nsoldiers participating in the Army Day Parade and Republic Day Parade, 2021 within 7 -10 days \nof arrival. 287 out of 2518 Army soldiers tested positive for Covid -19, all asymptomatic.  All \nthose who tested positive were quarantined at predecided  locations and repeat testing was done \nafter 10 days as per protocol.  These soldiers, who tested negative were allowed to return to the \ncamp. Accommodation and dining area were re duced to half the capacity to ensure adequate \nspacing.  All soldiers were provided with adequate face masks and hand sanitizers.  The strength \nof marching contingent was also reduced to 96 from 144 personnel to ensure interpersonal \ndistance. \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |459", "t": "Appointment of Additional Judges", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW & JUSTICE \nDEPARTMENT OF JUSTICE \n  \nLOK SABHA \n  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 459 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 03rd FEBRUARY, 2021 \n  \nAPPOINTMENT OF ADDITIONAL JUDGES \n  \n459. SHRI D. K. SURESH: \n  \nWill the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Government is taking into consideration the recommendations \nof the Economic Survey Report 2018 -19 on the appointment of additional \nJudges to achieve a highe r clearance rate and establishment of Indian Courts \nand services; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government is aware of the impact of a burdened Judiciary \non the ‘ease of doing business’ in the country where the pendency of cases is \nadding to the economic slowdown; and \n(d) if so, the steps taken by the Government to dispose of the court cases to \nbring downthe pendency in various courts? \n  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF LAW AND JUSTICE, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n(a)& (b): The Government, in consultation  with the Chief Justice, Supreme \nCourt of India, has increased the Judge strength of the Supreme Court  from 30 to \n33 Judges (excluding Chief Justice of India) w.e.f. 09 August 2019 .The sanctioned \nstrength of High Court Judges has increased from 906 in 2014 to 1080 in 2021.  \nThe strength of High Courts has been increased after receiving approvals from the \nState Governments, High Courts and Chief Justice of India . At present, there are \n4vacancies of Judges in Supreme Court and 418 vacancies of Judges in various \n\nHigh Courts. While every effort is made to fill up the existing vacancies \nexpeditiously, vacancies in High Courts do keep on arising on account of \nretirement, resignation or elevation of Judges and increase in the Judge strength. \nUnder Article 235 of the Constitution of India, the administrative control \nover the members of district and subordinate judiciary in the States vest with the \nconcerned High Court.  Further, in exercise of powers conferr ed under proviso to \nArticle 309 read with Articles 233 and 234 of the Constitution, the respective State \nGovernment, in consultation with the High Court, frames the Rules and \nRegulations regarding the issues of appointment, promotion, reservations etc. of \nJudicial Officers in the State Judicial Service.  Therefore, Central Government has \nno role in this regard. \n  \n(c)&(d): Disposal of cases in courts is within the domain of judiciary. \nGovernment has no role in disposal of cases in courts. However, the Union \nGovernment is committed to speedy disposal and reduction in pendency of cases. \nNational Mission for Justice Delivery and Legal Reforms was set up in August, \n2011 with the twin objectives of increasing access by reducing delays and arrears \nin the system and  enhancing accountability through structural changes and by \nsetting performance standards and capacities.  The Mission has been pursuing a co-\nordinated approach for phased liquidation of arrears and pendency in judicial \nadministration, which, inter-alia, involves better infrastructure for courts including \ncomputerization, increase in strength of subordinate judiciary, policy and \nlegislative measures in the areas prone to excessive litigation, re -engineering of \ncourt procedure for quick disposal of cases and  emphasis on human resource \ndevelopment. The major steps taken during the last five years under various \ninitiatives are as follows: \n(i) Improving infrastructure for Judicial Officers of District and Subordinate \nCourts: As on date, Rs. 8,288.30 crores have been released since the inception \nof the Centrally Sponsored Scheme (CSS) for Development of Infrastructure \n\nFacilities for Judiciary in 1993 -94. The number of court halls has increased \nfrom 15,818 as on 30.06.2014 to 20,062 as on 28.01.2021  and number of \nresidential units has increased from 10,211 as on 30.06.2014 to 17,736 as on \n28.01.2021 under this scheme. In addition, 2,808 court halls and 1,843 \nresidential units are under construction.  \n(ii) Leveraging Information and Communication Technology (ICT) for improved  \njustice delivery : Government has been implementing the e -Courts Mission \nMode Project throughout the country for Information and Communication \nTechnology enablement of district and subordinate courts.  Number of \ncomputerized District & Subordinate courts h as increased from 13,672 (in \n2014) to 18,735 as on 28.01.2021, registering an increase of 5,063.   New and \nuser-friendly version of Case Information Software has been developed and \ndeployed at all the computerized District and Subordinate Courts.  All \nstakeholders including Judicial Officers can access information relating to \njudicial proceedings/decisions of computerized District & Subordinate Courts \nand High Courts on the National Judicial Data Grid (NJDG). As on 01.01.2021, \nlitigants can access case statu s of over 17.90 crore cases and 13.36 crore \norder/judgements pertaining to the"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |460", "t": "Handing over of Coal Mines to Private Companies", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n    MINISTRY OF COAL \nLOK SABHA \n  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 460 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2021 \n  \nHanding over of Coal Mines to Private Companies \n  \n460. SHRI UTTAM KUMAR REDDY NALAMADA: \n  \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)       whether Singareni Collieries Ltd. which is a joint venture of Government of \nIndia and Telangana Government has handed over any mines to private companies \nand if so, the details thereof; \n          (b)       the reasons for these mines being handed over to private companies; \n(c)        the details of amount of coal that has been mined by private companies from \nthe mines handed over by Singareni Collieries Ltd., year wise since the mines have \nbeen handed over; and \n(d)       the total amount of coal production by Singareni Collier ies Ltd. in the last ten \nyears, year wise?  \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS, COAL AND MINES \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n  \n  \n (a):                No mines of SCCL are handed over to private companies. \n(b) :               Not applicable in view of answer given to part (a) above. \n(c):               Not applicable in view of answer given to part (a) above. \n(d) :               The details of coal produced by Singareni Collieries Ltd. during last ten years, \nyear wise are as below: \n  \nYear 2010-11 \n2011- \n12 \n2012-1\n3 \n2013- 1\n4 \n2014- 1\n5 \n2015- 1\n6 2016-17 2017-18 \n2018- \n19 \n2019- \n20 \nProducti\non  \n(Million \nTonnes) 51.33 52.21 53.19 50.47 52.54 60.38 61.34 62.01 64.4 64.04 \n  \n  \n  \n            ***** \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |461", "t": "Construction of Toilets", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI  \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.461 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 4.2.2021 \n \nConstruction of Toilets \n \n†461 . SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISRA TENI: \n \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the total number of toilets constructed so far unde r the “Swachh Bharat Mission (Rural)” in \nthe country; \n \n(b) whether any target has been set up by the Government for the years 2020-22; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof, Block/district/state-wise? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \nJAL SHAKTI \n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA) \n \n \n(a)  As per the data reported by the States/UTs on the  online Integrated Management \nInformation System of Swachh Bharat Mission (Grameen) [SBM(G)], 10.75 crore Individual \nhousehold latrines (IHHLs) have been constructed under S BM(G) since the launch of the \nscheme on 2 nd  October, 2014. \n \n(b) to (c)  SBM(G) is a demand driven scheme, hence no pre-fixed targets can be set. \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |462", "t": "CMRL", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 462 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nCMRL \n \nNO.  462  DR. T. SUMATHY (a) THAMIZHACHI THANGAPAND IAN:  \n     \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is facing any challenge in e xpediting phaseII, \ncorridor 3 and corridor 4 of the Chennai Metro Rail Limited (CMRL);  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof including the amount all ocated, disbursed and \nutilized so far; \n(c)  whether the Government has allowed CMRL to mobilize  funds from any \nprivate players or foreign financial institutions; and \n(d)  if so, the details thereof including the expected t ime of completion of \nthe two corridors? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \n MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a)  Chennai Metro Rail Project Phase-II is at present b eing implemented as \na State Sector Project.  \n(b)  Does not arise. \n(c)  & (d) The proposal for Japan International Cooperat ion Agency (JICA) \nloan for ₹ 20,196 crore (equivalent to 357,476 mill ion Japanese Yen) for \nthe project as a State Sector Project was supported  by the Ministry. \nLoan agreement for Tranche I for the project was si gned on 21.12.2018 \nwith JICA for 75.519 billion yen. Also, different c omponents of the \nproject have been posed to Asian Development Bank ( ADB), Asian \nInfrastructure Investment Bank (AIIB) and New Devel opment Bank \n(NDB) for external assistance as a State Sector Project. \n \nAs per Detailed Project Report, the two corridors a re to be \ncompleted by December2026. \n*****  P "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |463", "t": "Metro Rail Services", "head": "O.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 463 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \nMETRO RAIL SERVICES \nNO. 463. SHRI SHRIMATI RANJANBEN DHANANJAY BHATT: \n   \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n(a)  whether the Government is considering to introduce metro \nrail services in various cities of the country as p er the \nrequirement and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  whether the Government is also considering to intro duce \nmetro rail services in various cities of Gujarat; \n \n(c)     if so, the details thereof including the na mes of the cities \nproposed in this regard along with the time by whic h the \nsaid service is likely to be introduced in the said cities; and \n \n(d)  if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n(a)  Urban transport, which is an integral part of urban development, \nis a State subject. States/Union Territories (UTs) are responsible for  \nContd. .2/-  \n\n-2- \n \ndevelopment of urban transport infrastructure inclu ding metro rail \nprojects in their cities. The Central Government co nsiders financial \nassistance for metro rail projects in the cities or  urban agglomerates \nsubject to feasibility of the project and availabil ity of resources, as \nand when posed by the concerned State/UTs. \n(b to d) Metro rail projects have been approved for  cities of \nGujarat as detailed below: \nS.No \n Name of the \nProject \nName of the \ncities \nLength \n(in \nKms) \nSanctioned/Expected \nDate of Completion \nof Project \n1 Ahmedabad \nMetro Rail \nProject (Phase-\nI) \nAhmedabad \n& \nGandhinagar \n40.23 The stretch of 6.5 \nkms from Vastral \nGam to Apparel Park \n \nis operational since \nMarch 2019. Rest of \nthe project is \nexpected to be \ncompleted by June \n2022 in phases. \n2 Ahmedabad \nMetro Rail \nProject (Phase-\nII) \n28.254 \n March 2024 \n3 Surat Metro Rail \nProject \nSurat 40.35 June 2024 \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |464", "t": "Green National Highway Corridors", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nADMITTED UNSTARRED QUESTION No. 464 \nANSWERED ON 4TH FEBRUARY, 2021 \n \nGREEN NATIONAL HIGHWAY CORRIDORS \n \n464. SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE:  \nSHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE:  \nDR. DNV SENTHILKUMAR S.:  \nDR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE:  \nDR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE:  \nSHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA: \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n \n be pleased to state: \n (a) the main objective of developing green national highway \ncorridors in the country; \n \n(b) whether the Government has signed loan pact with the World \nBank to develop safe and green national highway corridors in \ndifferent parts of the country;  \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof along with the main terms and \nconditions of the loan; and  \n \n(d) the States that have been identified for these corridors; and  \n \n(e) whether the said project aims to generate job opportunities for \nwomen by training women led m icro-enterprises as well as women \ncollectives to implement green technologies in these corridors and \nif so, the steps taken by the Government in this regard; and  \n \n(f) the other steps taken by the Government to construct more such \ncorridors by integrating t echnology design such as local and \n\nmarginal materials, industrial byproducts and other bioengineering \nsolutions? \n \nANSWER \n \n  THE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n      (SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n \n \n(a) The objective of the project is to demonstrate safe and green \nNational Highway corridors in selected States and enhance the \ninstitutional capacity of MoRTH in mainstreaming safety and green \ntechnologies. The key aspect of the project is  to focus on climate \nresilience, limiting  the use of natural resources for road \nconstruction by use of local, marginal materials, plastic waste and \nfly ash as well as adoption of environment sustainable green \ntechnologies for design of pavements and embankments. \n \n (b) to (c) The Government has signed loan pact with the World Bank \nto develop safe and Green National Highway C orridor Project \n(GNHCP). The main terms and conditions of the loan  are as per \nannexure. \n \n(d) The project include upgradation of about 781 km length of \nvarious National Highways passing through the states of Rajasthan, \nHimachal Pradesh, Uttar Pradesh and Andhra Pradesh. \n \n(e) The said project will indirectly create job opportunities for local \npeople. \n \n(f) The project could serve as model for the upcoming National \nHighways Projects. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (B) & (C) OF THE LOK \nSABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 464  FOR ANSWER ON 4 TH \nFEBRUARY, 2021 ASKED BY SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE, SHRI SUNIL \nDATTATRAY TATKARE,  DR. DNV SENTHILKUMAR S., DR. AMOL \nRAMSING KOLHE,DR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE AND SHRI \nKULDEEP RAI SHARMA MEMBERS OF PARLIAMENT (LS) \nREGARDING GREEN NATIONAL HIGHWAY CORRIDORS. \n \nTHE DETAILS OF THE MAIN TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF THE LOAN \nWITH WORLD BANK: - \n \n Total Project Cost: Rs. 7662.47 crore [Civil Construction Cost = \nRs. 4701.17 crore] \n World Bank share in total cost - Rs. 3500 crore, {US$ 500 \nMillion} \n Grace period- 5 years \n Variable Spread option is opted as it has been lesser then that \nfixed spread in the recent years. 18.5 years period has been \npreferred considering overall dept profile. \n Status of GoI Approval : Approved in a CCEA meeting held on \n13.03.2020. Loan Agreement signed with World Bank on \n22.12.2020. \n \nDescription \nEstimated \nCost in Rs. \nCrores \nWorld Bank Share \nin Rs. Crores \nComponent A ( Green Highway \nCorridor Improvement & \nMaintenance Component)  \n7005.21 2974.2 \nComponent B ( Institutional \nCapacity Enhancement Component 177 141.6 \nComponent C ( Road Safety \nComponent) 413 330.4 \nComponent D  ( Research and \nDevelopment Component) 67.26 53.8 \nTotal Cost of Components \n(A+B+C+D) 7662.47 3500 \nLoan Component for GNHCP - $500 Million \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |465", "t": "Deep Sea Port", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.465\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 4TH FEBRUARY, 2021\nDEEP SEA PORT\n465. SHRI SISIR KUMAR ADHIKARI:\nwiil the Minister of PoRTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS be pleased to state:\nTf,fr, +d qfu{fa 3ik sE.qrri }i*\n(a) whether the Government proposes to set up deep sea port in Great Nicobar lsland\nand also at TaiPur of West Bengal;\n(b) if so, whether it is a fact that transshipment zone at Tajpur costs far less than the\nestimated costs of new deep sea port of Nicobar Island' and\n(c) if so, the comparative study report of both projects therein?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\n(SHRI MANSUKH MANDAVIYA)\n(a) There is no proposal for setting up a port at Ta.ipur, West Bengal by Government\nof lndia. However, Andaman and Nicobar administration envisages setting up a\nContainer Transshipment Port at South Bay in Great Nicobar lsland'\n(b)&(c) Does not arise."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |467", "t": "Profile of Beneficiaries under PM SVANidhi", "head": "O.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 467 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nPROFILE OF BENEFICIARIES UNDER PM SVANidhi \n \n         NO. 467 SHRI PARBATBHAI SAVABHAI PATEL: \n   SHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA: \n \n         Will the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFF AIRS be pleased to \nstate:- \n \na)  whether the Government has started preparing social  and \nfinancial profile of beneficiaries of PM Street Ven dor’s \nAtmaNirbhar Nidhi (PM SVANidhi) Scheme and if so, t he details \nthereof;  \nb)  whether benefits of various Centrally sponsored sch emes are \nlikely to be provided for overall socioeconomic upl iftment \nthrough these profiles;  \nc)  if so, the details thereof; and  \nd)  whether financial upliftment benefits are also like ly to accrue to \nthe general public of Gujarat and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF \nTHE MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a)  Yes, Sir. Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs has  launched the \nprogram of socio-economic profiling of PM Street Ve ndor’s \nAtmaNirbhar Nidhi (PM SVANidhi) beneficiaries and t heir families in \n125 Urban Local Bodies (ULBs), selected for saturat ion in the first \nphaseon 4th January 2021, as an additional component of the Scheme. \nThe socio-economic profile so created aims to asses s their potential \neligibility for various Central Government welfare schemes and \nfacilitate linkages to these, for their holistic de velopment and socio-\neconomic upliftment.  \n \n \nContd…/2 \n\n-2- \n \n(b)&(c) Yes, Sir. The benefits of the Central Gover nment Schemes, \nnamely, Pradhan Mantri Jeeban Jyoti Bima Yojana, PM  Suraksha Bima \nYojana, Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana, Registratio n under Building \nand Other Constructions Workers (Regulation of Empl oyment and \nConditions of Service) Act (BOCW), Pradhan Mantri S hram Yogi \nMaandhan Yojana, National Food Security Act (NFSA) portability \nbenefits - One Nation One Ration Card (ONORC), Jana ni Suraksha \nYojana and Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana (PMM VY), are to be \nprovided to eligible PM SVANidhi beneficiaries and their family \nmembers. \n(d)  This program is only for PM SVANidhi beneficia ries and their \nfamily members of 125 ULBs across the country which  includes \nRajkot, Jamnagar, Junagarh, Vadodara, Gandhinagar, Anand and \nNadiad ULBs of Gujarat. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |468", "t": "Allocation of Funds for Minority Programmes", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA  \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 468 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \nALLOCATION OF FUNDS FOR MINORITY PROGRAMMES \n \n468. SHRIMATI NUSRAT JAHAN RUHI: \n          DR. SUBHAS SARKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of funds allocated, released and spent/utilized under various welfare \nschemes/programmes for the minorities during the last five years, States/scheme-wise; \n(b) the amount spent on the scholarship of the students of minority communities; \n(c) the number of students benefited from such scholarship during the said period; \n(d) the classification of scholarship beneficiaries among six religious minorities during \n2015 to 2020 viz. Muslims, Christians, Sikhs, Buddhists, Jains and Parsis; and \n(e) the details of beneficiaries under above schemes/programmes in the country \nincluding West Bengal during the above period? \n \n  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS  \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n \n(a):        Scheme-wise details of the  funds allocated and released/utilised under various \nschemes to meet the scheme objectives during the last five years i.e. 2015 -16 to 2019-\n20 is  at Annexure. No state-wise allocation is done in the Budget.  \n \n(b) & (c ): Since 2014 -15 to till date, more than 4 crore scholarships  have been \n\nsanctioned to the students belonging to economically weaker sections of minority \ncommunities, and Rs.11726.45 crores have been released to the beneficiaries. An \namount of Rs.  Rs.9223.68 crores has been spent for providing scholarship to about \n3.06 crore beneficiaries under various scholarship schemes of the Ministry during the \nperiod from 2015 -16 to 2019 -20, o ut of which about 54% scholarships have been \nawarded to minority girl students. \n \n(d):  Details of community -wise number of beneficiaries under scholarship schemes \nduring the last five years are given below: \n \n               Name of Minority Community \nMuslims Christians Sikhs Parsis Jains Buddhists \nTotal No. of \nScholarships \nfrom 2015-16 \nto 2019-20 \n2,33,15,751 36,04,499 25,41,879 4,562 4,08,415 7,44,440 \n \n(e). During the last five years total 3.19 crore beneficiaries have been benefitted under \nvarious schemes of the Ministry out of which 50.96 lakh  beneficiaries belong to West \nBengal. \nUnder Pradhan Mantri Jan Vikas Karykaram (PMJVK ), the Ministry accorded approval \nfor plans/projects of the State of West Bengal having total project cost of Rs. 2514.6136 \ncrore and central share of Rs. 1629.62 crore. A n amount of Rs. 1680.1977 crore has \nbeen released by the Ministry as installments of Central Share. \nThe projects approved by the Ministry include  School Buildings (63), Additional Class \nRooms (8627), ACR Blocks (107), Cyber Gram (170005), Hostels (161), ITI Buildings \n(15), IAY (6517),Skill Centres (4), Health Projects (342), Working Women Hostel (1), \nDrinking Water Supply (5779), Anganwadi Centres ( 2765), Sadhbhav Mandaps (151), \nTeaching Aid (169), Market Sheds (138), Toilets Blocks (2842) etc. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure: \nAnnexure referred in reply to Part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 468 \nfor answer on 04.02.2021 \nScheme-wise details of Budget Allocation and Expenditure since 2015-16 \nto 2019-20 \nS.No. Schemes B.E. \n(Rs. in Crores) \nExpenditure \n(Rs. in Crores) \n1 Pre-Matric Scholarship 5121.40 \n 5210.82 \n2 Post-Matric Scholarship 2868.11 2103.32 \n3 Merit-cum-Means 1951.97 1471.13 \n4 Maulana Azad National \nFellowship 537.83 498.16 \n5 Interest subsidy on \neducational loans on \noverseas studies 70.19 84.58 \n6 Free Coaching and Allied \nschemes 287.00 189.04 \n7 Support for students \nclearing Prelims- 40.00 28.89 \n8 Skill Development \nInitiative 1027.45 947.96 \n9 USTTAD (Hunar Haat) 139.01 144.21 \n10 Nai Manzil (skill to 8th to \n10th) 610.95 339.87 \n11 Equity of NMDFC 695.02 755.00 \n12 Leadership development \nof Minority Women 75.00 65.86 \n13 Scheme for Containing \nPopulation Decline of \nSmall Minority community 14.00 13.58 \n14 Hamari Dharohar - 47.01 19.48 \n15 Research/ Studies, \nMonitoring, Evaluation \nand Publicity 255.25 174.40 \n16 Pradhan Mantri Jan Vikas \nKaryakaram \n6366.64 6255.53 \n \n17 Maulana Azad Education \nFoundation - 554.01 412.50 \n18 Grants to SCAs of \nNMDFC - 10.00 7.50 \n19 Quami Waqf Board \nTariquatti Scheme 70.14 49.09 \n20 Sahari Waqf Sampati \nVikas Yojana 15.86 14.93 \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |469", "t": "Introduction of NETC through FASTag", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 469 \nANSWERED ON 4TH FEBRUARY, 2021 \nINTRODUCTION OF NETC THROUGH FASTAG \n469. SHRI DHANUSH M. KUMAR: \n SHRI GAJANAN KIRTIKAR: \n SHRI GAUTHAM SIGAMANI PON: \n SHRI C.N. ANNADURAI: \n SHRI SELVAM G.: \n  Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nlM+d ifjogu vkSj jktekxZ ea=h \nbe pleased to state: \n(a)  the  objectives  for  introduction  of  National Electronic Toll \nCollection (NETC) through FASTag; \n(b) whether the Government is considering to extend the deadline for 100 \npercent implementation of FASTag system  of  toll  collection  and  if  so,  \nthe  details  thereof; \n(c)  the  measures  taken  by  the  Government  to discourage  cash  \ntransactions  at  toll  plazas; \n(d)  the  number  of  FASTags  issued  during  the  last two years in the \nState of Tamil Nadu and the challenges faced  by  the Government  to  \nimplement FASTag; \n(e)  whether  the  Government  has  made  any comparative analysis of the \ncollection of toll before and after implementation of FASTags; \n(f)  if  so,  the  details  thereof;  and \n(h)  the  steps  taken  by  the  Government  to  resolve the difficulties in \nthe implementation of FASTag? \n \n \n \n\nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) National Electronic Toll Collection (NETC) through FASTag has been \nimplemented on pan India basis to reduce waiting time, remove \nbottlenecks and ensure seamless movement of traffic at fee plazas by \nallowing electronic payment of user fees on National Highways. \n(b) There is no such proposal under consideration.   \n(c) To discourage cash t ransactions, Government, vide Gazette \nNotification no. GSR 523( E) dated 24.08.2020 , notified that fee payable \ntowards discounts as under sub -rules (2), (3) and (3A) of rule 9  of the \nNational Highways Fee (Determination of Rates and Collection) Rules, \n2008, shall be paid through pre -paid instruments, smart card or through \nFASTag or on board unit (transponder) or any other such device. \n(d) As on 12.01.2021 , a total number of 18,64,115  FASTags have been \nissued in the state of Tamil Nadu as per Vehicle Registration Number. \n(e) &(f) After the implementation of FASTag in phased manner in \nDecember, 2019, waiting time in FASTag lanes has significantly reduced , \nwhich will further improve as the system gets more operationalised. With \na monthly fee collection of Rs 2,088.26 Crore through FASTag in \nDecember 2020, FASTag penetration has reached 73.36%, as compared \nto 44.31% in December 2019.  \n(h) Citizens are being  informed about  benefits of adopting \nFASTag through various platforms such as social media, print media, \nhoardings and advertisements etc.  Multiple channels and Point of Sale \n(PoS) ha ve been operating , including e -commerce platforms for easy \navailability of FASTag to customers. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |47", "t": "Reduction of Food Subsidy Bill", "head": "GOVERNMET{T OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF GOilSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & pUBLtC DISTRIBUTION\nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 47\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2\"d Februaryr2