30  \nIchalkaranji Total    74.68  \nJalgaon \n  \n  \nOthers(Parks)  4.49  \nSewerage and Septage Management  195.68  \nWater Supply  253.25  \nJalgaon Total    453.42  \nJalna \n  \nOthers(Parks)  2.93  \nSewerage and Septage Management  68.59  \nJalna Total    71.52  \nKalyan Dombivali \n  \n  \nOthers(Parks)  4.50  \nSewerage and Septage Management  286.20  \nWater Supply  194.20  \nKalyan Dombivali Total    484.90  \nKolhapur \n  \n  \nOthers(Parks)  4.50"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |502", "t": "Status of Minority Institutions", "head": "                              GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n \n                                                          LOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.502 \n \n     TO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \nSTATUS OF MINORITY INSTITUTIONS \n502. SHRI ANTO ANTONY:  \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether  the Government has evaluated socio -cultural, educational and economic \ncontributions of the institutions run by the minority communities of the country and if so, \nthe details thereof;  \n(b) whether the Government is being represented by several minority gro ups explaining \nthe hurdles caused by several restrictions brought during the last seven years and if so, \nthe details thereof;  \n(c) whether the Government is being appraised of the hardships being faced by minority \ninstitutions by any of the States;  \n(d) if so, steps taken to mitigate the hardships of the minority institutions;  \n(e) whether the Government would initiate process of discussions involving the State \nGovernments, the minority groups of each State for the facilitation of uninterrupted \nexecution of charity, health service delivery and educational projects run by the minority \ninstitutions and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a) & (b) :    No, Sir. \n(c) to  (e): On receipt of any complaint rega rding hardship being faced by Minority \nEducational Institutions of any State National Commission for Minority Educational \nInstitutions (NCMEI), under administrative control of Ministry of Education, passes \nappropriate order. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |503", "t": "Support to Artisans Involved in Agarbatti Manufacturing", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 503\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021\n \n \nSUPPORT TO ARTISANS INVOLVED IN AGARBATTI MANUFACTURING\n \n \n503.\n \n \nMS. PRATIMA BHOUMIK:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL \nAND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government has been paying special attention/support to Artisans, \ninvolved in Agarbatti making and if so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the Government has issued any special package for th\ne Artisans who are \ninvolved in Agarbatti making business in North\n-\nEastern region especially for Tripura State \nand if so, the details thereof and if not, whether the Government would consider projects \nreceived from any other State in this regard and if so, \nthe details thereof; and \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government has prepared any roadmap for the export of Agarbati, if so, \nthe details thereof and if not, the reason therefor?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NITIN GADKARI)\n \n \n(a):\n \n \nYes Sir. Government of India, Ministry of \nMicro, Small and Medium Enterprises \n(\nMSME\n)\n \nhas issued ‘Operational Guidelines on “Agarbatti Project”, under “Gramodyog \nVikas Yojana (GVY)” Scheme, to be implemented through the Khadi and Village Industries \nCommissi\non (KVIC) on 04.09.2020, to support artisans involved in making of Agarbatti \nthrough training, hand\n-\nholding, financing etc. As per this, 500 pedal operated and 400 \nautomatic Agarbatti making machines, along with 80 mixing machines, to individual artisans \n&\n \nSelf\n-\nHelp Groups (SHGs) respectively are proposed to be provided. This will benefit about \n1300 artisans.  As part of the programme, Centre of Excellence (CoEs) with Indian Institute \nof Technology (IIT), Mumbai/Technology Centre of Ministry of MSME and Vis\nvesvaraya \nNational Institute of Technology (VNIT), Nagpur are also proposed to be set up, for machine \ndevelopment and development of various other products.\n \n \n(b)\n:\n \nOne Pilot Project each has been allocated for NER States like Tripura and Assam \n(with 8 Self \nHelp Groups), under this programme.\n \n \n(c)\n:\n \nNo Sir.  At present, no such proposal is under consideration of the Government.\n \n \n \n*****\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |504", "t": "Promotion of Local Production", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 504\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021\n \n \nPROMOTION OF LOCAL PRODUCTION\n \n \n†504.\n \n \nSHRI CHUNNI LAL SAHU: \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether any scheme is being run to promote local production;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nwhether the Government is considering to launch any scheme to provide self\n-\nemployment to\n \nthe migrant laborers who returned during the COVID\n-\n19 period; and\n \n(\nd\n)\n \nif so, the details thereof, State\n-\nwise? \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER  FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NITIN GADAKRI)\n \n \n(a) to (d):\n \nMinistry of \nMicro, Small and Medium Enterprises (\nMSME\n)\n \nthrough Khadi \nand Village Industries Commission (KVIC) and other Statutory Bodies implements \nschemes/programmes for the development of various village and traditional industries in \nrural as well as backward areas of the country. These industries besides e\nncouraging local \nproduction also generate sustainable employment opportunities for the rural and urban \nunemployed youth. Major Schemes being implemented by the Ministry in this regard are as \nunder:\n \n1\n \nPrime Minister’s Employment Generation Programme\n; PMEGP is a major \ncredit\n-\nlinked subsidy programme aimed at generating self\n-\nemployment opportunities \nthrough establishment of micro\n-\nenterprises in the non\n-\nfarm sector by helping \ntraditional artisans and rural/urban unemployed youth. Beneficiary can avail l\noan up \nto Rs. 25 lakh in the manufacturing sector and Rs. 10 lakh in the service sector with \nMargin Money (MM) subsidy up to 35%. \nSince inception of scheme and up to \n31.01.2021, a total of about 6.53 lakh micro enterprises have been assisted with an \nMM sub\nsidy of Rs. 15,308.23 crore generating employment for an estimated 53.8 \nlakh persons.\n \n \n \n2\n.\n \nScheme of Fund for Regeneration of Traditional Industries\n; SFURTI scheme \nsupports traditional artisans and organize them in clusters for sustainable \nemployment. Funds u\np to Rs.5 crore are provided for setting up Common Facility \nCenters, procuring new machineries, creating Raw Material Banks, marketing \nlinkages, re\n-\nskilling, exposure visits, etc. \n \n335 clusters have been approved till 31.01.2020 benefitting 1.98 lakh artis\nans with \nGoI assistance of  Rs. 785 cr. 82 of these clusters have become functional.\n \n \n\n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \n3. Gram\nud\nyog Vikas Yojana (GVY): The Scheme focuses on promotion of village \nindustries in (i) agro\n-\nbased and food processing (including Honey Mission), (ii) \nmine\nral based (including Kumbhar Sashaktikaran Programme), (iii) wellness and \ncosmetic, (iv) handmade paper, leather and plastics, (v) rural engineering and (vi) \nservices sector.  Under Honey Mission, 14380 beneficiaries have been assisted with \nbee\n-\nboxes with \nbee\n-\ncolonies, tool kits and training till 31.12.2020. Under Kumbhar \nSashaktikaran Programme, equipments like electric pottery wheels, pug mill, etc. \nhave been provided to 14515 potters till Nov.2020.\n \n \nIn addition, Ministry of MSME through \nKVIC has taken the following initiatives for \nproviding employment opportunities to people including migrant workers under \nAtamnirbhar Bharat Abhyian (ABA) in 116 districts of 6 States:\n \n\n \nUnder PMEGP, Margin Money amounting to Rs. 207.49 crore has been disbur\nsed \nfor setting up 5924 micro units.\n \n\n \nUnder beekeeping/Honey Mission, 335 beneficiaries have been trained and 3350 \nbee colonies and toolkits have been distributed.\n \n\n \nUnder Kumbhar Shashkatikaran Programme, 1000 potters have been trained and \nelectric pottery w\nheels have been distributed.\n \n \nState\n-\nwise list is enclosed at Annexure\n-\nI.\n \n \n*****\n \n \n\n \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \n \nAnnexure I referred to in reply to part (a) to (d) of the Lok Sabha \nUnstarred Question\n \nNo. \n504 for answer on 04.02.2021\n \n \nCurrent Status of Implementation of \nAtmanirbhar Bharat Abhiyan\n \nby KVIC in the 116 \nDistricts of the 6 States (State\n-\nwise Position)\n \nPMEGP\n:\n \nS\n. \nNo\n.\n \nState\n \nNo. of Project\n \nMM\n \n(In Lakh)\n \n1\n \nBIHAR\n \n779\n \n2735.67\n \n2\n \nJHARKHAND\n \n125\n \n322.73\n \n3\n \nMADHYA \nPRADESH\n \n1037\n \n3135.46\n \n4\n \nODISHA\n \n278\n \n902.36\n \n5\n \nRAJASTHAN\n \n1036\n \n3789.47\n \n6\n \nUTTAR PRADESH\n \n2669\n \n9863.49\n \n \nTotal\n \n5924\n \n20749.18\n \n \nBeekeeping/Honey Mission\n:\n \nS\n. \nNo\n.\n \nState\n \nNo. of Beneficiaries\n \n \nNo. of Bee Boxes with \nBee \n \nColonies\n \n1\n \nBIHAR\n \n150\n \n \n1500\n \n2\n \nUTTAR PRADESH\n \n185\n \n \n1850\n \n \nTotal\n \n335\n \n \n3350\n \n \nKumbhar Shashkatikaran \nProgramme\n:\n \nS\n. \nNo\n.\n \nState\n \nNo. of Beneficiaries\n \n \nNo. of Electric Pottery \nwheel distributed\n \n1\n \nBIHAR\n \n500\n \n \n500\n \n2\n \nUTTAR PRADESH\n \n500\n \n \n500\n \n \nTotal\n \n1000\n \n \n1000\n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |505", "t": "Repairing of Damaged Roads in Tamil Nadu", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 505 \nANSWERED ON 4TH FEBRUARY, 2021 \n  \nREPAIRING OF DAMAGED ROADS IN TAMIL NADU \n  \n505. SHRI MANICKAM TAGORE B.: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n  \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the National Highways Authority of India (NHAI) has \nidentified road sectors which have been damaged due to recent rain in \nTamil Nadu; and \n (b) if so, the details thereof; and \n (c) whether the Government has issued any directions to NHAI to get \nthese road sectors repaired especially in Virudhunagar District; and \n (d) if so, the details thereof along with financial allocation made for \nthis purpose? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n  (SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a) to (c)  National Highway stretches falling under the jurisdiction of \nNational Highways Authority of India (NHAI) in the State of Tamil Nadu \nincluding Virudhunagar District, which were damaged due to the \nrecent rains,  have been identified.  \nRepair/rectification work for a total of 19 National Highway \nstretches in the State of Tamil Nadu, which were damaged due to \nrecent rains, has been undertaken. Cost of repair for 8 stretches is \napproximately Rs 9.75 Crore. There is no cost implication for repair of \nrest of  the 11 stretches, as it is a part of scope of the \nContractors/Concessionaires. \n****** \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |506", "t": "Hydrogen Fuel Cell based Project", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.506 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \nHYDROGEN FUEL CELL BASED PROJECT \n \n†506.  SHRI MOHANBHAI KALYANJI KUNDARIYA: \nSHRI DIPSINH SHANKARSINH RATHOD: \nSHRI ANIL FIROJIYA: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether any plan has been chalked out by NTPC  to launch Hydrogen Fuel Cell \nbased Bus and car project in Madhya Pradesh similar to that of Leh and Delhi; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(d)  the name of districts of Madhya Pradesh where battery charging and swapping \nstations have become operational and where preparations are underway to start the \nsame? \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWE R, NEW & \nRENEWABLE ENERGY AND THE MINISTER OF STATE FOR SKILL DEVELOPMENT & \nENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n( SHRI  R.K. SINGH)  \n \n(a) :  No, Sir. \n(b) : Does not arise in view of (a) above. \n(c) : The Hydrogen Fuel cell technology is in nascent stage of adoption in the \ncountry with no commercial projects. The projects at Leh and Delhi have been taken \nup as pilot projects.  \n(d) : NTPC Ltd. has not installed any battery Charging/Swapping Stations in \nMadhya Pradesh. However, NTPC has installed 12 Standalone Public Charging \nStations (PCS) for Electric Vehicles in Bhopal District of Madhya Pradesh which are \noperational. Further, 63 PCS in Bhopal and 24 PCS in Jabalpur have been sanctioned \nfor installation under the FAME II Scheme by the Department of Heavy Industry.  \n \n************** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |507", "t": "Online Dashboard for PM SVANidhi", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 507 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nONLINE DASHBOARD FOR PM SVANIDHI \n \nNO. 507.  KUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE: \n   \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state:- \na)  whether the Government has launched online dashboar d of PM Street \nVendor’s Atmanirbhar Nidhi (PM SVANidhi) recently a nd if so, the details \nthereof; \nb)  the objectives and salient features of PM SVANidhi and the number of \nstreet vendors likely to be benefitted; \nc)  the number of applications received and sanctioned so far, State-wise; \nd)  whether Small Industries Development Bank of India (SIDBI) is the \nimplementing partner for the scheme and if so, the details thereof; and \ne)  whether the Government is in the process of prepari ng a plan to capture \nsocio economic profile of all PM SVANidhi beneficia ries and if so, the \ndetails thereof including the tenure of the scheme? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n    (SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n(a)  The dashboard of PM SVANidhi scheme launched on Aug ust 28, 2020 \nprovides a snapshot of various aspects of the Scheme.  \n \nThese include ranking of the Lending institutions, States and cities, \ncategorization of applications by gender and vendin g activity,number \nof applications received, sanctioned & disbursed, a mount disbursed, \ndigital payments undertaken by the beneficiaries, c ash back paid, \ninterest subsidy paid, Letter of Recommendation (Lo R) issued, \naverage number of days taken to sanction a loan, av erage age of the \napplicants, etc. \nContd…2/ \n\n-2- \n \n \n(b)  The Prime Minister Street Vendor’s AtmaNirbhar Nidh i Scheme (PM \nSVANidhi) aims at facilitating collateral free work ing capital loans \nupto ₹10,000 of 1 year tenure to about 50 lakh stre et vendors across \nthe country. It provides for incentives in the form  of interest subsidy @ \n7% per annum on regular repayment of loan and cash-back upto₹1,200 \nper annum on undertaking prescribed digital transactions.  \n \nIn order to cover the Street vendors left out of th e Survey or who \nare from rural / peri-urban areas but vend in the u rban areas, ‘Letter of \nRecommendation’ (LoR) has been introduced to make t hem eligible to \navail benefits of the Scheme. \n \nThe scheme provides for Socio-economic profiling of  \nbeneficiaries and their families to determine their eligibility for various \nCentral government welfare schemes and link them to these schemes. \nIt also aims to facilitate beneficiary Street Food Vendors to e-\ncommerce platforms to serve customers in the post COVID times.  \n \n(c)  A statement giving State / Union Territory wise det ails of the loan \napplications received and sanctionedis at Annexure-I. \n \n(d)  Yes, Sir. A Memorandum of Understanding was entered  into with \nSmall Industries Development Bank of India (SIDBI) on June 19, 2020 \n \n(e)  Ministry has launched the program of socio-economic  profiling of PM \nSVANidhi beneficiaries and their families in 125 ci ties, selected for \nsaturation in the first phase, on January 4, 2021. The program aims to \nassess the potential eligibility for various Centra l welfare schemes \nand to further facilitate the access to these schemes.  \n \n \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 507TO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 4, 2021. \n \nSTATE / UT-WISE DETAILS OF APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AND SANCTIONED \nUNDER PM SVANidhi SCHEME  \n(AS ON 31-01-2021) \n \nS.No  State Total \nApplications \nReceived \nTotal \nApplications \nSanctioned \n1 Andaman and Nicobar Islands 419  291  \n2 Andhra Pradesh 2,04,407  1,16,658  \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 2311  711  \n4 Assam 28,180  9,831  \n5 Bihar 76,684  22,358  \n6 Chandigarh 2,339  1,444  \n7 Chhattisgarh 70,106  33,141  \n8 D&NH and DD 2,072  1,191  \n9 Delhi 49,435  26,739  \n10 Goa 1,201  857  \n11 Gujarat 1,98,247  1,24,816  \n12 Haryana 34,973  16,489  \n13 Himachal Pradesh 3,169  1,901  \n14 Jammu and Kashmir 10,819  8,137  \n15 Jharkhand 31,621  16,076  \n16 Karnataka 2,01,420  97,077  \n17 Kerala 10,725  7,348  \n18 Ladakh 256  208  \n19 Madhya Pradesh 3,91,955  2,73,003  \n20 Maharashtra 3,57,737  1,52,825  \n21 Manipur 11,917  5,176  \n22 Meghalaya 104  23  \n23 Mizoram 585  394  \n24 Nagaland 1,745  814  \n25 Odisha 49,438  24,102  \n26 Puducherry 1,655  1,194  \n27 Punjab 63,649  14,361  \n28 Rajasthan 1,17,187  44,073  \n\n29 Tamil Nadu 2,27,982  62,151  \n30 Telangana 4,42,366  3,33,268  \n31 Tripura 3,359  2,159  \n32 Uttar Pradesh 7,51,808  4,29,945  \n33 Uttarakhand 12,962  8,399  \n34 West Bengal 6,545  1,710  \nGrand Total 33,69,378  18,38,870  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |508", "t": "Drinking Water Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 508 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021  \n  \nDrinking Water Projects \n508. SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Government has proposed to launch pilot projects for clean drinking water \nfor villages, under Swajal project;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c)  whether the drinking water project involves lo cal bodies for civil work and \nmaintenance; and  \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof along with the project launching States including Karnataka? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA) \n(a) &(b)  Government of India, in partnership with States, is implementing Jal Jeevan \nMission (JJM)-Har Ghar Jal to enable every rural ho usehold in the country to have assured \npotable water through Functional Household Tap Conn ection (FHTC) by 2024, with an \nestimated outlay of Rs. 3.60 lakh Crore. Further, S wajal, that was part of erstwhile NRDWP \nand implemented in aspirational districts, has been subsumed under JJM. \n(c) & (d)  In consonance with 73rd Amendment of Con stitution of India and to instil the sense \nof ownership among villagers, village level plannin g and community participation in all \ndecisions pertaining to water supply systems have b een envisaged under JJM. Further, Gram \nPanchayat or its sub-committee/ user group i.e. Vil lage Water & Sanitation Committee \n(VWSC) or Pani Samiti having at least 50% women mem bers and suitable representation to \nmarginalized sections of society, has been empowere d to plan, implement, manage, operate \nand maintain in-village water supply system. \nState/ UT-wise details of progress made under JJM, including that of Karnataka, is in public \ndomain and available on the JJM dashboard @ \nhttps://ejalshakti.gov.in/jjmreport/JJMIndia.aspx . \n**** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |509", "t": "NRDWP", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 509 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \nNRDWP \n \n†509. SHRI MAHABALI SINGH: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the National Rural Drinking Water Programme  (NRDWP) is meeting its desired \nobjectives in Bihar; \n(b) if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(c) the efforts made by the Union Government to provide drinking water in all the rural areas \nof Bihar by the year 2024? \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA) \n \n(a) to (c) Since August, 2019 to enable every rural  household in the country, including those \nin the State of Bihar, to have potable water supply  through Functional Household Tap \nConnection (FHTC) by 2024, Government of India is implementing Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) \n– Har Ghar Jal in partnership with States after sub suming National Rural Drinking Water \nProgramme (NRDWP). \nUnder JJM, in 2019-20, out of Central allocation of  \u0001  787.31 Crore, the State had drawn         \n\u0001  417.35 Crore. In 2020-21, out of \u0001  1,839.16 Crore allocated to the State, the State has been \nable to draw \u0001  350.42 Crore, so far. \nAs reported by the State in JJM – Integrated Manage ment Information System (IMIS), as on \n01.02.2021, out of 1.97 Crore rural households in t he State, 1.25 Crore (63.09%) rural \nhouseholds have tap water connection. \n*** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |51", "t": "Special Schemes during Corona Pandemic", "head": "������������������� �\n��������������������������������������� �\n��\n��������� �\n�������������������� �� �\n������������ � �� ��������������� �\n�\n���������������� ���� �� �\n�\n��� ��������������������� ��\n����������\n������������������������������������������������ �\n�����������������������������������������������������\n�\n�������������������� �\n��\n��� �������� ��������� �������� � ���� �� ��� ��� ���� ����� ��������� ��� ������\n�������������������������������������������������������� ��\n�\n��� ���������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������� �����������������������������������������������������������\n����������� ��\n�\n��� ������������������������������������������������������������������� ��\n�\n��� ��������������������������������������������������������� ������������������\n����� ��������� ������� ��� ����� �������� ��� ����� �������� ���� ��� ������\n�������������������������� ����� �\n�\n��� ����������������������������������� ���\n�\n�\n������ �\n�\n������������������������������������������� �\n�\n��������������������������� �\n�\n��\n�\n����������������������� ����������������������������������� �\n�\n�\n�"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |510", "t": "Measures to Reduce Road Accidents", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 510  \nANSWERED ON 4TH FEBRUARY, 2021 \n \nMEASURES TO REDUCE ROAD ACCIDENTS \n \n510. SHRI S. MUNISWAMY:  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a)  the measures taken by the Government to reduced road accidents \nin the country;  \n(b)  whether the Government has any plan to ensure better \nconnectivity and reduce fatal accidents in Mulbagal -Hoskotte-\nBanglore stretch of N H - 75 in Kolar district of Karnataka;  \n(c)  if so, the details thereof;  \n(d)  whether the Government has received any proposal in this regard; \nand  \n(e)  if so, the actions taken by the Government thereon? \n \n \n \n\nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a)   The Ministry of Road Transport &  Highways has notified “Member \nof Parliaments’ Road Safety Committee” in each district of the country to \npromote awareness amongst road users under the chairmanship of \nHon’ble Member of Parliament (Lok Sabha) from the district.  \n \nThe Ministry has formulated a multi-pronged strategy to address the \nissue of road safety based on Education, Engineering (both of roads and \nvehicles), Enforcement and Emergency Care. Accordingly, various \ninitiatives have been taken by the Ministry as detailed below:- \n \n(i) Education:   \n The Ministry implements a scheme for undertaking publicity \nmeasures and awareness campaigns on road safety to create \nawareness among road users through the electronic media, print \nmedia, NGOs etc.  \n Observance of National Road Safety Week every year for \nspreading awareness and strengthening road safety. \n A Certification Course for Road Safety Auditors has been \ncommenced in Indian Academy of Highway Engineers (IAHE). \n \n(ii) Engineering (both of Roads and vehicles) \n Road engineering \n High priority to identification and rectification of black spots \n(accident prone spots) on national highways. \n\n Road safety has been made an integral part of road design at \nplanning stage. \n The threshold for four laning of National Highways have been \nreduced from 15,000 Passenger Car Units (PCUs) to 10,000 \nPCUs. \n Ministry has delegated powers to Regional Officers of MORTH \nfor technical approval to the detailed estimates for \nrectification of identified Road Accident black spots. \n Guidelines for pedestrian facilities on National Highways for \npersons with disabilities have also been issued to all States \n/ UTs. \n \n Vehicle engineering: \n Safety standards for automobiles have been improved.  \n Ministry has notified fitment of Speed Limiting devices on all \ntransport vehicles  \n Scheme for setting up one model Inspection & Certification \nCentre in each State/UT with Central assistance for testing \nthe fitness of vehicles through an automated system \n \n(iii) Enforcement  \n The recently passed motor vehicles (Amendment) Act, 2019 \nprovides for strict enforcement through use of technology and \nfurther provides for strict penalties for ensuring strict \ncompliance and enhancing deterrence for violation of traffic \nrules.  \n Issue of Guidelines for protection of Good Samaritans and draft \nrules as per MV(amendment) Act, 2019 has been published. \n \n(iv) Emergency care:  \n\n The Motor Vehicles (Amendment) Act, 2019 provides for a \nscheme for the cashless treatment of victims of the accident \nduring the golden hour. \n The National Highways Authority of India has also  deployed \n550 ambulances at toll  plazas on the completed corridor of \nNational Highways.   \n Out of 550 ambulances, 171 ambulances have been upgraded \nto Basic Life Support as per AIS-125. \n \n(b) to (e) In the stretch of Bangalore -Hoskote-Mulbagal, the following \nmeasures were taken up to reduce fatal accidents and ensure better \nconnectivity; \n \ni)     For safe pedestrian crossing of Highway,  8 Nos. of FOB’s \nwere constructed at various locations of the project highway \nto ensure safety of road users. \nii)     Under Road Safety to users, Service Road for an amount of \nRs.5.14 Cr was constructed at Kondrajanalli (from \nKm.31+030 to 31+150) and at DC office, Kolar (from \nKm.20+800 to 22+100). \niii)    Additional Street lighting has been provided at 4 locations in \nthe project highway under road safety.  \n \n     ***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |511", "t": "Drainage System along National Highways", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 511 \nANSWERED ON 04TH FEBRUARY, 2021 \n  \nDRAINAGE SYSTEM ALONG NATIONAL HIGHWAYS \n  \n511. SHRI SHRINIWAS PATIL: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether some portion of the planned estimate for roads upgraded \nfrom State Highways and reassigned to National Highways Authority of \nIndia (NHAI) is earmarked for ensuring proper drainage of water to \navoid water logging in the fields adjoining the newly widened \nconstructed roads to avoid damage to the crops in the adjoining \nagricultural lands; \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the percentage of the planned \nestimate allocated for the purpose; \n(c) whether the affected farmers are eligible for any compensation \nfrom NHAI for resultant crop damage due to blockage of the pre -\nexisting natural drainage or inadequate drainage provided by NHAI; \nand \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) Adequate provisions are made in the estimates being prepared for \nupgradation of roads to take care of drainage aspects  under the scope \nof relevant items and made part of scope of the contracts for  all newly \nconstructed/widened roads taken -up by NHAI duly following Indian \n\nRoad Congress (IRC) Standards & Guidelines  and specifications of \nMinistry of Road Transport and Highways. \n  \n(b) Construction of roadside lined/unlined drains, Cross Drainage \nstructures viz., Culverts, Minor & Major bridges across natural \nstreams/drainage etc., are carried out as per scope of contract by the \ncontractors/concessionaires as per standard specifications and IRC \ncodes. No fixed percentage of planned estimate can be explicitly \nallocated for this purpose as it varies from project to project \ndepending on the site requirements. \n  \n(c) & (d) Does not arise. \n  \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |512", "t": "Traditional sports in India", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 512 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \nTraditional sports in India \n \n512. SHRIMATI PRENEET KAUR: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes any policy/plan to develop and \npromote the culture of sports in India, especially in rural areas and if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n \n(b) the steps taken/being taken by the Government  to revive traditional \nsports in India; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes any policy/plan to hire more Indian \ncoaches; and \n \n(d) if so, the manner in which the Government proposes to do so; \n \n(e) whether the Government proposes any incentive  system/mechanism for \nthe Indian coaches and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(f) the steps taken/being taken by the Government  to facilitate different \nsports players forced to train indoors due to the COVID-19 pandemic; and \n \n(g) the measures taken/being taken by the  Government to boost and revive \nthe sports industry in light of the ongoing COVID-19 pandemic? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS  \n{ SHRI KIREN RIJIJU } \n \n(a) The Ministry of Youth Affairs & Sports is already running schemes for \npromotion of sports culture as well as attaining excellence in sports. These \nschemes include:- \n \n\n(i) Khelo India Scheme (ii) Assistance to National Sports Federations; \n(iii)Special Awards to Winners in International sports events and their \nCoaches; (iv) National Sports Awa rds, Pension to Meritorious Sports \nPersons; (v) National Sports Development Fund; and (vi) Sports Training \nCentres run through Sports Authority of India. The Ministry is also promoting \nrural and indigenous/tribal games under the ‘Khelo India Scheme’ and \nthrough the ‘Ek Bharat Shreshtha Bharat’  (EBSB) programme. Most athletes \nbenefitting under these schemes come from rural areas. \n \n(b) For traditional sports, t he Department of Sports conducted 36 EBSB \nChampionship events at 19 venues across the country throug h the National \nSports Federations  in 2019 -20. Approximately , 2250 sportsperson s \nparticipated in these events. \n \nDue to Covid -19 pandemic the Department of Sports used the Digital \nMedium to spread the awareness of Indigenous Games of various \nStates/UTs. The Department made documentaries of 19 indigenous games, \nnamely, Gatka, Roll Ball, Tug of War, Kalaripayattu, Thang -Ta, Kho -Kho, \nMallakhamb, Shooting Ball, Sqay, Kabaddi, Gilli Danda (Gujarat), Sikkim \nArchery, Dhoop Khel & Cowrie Khel (Assam), Paika Akhada & \nChhau(Odisha) and Akhada Kushti (Uttar Pradesh), Hekko (Nagaland), \nMizoram Games, Sila mbam (Puduc herry) Lagori and Langadi (Goa), and \nuploaded these documentaries on the Digital Repository of the Ek Bharat \nShreshtha Bharat website , Facebook page of the Fit  India Movement, \nYoutube channel of Fit India Movement and Youtube channel of MyGov \nIndia. \n \n(c) and (d) Yes, Sir. Following actions have been taken in this regard:- \n \n i. Notification for appointment for Olympians/Para -Olympians as \nCoach in Sports Authority of India has already been published. 23 vacancies \nof Asstt. Coach and 04 vacancies of Coach will be filled from \nOlympians/Para-Olympians. \n \n ii.    Recruitment Rules (RRs) of Coaches in SAI have been amended \nto appoint coaches on Contract/Deputation in SAI.   \n \n iii.    In order to fill the vacancy especially at the higher level, it is \ndecided to engage outstanding sportsperson/coaches working in other \norganization on deputation. \n \n Further, more and more Indian Coaches are engaged in training of \nathletes in National Coaching Camps, Khelo India Academies and National \n\nCentre of Excellence (NCoEs). The coaches so engaged also includes Ex \nInternational players. They are appointed by a duly constituted Committee. \n \n(e) Yes, Sir. The Governing Body of S ports Authority of India  has approved \nOut of Turn Promotions to sportspersons/ coaches for excellence in Sports \nevent at par with DoPT guidelines. \n \n(f) The steps taken/being taken by the Government  to facilitate different \nsports players forced to train indoors  due to the COVID-19 pandemic are at \nAnnexure. \n \n(g) The Ministry has organised discussions with representatives of the \nsports industry, including CII, ASSOCHAM, FICCI and CSRI regarding the key \nchallenges faced by them and  to boost and revive the sports industry in  \nlight of the ongoing COVID-19 pandemic. \n \n\nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( f) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 512 TO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \nREGARDING “ TRADITIONAL SPORTS IN INDIA ” ASKED BY SHRIMATI \nPRENEET KAUR, HON’BLE MEMBER OF LOK SABHA \n \nIn view of the COVID -19 pandemic, inter -alia, the following measures were \ntaken to ensure a smooth training for the sportspersons including those we \nwho have qualified for upcoming 2021 Olympic Games to be held in Tokyo \nJapan so that their performan"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |514", "t": "Stranded Vessels", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 514\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 4TH FEBRUARY, 202,1\nSTRANDED VESSELS\n514. SHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU:\nWill the Minister of PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS be pleased to state:\nqilfr , +d qfud-afr' 3ik E-d-flFt *i*\n(a) whether it is a fact that two lndian vessels were stranded for months off the coast of China as\nthey were caught in a trade dispute between China and Australia;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether it is a fact that an agreement has been reached to bring these stranded lndian\nseafarers home from China;\n(d) if so, the details thereof; and\n(e) if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\n(SHRI MANSUKH MANDAVIYA)\n(a) & (b) No Sir. As per the available information only one lndian vessel M.V Jag Anand (lMO\nNo.9463308) owned by M/s Great Eastern Shipping Company had arrived at the Port of Jingtang,\nNorth China on 13 June, 2O2O lor discharging of Coal which she had loaded in Australia. The\nvessel had 23 lndian Crew on board. The vessel was stranded on account of difficulties faced by\nthem regarding Crew Change at Chinese Ports as the Chinese Port Authorities have banned the\nentries of Foreigners w.e.f. 28 March,2020 and suspended all visa and residence permits with a\nview to control the spread of Covid-19. The vessel was waiting at the port of Jintang, China till\nfirst week ol January 2021 .\nlnforlnation was also received for one foreign flag vessel M.V. Anastasia (1MONo.962590)\nwith 16 lndian seafarers onboard stranded at Caofeidian Anchorage, China since 3rd August,\n2020.\nP.T.O.\n\n(c) to (e) No Sir. No agreement has been reached to bring these stranded lndian seafarers.\nHowever, on receipt of the information, the Directorate General of Shipping has taken up the\nmatter with the following authorities seeking assistance to facilitate the crew change of seafarers\n& repatriation of the crew:\ni.the Ministry of External Affairs, South West Division, New Delhi;\nii.the Embassy of lndia, Beijing, China;\niii.tthe Maritime Safety Administration of People Republic of China; and\niv.the Secretary General, lnternational Maritime Organization (lMO), London.\nLater on the lndian flag vessel, M. V. Jag Anand was allowed to sail to Japan for crew\nchange and all disembarked crew from the vessel on '18-19 January, 2021 al Chiba, Japan have\nsince been repatriated to lndia on 20 January, 2021 .fhc vessel M.V. Jag Anand then sailed ottt\nof Chiba, Japan on 21 January, 2021 for China.\nWith regard to the vessel, M V Anastasia, the matter has been taken up with the Chinese\nauthorities by the Ministry of External Affairs, Govt. of lndia/lndian Embassy at China for an early\nresolution. The foreign office of Hebei Province, China has informed that MV ANASTASIA can\nleave anchorage in order to effect the crew change, which is likely to take place from Tianjin Port.\nThe authorities in Tianjin port are examining the matter of facilitating the crew change."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |515", "t": "Loans under PM SVANidhi Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 515 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nLOANS UNDER PM SVANIDHI SCHEME \n \nNO. 515  SHRI DUSHYANT SINGH: \n   \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state:- \na)  the details of the amount of loan and the banks fro m which the loan amount have been \ndisbursed under the PM Street Vendor’s AtmaNirbhar Nidhi, State-wise; \nb)  the details of the number of loan applications rece ived and the loans sanctioned and \ndisbursed under the scheme, State-wise; \nc)  whether there is any time limit within which Urban Local Bodies have to conduct local \nenquiry and ascertain genuineness of the claim of a n eligible beneficiary not having other \nprescribed documents under the guidelines; \nd)  if so, the details thereof if not, the reasons therefor; \ne)  the number of days that was taken for persons who h ave received to finish paperwork \nunder the scheme in order get the loan within 30 days; and \nf)  the details of town vending committees present in each State? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n    (SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n(a)&(b): A statement indicatingState / Union Territ ory wise details of the loan \napplications received, sanctioned, disbursed and th e amount sanctioned and \ndisbursed under Prime Minister Street Vendor’s Amta Nirbhar Nidhi Scheme (PM \nSVANidhi), as on 31 \nst  January, 2021 is at Annexure-I. \n \nThe details of Lending Institution wise amount disbursed is at Annexure-II.  \n (c)&(d):     As per the Scheme, the vendors left o ut of the identification survey conducted \nby Urban Local Body (ULB), may apply through the PM  SVANidhi Portal for \nLetter of Recommendation (LoR) and then avail the b enefit of loan under the \nScheme.ULB shall complete the verification and issu e LoR within 15 days of \nsubmission of application. \n(e): There is no time limit prescribed for sanction  of loan under PM SVANidhi \nScheme. As on 31.01.2021, the average number of day s to sanction and \ndisbursement are 27 days and 41 days respectively. \n(f): As reported by the States/UTs, presently, 3,72 4 Town Vending Committees \n(TVCs) have been formed. The State / Union Territor y wise details are at \nAnnexure-III. \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE -I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 515 TO \nBE ANSWERED ON 4 FEBRUARY, 2021. \n \nSTATE / UT-WISE DETAILS OF APPLICATIONS RECEIVED, LOAN APPLICATIONS \nSANCTIONED, LOAN APPLICATIONS DISBURSED, AMOUNT SANCTIONED AND AMOUNT \nDISBURSED UNDER PM SVA NIDHI  \n(AS ON 31-01-2021) \n \nS.No  State  Total \nReceived \nApplication \nTotal \nSanctioned \nApplication \nTotal \nDisbursed \nApplication \nDisbursed  \namount \n  (₹in Crore) \n1 Andaman  and Nicobar \nIslands \n419  291  240  0.240  \n2 Andhra Pradesh  2,04,407  1,16,658  96,482  95.793  \n3 Arunachal Pradesh  2311  711  608  0.607  \n4 Assam  28,180  9,831  6,301  6.282  \n5 Bihar  76,684  22,358  10,780  10.563  \n6 Chandigarh  2,339  1,444  1,196  1.187  \n7 Chhattisgarh  70,106  33,141  27,526  27.239  \n8 D&NH and  DD  2,072  1,191  639  0.635  \n9 Delhi  49,435  26,739  19,167  18.965  \n10  Goa  1,201  857  677  0.670  \n11  Gujarat  1,98,247  1,24,816  80,041  79.306  \n12  Haryana  34,973  16,489  11,451  11.323  \n13  Himachal Pradesh  3,169  1,901  1,349  1.345  \n14  Jammu and Kashmir  10,819  8,137  6,177  6.161  \n15  Jharkhand  31,621  16,076  13,366  13.269  \n16  Karnataka  2,01,420  97,077  58,950  58.729  \n17  Kerala  10,725  7,348  6,289  6.231  \n18  Ladakh  256  208  186  0.186  \n19  Madhya Pradesh  3,91,955  2,7