 \nNo. of \nParticipants \nNo. of Prog. \norganized \nNo. of \nParticipants \nNo. of Prog. \norganized \nNo. of \nParticipants \nNo. of Prog. \norganized \nNo. of \nParticipants \n1. Youth Club Development \nConvention at Block Level \n04 40 05 50 09 90 09 90 07 \n \n470 \n2. Training on Youth \nLeadership & Community \nDevelopment \n04 174 05 204 01 41 01 45 02 \n \n80 \n3. Promotion of Sports           \na Sports Material to Youth \nClubs \n60 - 50 - - - 45 - 220 - \n \nb Block level Sports Meets 03 954 04 877 09 1922 09 1744 09 1273 \nc District Level Sports \nMeets \n01 36 01 72 01 97 01 114 01 135 \n4. Education in Basic \nVocations \n12 300 10 258 09 258 09 228 05 150 \n5. District Level Promotion of  \nArt & Culture \n01 83 01 77 01 91 01 190 01 255 \n6. Observance of Days of  \nNational importance and \nNational Youth Day & \nWeek "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |532", "t": "Training and high performance centres", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 532 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04/02/2021 \n \nTraining and high performance centres \n \n†532. SHRI SANJAY BHATIA: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to set up training and high \nperformance Centres of international / Standard to promote Boxing in \nBhiwani and Kaithal, Hockey in Shahabad, Volleyball in Kurukshetra and \nWrestling in Sonipat areas with a view of outstanding performance of \nathletes particularly of Haryana in specific Sports disciplines to make \nIndia a power in sports;  \n(b) if so, whether the Government also proposes to set up any high level \ntraining centres/sports academies in Panipat line to promote more other \nlocal talents like athlete Neeraj Chopra and Gagan Narang of Panipat \ndistrict;  \n(c) if so, whether the Government proposes to set up any National Sports \nUniversity in Haryana in view of the achievement made by sportspersons \nof Haryana State during the last so many decades; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) & (b)  Sports is a State Subject and the primary responsibility of \ndevelopment of sports, including setting up training a nd high \nperformance Centres of International Standard in the country to promote \nspecific sports disciplines, like boxing, rests with the State / Union \nTerritory (UT) Governments and the  National Sports Federations (NSFs) , \nwhich are responsible for governance of individual sports disciplines. \nThe Central Government, through the sports development schemes \nimplemented by it and organizations functioning under its aegis, \n\ncomplements and supplements the efforts of the State Governments and \nNSFs in this regard. \nSports Authority of India (SAI), an autonomous body under the aegis of \nthe Ministry of Youth Affairs & Sports is implementing the following \nSports Promotional Schemes across the country to identify talented \nsportspersons in the age group of 8 -25 years and nurture them to excel \nat National and International competitions:- \n National Centres of Excellence (NCOE) \n SAI Training Centre (STC) \n Extension Centre of STC \n National Sports Talent Contest (NSTC) \nThe above schemes are implemented to promote various sports \nincluding Boxing, Hockey, Volleyball & Wrestling in various SAI Centres \nincluding in the State of Haryana. Details of the above schemes \noperational in Haryana are given in Annexure I. \nIn ad dition, under the Khelo India – National Programme for \nDevelopment of Sports , various initiatives have been undertaken to \nsetup training facilities across India including the State of Haryana. \nAcademies have been accredited for support under the vertical o f \nSupport for National/Regional/State Academies in Haryana.  List of such \nAcademies in the State of Haryana is given at Annexure-II. \n \nApart from this , the following Khelo India Centres (KICs) are \noperational in the State of Haryana: \ni. Kharkhoda for Boxing, Judo, Kabaddi, Wrestling & Wushu. \nii. MorMajra, Karnal for Kabaddi exclusively for girls. \niii. Kurukshetra University, Kurukshetra for Hockey exclusively for \ngirls  \niv. Convent Gurukul, Kurukshetra for Hockey only. \nv. Gurunanak Khalsa College, Kurukshetra for Weightlifting only. \n(c) & (d) At present, there is no proposal in the Ministry to set up any \nNational Sports University in Haryana. \n \n \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure – I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED Q NO. 532 FOR 04/2/2021 REGARDING TRAINING AND HIGH \nPERFORMANCE CENTRES, ASKED BY SHRI SANJAY BHATIA , HON’BLE \nMP. \n \nDetails of the SAI Sports Promotional Schemes operational in Haryana \n \ni)  National Centres of Excellence (NCOE) \n Sonepat for Archery, Athletics, Kabaddi, Hockey & Wrestling. \n Rohtak exclusively for Boxing  \nii) SAI Training Centres (STC) \n Kurukshetra for Athletics, Cycling, Hockey & Volleyball  \n Bhiwani for Athletics, Boxing & Wrestling  \n Hissar for Athletics, Boxing, Hockey & Wrestling  \niii) National Sports Talent Contest (NSTC) \nAdopted Akharas exclusively for Wrestling: \na) Lala Diwan Chand, Jhajjar \nb) Ch. Bharat Singh Memorial Sports School, Nidani \nc) Capt. Ram Karan, Rohtak \nd) Saheed Bhagat Singh, Hissar \ne) Vikas Pehlwan, Rohtak \n \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure - II \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED Q NO. 532 FOR 04/2/2021 REGARDING TRAINING AND HIGH \nPERFORMANCE CENTRES, ASKED BY SHRI SANJAY BHATIA, HON’BLE \nMP. \n \nList of Academies accredited under the Khelo India – National \nProgramme for Development of Sports \n \nS. \nNo. \nState/ \nUT \nSAI/ \nNON \nSAI \nAccredited \nAcademies \nBoys\n/ \nGirls \nAcco\nmmod\nation \nSports \nDiscipline \nFOP \n1 Haryana NON SAI Motilal Nehru \nSchool of Sports, \nHaryana \nB&G R Basketball Yes \n2 Haryana NON SAI Saheed Bhagat \nSingh Internat"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |533", "t": "Restructuring of Sick MSMEs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 533\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON\n \n04.02.2021\n \n \nRESTRUCTURING OF SICK MSMEs\n \n \n533\n.\n                \nSHRI B.Y.RAGHAVENDRA:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL &\n \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nthe total number of sick Micro, Small and Medium  Enterprises (MSMEs)  in the \nCountry during the last three years;\n \n(b)\n \nwhether the Reserve Bank of India has appointed a special committee for \nrestructuring the b\nank loan of sick MSMEs and if so, the details thereof;\n \n(c)\n \nthe salient features of restructuring the bank loan of sick MSMEs; and\n \n(d)\n \nthe number of small scale industries restructured during the last three years, \nState/year and industry\n-\nwise;\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NITIN GADKARI)\n \n \n(a) to (\nd\n)\n:\n \nThe Reserve Bank of India has issued guidelines on ‘Framework for Revival \nand Rehabilitation of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises’ to Scheduled Commercial \nBanks (excluding \nRegional Rural Banks) in March 2016. With the issuance of these \nGuidelines, the concept of sickness no longer exists.  Under this Framework, banks have \nbeen advised to identify incipient stress in the MSME accounts and refer it to the \nCommittees formed und\ner the framework for Corrective Action Plan for rectification, \nrestructuring and recovery.  The latest available data furnished by the Reserve Bank of \nIndia, regarding revival and rehabilitation of MSMEs for the half year ended March, 2017 \nto half year end\ned March, 2020 is given in Annexure I. \n \n \nMoreover, with a view to facilitate meaningful restructuring of stressed MSME \naccounts, RBI has permitted a one\n-\ntime restructuring of loans to MSMEs subject to certain \nconditions. The restructuring of the MSME borro\nwer account under the revised guidelines \nis to be implemented by March 31, 2021 and such borrower accounts which may have \nslipped into NPA category between March 2, 2020 and date of implementation may be \nupgraded as ‘standard asset’, as on the date of impl\nementation of the restructuring plan. \n \n \n*****\n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure I \n \n \n \n \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \nreferred to in reply to part (a) \nto\n \n(\nd\n) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 533 \nfor answer on 04.02.2021.\n \n \n      \nData on Framework for Revival and Rehabilitation of MSME\n \n(Accounts \nin Actual)\n \nSI. \nNo.\n \nFor the half year\n \nAccounts \nreferred to \nthe \nCommittee \nduring the \nhalf year \n(1)\n \nAccounts \nresolved by \nthe \nCommittee \nduring the \nhalf year (2)\n \nCorrective Action Plan by the \nCommittee (out of 2)\n \nRectification\n \nRestructuring\n \nRecovery\n \n1\n \nOctober 2016\n-\nMarch 2017\n \n100803\n \n137282\n \n80905\n \n2197\n \n54180\n \n2\n \nApril 2017\n-\nSeptember 2017\n \n87062\n \n95107\n \n58512\n \n207\n \n36388\n \n3\n \nOctober 2017\n-\nMarch 2018\n \n130208\n \n130473\n \n81492\n \n1024\n \n47957\n \n4\n \nApril 2018\n-\nSeptember 2018\n \n150165\n \n123227\n \n76172\n \n201\n \n46854\n \n5\n \nOctober 2018 to \nMarch \n2019\n \n142275\n \n146519\n \n78737\n \n15425\n \n52357\n \n6\n \nApril 2019\n-\nSeptember 2019\n \n172949\n \n150613\n \n71203\n \n14240\n \n65170\n \n7\n \nOctober 2019\n-\nMarch 2020\n \n339728\n \n324621\n \n71938\n \n148369\n \n104314\n \nSource: RBI\n \n*Number of cases resolved by the committee is more than the cases referred due to \nsome pending \ncases with the committee at the beginning of the half year which have been resolved during the \nperiod.\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |534", "t": "Illegal Encroachment of Port Trust Land", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.534\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 4TH FEBRUARY,2021\nILLEGAL ENCROACHMENT OF PORT TRUST LAND\n534. SHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS:\nWill the Minister of PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS be pleased to state:\nqilm,*a qfa{ad-3i1T df,fll?t a*\n(a) whether the coastal operational areas of Mumbai poft trust and the M.V.P.T. roads\nhave been encroached illegally; and\n(b) if so, the initiatives being taken by the Government to make the land free of illegal\nencroachment?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\n(SHRI MANSUKH MANDAVIYA )\n(a) Some portions/patches of roads in Mumbai Port Trust (MbPT) estate have been\nencroached and few hutments/ encroachments are existing since decades.\n(b) MbPT undertakes periodical programmes for removal of such encroachments. As a\nresult of these initiatives, approxlmately 1,09,874 sq mtrs of MbPT land area have been\nmade encroachment free.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |535", "t": "PMAY-U", "head": " \n \nO.I.H. \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA  \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 535 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nPMAY-U \n \nNO. 535 SHRIMATI JASKAUR MEENA:  \n  SHRI NIHAL CHAND: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)  the salient features of Pradhan Mantri Awas Yo jana - Urban (PMAY-U);  \n(b)  the details of funds approved, allocated and u tilised in Rajasthan during the \nlast three years under this scheme;  \n(c)  the number of districts added so far under thi s scheme and the details \nthereof;  \n(d)  the details of targets fixed and progress made  thereon; \n(e)  whether people belonging to Scheduled Tribes h ave been added under the \nsaid scheme;  \n(f)  if so, the details thereof including the facil ities being provided to them \nunder the said scheme; and \n(g)  if not, the time by which these people are lik ely to be included under the \nsaid scheme and the details thereof; and  \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n (a): Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs (MoHUA)  has been implementing \nPradhan Mantri Awas Yojana – Urban (PMAY-U) since 2 5.06.2015 for providing \nassistance to States/ Union Territories (UTs) for a ddressing the housing \nrequirement of the people belonging to Economically  Weaker Section (EWS), Low \nIncome Group (LIG) and Middle Income Group (MIG) ca tegories in urban areas \nthrough following four verticals:  \n \n \nContd...2/ \n \n \n\n-2- \nS. \nNo. \nVertical Carpet Area of the house Central Assistanc e \nper house \n1. In-situ Slum \nRedevelopment \n(ISSR) \nUpto 30 square meter (sqm) Rs. 1.00 lakh \n2. Credit Linked \nSubsidy Scheme \n(CLSS) \nUpto 30 sqm for EWS (annual \nhousehold income up to \n₹3,00,000), 60 sqm for LIG \n(annual household income \nfrom ₹3,00,001 to ₹6,00,000), \n160 sqm for MIG-I(annual \nhousehold income from \n₹6,00,001 to ₹12,00,000),   \nand 200 sqm for MIG-II \n(annual household income \nfrom ₹12,00,001 to \n₹18,00,000). \nInterest subsidy of \n6.5 %, 4% and 3% on \nloan amounts upto \nRs. 6 lakh, Rs. 9 lakh \nand Rs. 12 lakh for \nbeneficiaries \nbelonging to EWS/LIG, \nMIG-I and MIG-II \ncategories \nrespectively. \n3. Affordable \nHousing in \nPartnership (AHP) \nUpto 30 sqm Rs. 1.50 lakh \n4. Beneficiary-led \nindividual house \nconstruction/ \nenhancement \n(BLC) \nUpto 30 sqm Rs. 1.50 lakh \n \nThe scheme covers the entire urban area consisting of Statutory Towns, Notified \nPlanning Areas, Development Authorities, Special Ar ea Development Authorities, \nIndustrial Development Authorities or any such auth ority under State legislation \nwhich is entrusted with the functions of urban planning & regulations.  \nAll houses constructed under PMAY-U have basic amen ities like water supply, \nelectricity, kitchen and toilet. The Mission promot es women empowerment by \nproviding the ownership of houses in name of female  member or in joint name. \nPreference is also given to differently abled perso ns, senior citizens, Scheduled \nCastes (SCs), Scheduled Tribes (STs), Other Backwar d Classes (OBCs), Minority, \nsingle women, transgender and other weaker & vulner able sections of the \nsociety. \n(b) to (d):Based on the project proposals received so far from the State \nGovernment of Rajasthan under the Scheme, a total 2 .1 lakh houses have been \nsanctioned; out of this, 1.4 lakh are at various st ages of construction and 1.1 lakh \nhave been completed/ delivered.  Central Assistance  of ₹3709 crore has been \nsanctioned; out of which, ₹1935 crore have been rel eased to the State of \nRajasthan and Central Nodal Agencies.  Central Assi stance of ₹1144 crore has \nbeen utilised during the last three years. A total of 466 cities/ towns of 33 \ndistricts in Rajasthan have been covered under the scheme.  \n(e) to (g): Yes, Sir.  Out of 2.1 lakh sanctioned h ouses in Rajasthan, 14,142 \nhouses have been sanctioned for the beneficiaries b elonging to Scheduled Tribe \ncategory. All houses under PMAY-U have basic amenit ies like water supply, \nelectricity, kitchen and toilet along with required  social and physical \ninfrastructure. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |536", "t": "Production from Energy Plants", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-536 \nANSWERED ON-04.02.2021 \n \nPRODUCTION FROM ENERGY PLANTS \n \n536. SHRI VISHNU DATT SHARMA \nSHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT \nSHRI JUGAL KISHORE SHARMA \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether energy plants for generating electricity, biogas, bio-CNG from solid waste are \nfunctioning across the country and if so, the details thereof along with the quantum of \nproduction/generation from these plants, State-wise; \n(b) whether the Government proposes to set up certain new energy plants; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the details of number of plants proposed to be set up in Jammu & Kashmir, Madhya \nPradesh and Bihar? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (I/C) FOR NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY, POWER and \nMoS for  SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n(a)  A total of 21 9 Waste-to-Energy plants w ith aggregate capacity of 373.5 MWeq \nhave been set up in the country to generate electricity or biogas or BioCNG from urban, \nindustrial, agricultural waste/residues and municipal solid waste. The State -wise details of \nwaste-to-energy plants set up, as on 31.12.2020, are given in Annexure. \n \n(b) to (d)  The Ministry of New and Renewable Energy,  under “Programme on Energy from \nUrban, Industrial, Agricultural Wastes /  Residues and Municipal Solid Waste ” provides \nassistance, including financial assistance to developers for setting up of Waste to Energy \nplants which produce Biogas or BioCNG  or Power from Urban, Industrial and Agricultural \nWaste / Residues  and municipal solid waste . As on 29.01.2021, under this Programme, this \nMinistry has issued sanction of CFA of Rs 48 Cr to 16 new Waste to Energy projects, with \ncumulative installed capacit y of 15.12 MWeq, located in the States of Andhra Pradesh, \nHaryana, Gujarat, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu, Telangana and Uttar Pradesh. \n \n***** \n \n  \n\nAnnexure \nAnnexure referred to in Reply to part (a) of the Lok Sabha unstarred Question No. 536 \nfor 04.02.2021 regarding “Production from Energy Plants” \n \n“State/UT-wise number and installed capacity of Waste -to-Energy plants set up in the  \ncountry, as on 31.12.2020” \n  Biogas projects \n(A) \nBioCNG Projects \n(B) \nPower projects \n(C) \nTotal Waste-\nto-Energy \n(A+B+C) \nS.No. State/UT Installed  \nCapacity \n(m3/day) \nNos. Installed  \nCapacity \n(kg/day) \nNos. Installed  \nCapacity \n(MW) \nNos. Installed  \nCapacity \n(MWeq) \nNos. \n1.  Andhra Pradesh 90540 7 850 1 43.8 19 51.5 26 \n2.  Bihar 12000 1 - - - - 1.0 1 \n3.  Chhattisgarh - - 400 1 0.3 1 0.4 2 \n4.  Delhi  -  -  - -  52.0 3 52.0 3 \n5.  Goa - - - - 0.3 1 0.3 1 \n6.  Gujarat 24800 4 39538 7 11.3 10 21.6 21 \n7.  Haryana - - 4250 3 5.2 3 6.1 6 \n8.  Himachal Pradesh 12000 1 - - - - 1.0 1 \n9.  Karnataka 58060 3 9521 3 7.8 6 14.6 11 \n10.  Kerala 2760 1 - - - - 0.2 1 \n11.  Madhya Pradesh 27014 5 1200 1 17.8 5 20.3 11 \n12.  Maharashtra 144636 12 27723 4 29.9 16 47.7 32 \n13.  Punjab 34800 5 1847 1 14.9 7 18.2 13 \n14.  Rajasthan - - 4000 2 3.0 1 3.8 3 \n15.  Tamil Nadu 150218 28 - - 11.6 8 24.1 35 \n16.  Telangana 37100 5 - - 46.8 6 49.9 11 \n17.  Uttar Pradesh 62320 6 2000 1 44.6 22 50.2 29 \n18.  Uttarakhand 73260 6 5880 2 1.9 2 9.2 10 \n19.  West Bengal 14000 2 - - - - 1.2 2 \n Grand Total 743508 86 97209 26 291.34 110 373.5 219* \n*3 projects are generating multiple outputs (Power & Biogas/BioCNG) counted as power \nprojects. \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |537", "t": "Cost of Solar Panels", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-537 \nANSWERED ON-04.02.2021 \n \nCOST OF SOLAR PANELS \n \n537. SHRI S. JAGATHRAKSHAKAN \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the whether the Government is aware that the domestic manufacturing sector needs doses \nof reforms and reliefs to match the cost of solar panels from China and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(b) whether a long-term perspective to manufacturing industry coupled with tariff and non-\ntariff barriers can make our domestic manufacturing sector truly „Atmanirbhar‟ and if so, \nthe details thereof; and \n(c) the measures taken/being taken by the Government thereto? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (I/C) FOR NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY, POWER and MoS \nfor  SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) Yes Sir.  \nThe Directorate General of Trade Remedies (DGTR) in its findings dated 18.07.2020 in matter \nof review investigation for continued imposition of Safeguard Duty on imports of “Solar Cells \nwhether or not assembled in modules or panels” into India, has stated that the imports of solar \ncells and modules in India have not only continued to cause serious inj ury to the Domestic \nIndustry but also threaten to cause serious injury to the domestic producers of solar cells and \nmodules.  \n(b) & (c) The Government has set a target of 450 GW of renewable power by year 2030, which \nwould inter-alia, require addition of around 30 GW of solar power per annum for around next 10 \nyears, thereby providing a long-term perspective for solar manufacturing industry. \n \nIn order to enhance India‟s manufacturing capabilities and enhancing exports under Atmanirbhar \nInitiative, on 11.11. 2020, the Government has approved the introduction of Production Linked \nIncentive (PLI) Scheme in ten sectors, including High Efficiency Solar PV Modules. The outlay \nfor PLI Scheme for High Efficiency Solar PV Modules is Rs. 4,500 crores. \n \nIn addition to P LI, the Government has taken following measures to support domestic solar PV \nmanufacturing: \n \n(i) Modified Special Incentive Package Scheme (M -SIPS) Scheme of Ministry of Electronics & \nInformation Technology: The scheme mainly provides subsidy for capital e xpenditure – 20% for \n\ninvestments in Special Economic Zones (SEZs) and 25% in non -SEZs. The Scheme was open to \nreceive applications till 31st December, 2018. \n \n(ii) Preference to „Make in India‟ in Public Procurement in Renewable Energy Sector: Ministry \nof N ew & Renewable Energy (MNRE) vide its Order No. 283/22/2019 -GRID SOLAR dated \n23.09.2020, has inter -alia, prescribed that in public procurement of solar PV modules and \ninverters, only Class -I local supplier shall be eligible to bid. Class -I local supplier m eans a \nsupplier or service provider, whose goods, services or works offered for procurement, has local \ncontent equal to or more than 50%. \n \n(iii) Imposition of Safeguard Duty on import of solar PV cells & modules: The Government, \nthrough notification no.  01/2018-Customs (SG) dated 30 th July, 2018, and notification no. \n02/2020-Customs (SG) dated 29 th July, 2020, has imposed Safeguard Duty on import of solar \ncells whether or not assembled in modules or panels. \n  \n(iv)  Domestic Content Requirement: Under the current schemes of the Ministry of New & \nRenewable Energy (MNRE) like CPSU Scheme Phase -II, PM -KUSUM and Grid -connected \nRooftop Solar Programme Phase-II, wherein government subsidy is given, it has been mandated \nto source solar PV cells and modules from domestic sources, as per extant Guidelines. \n \n(v) Increased duty on solar inverters and solar lanterns: To encourage domestic production, \nGovernment has raised duty on solar invertors from 5% to 20%, and on solar lanterns from 5% to \n15%. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |538", "t": "Funds for Polavaram Project", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 538\nANSWERED ON 04.02.2021\nFUNDS FOR POLAVARAM PROJECT\n538. SHRI JAYADEV GALLA\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Technical Advisory Committe e on Polavaram has approved Rs. 55,548.87 crores in\nFebruary, 2019, after studying in detail the estimates prepared by the Government of Andhra Pradesh and if\nso, the details thereof;\n(b) whether it is true that the Estimates Revised Committee has brought down the estimates approved by\nTechnical Advisory Committee by more than Rs.7,800 crores;\n(c) if so, the details thereof and the reasons for bringing down the estimates;\n(d) whether there is any reduction in the compensation paid to the affected people;\n(e) if so, the details thereof along with the reasons therefor; and\n(f) the quantum of fund so far paid for Polavaram and the details of dues yet to be paid and the time by\nwhich the Government is going to clear the dues?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI & SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA)\n(a) Advisory Committee of Department of Water Resources, River Development & Ganga Rejuvenation\n(DoWR, RD & GR) on Irrigation, Flood Control & Multipurpose Projects has accepted Revised Cost\nEstimate (RCE) of Polavaram Irrigation Project (PIP) at Rs.55,548.87 Crore (Irrigation plus water supply –\nRs.50,987.96 crore, and Power-Rs.4,560.91 crore). at 2017-18 price level (PL), in its 141 st meeting held on\n11th February, 2019.\n(b) to (e) In March, 2020, the Revised Cost Committee constituted by the Deptt re commended RCE of the\nPIP at Rs.47,725.74 crore (Irrigation plus water supply – Rs.43,164.83 crore, and Power-Rs.4,560.91 crore)\nat 2017 -18 PL. The cost difference between two estimates is primarily attributed to reduction in\nGovernment/Forest land, change in quantities for Left and Right Main Canals, etc. Further, the cost\ndifference has also arisen due to revision in infrastructure cost from Rs. 7.0 lakh to 6.52 lakh per Project\nDisplaced Family (PDF), as the GST on this component has been corrected from earlier 18% to 12%.\n(f) Ministry of Finance, Government of India vide its letter d ated 30.09.2016 informed that it will provide\n100% of the remaining cost of the irrigation component only of the PIP for the period starting from\n01.04.2014, to the extent of the cost of the irrigation component on the date. Accordingly, the Government\nof India has been reimbursing the expenditure incurred by the Govt of AP in the project with effect from\n01.04.2014, upon receipt of verified bills and recommendations from the Polavaram Project Authority\n(PPA) and Central Water Commission (CWC). According to the State Government, after declaration of the\nProject as National Project, an expenditure of Rs.12,311.32 crore has been incurred by the State on PIP up\nto 31.12. 2020. Central Assistance of Rs .10,848.36 crore (including Rs.2,234.20 crore in December, 20 20)\nhas been released from time to time to the project since April, 2014.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |539", "t": "Kuri Chhu Dam", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 539\nANSWERED ON 04.02.2021\nKURI CHHU DAM\n539. SHRI ABDUL KHALEQUE\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has contacted the concerned authority in Bhutan regarding regulation for\nrelease of Kuri Chhu dam water which causes flood in Barpeta HPC; and\n(b) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI & SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA)\n(a) & (b) Yes, Sir.\nThere is a structured Joint Group of Experts (JGE) between India and Bhutan to discuss all\nissues related to flood management. The JGE meets regularly and issues like landslides, river data, sharing\nfor advance flood warning, etc. are discussed by it. The specific issue of regulation of release of Kurichhu\nDam water was discussed with Bhutan during the meetings of JGE. The Bhutanese side informed during\nthe JGE meetings that it being a run -of-river project, the inflow is always equal to the outflow from the\npower project, and therefore, there is no question of extra releases of water from the project.\nApart from this, there exists a bilateral arrangement under international co -operation between\nRoyal Government of Bhutan and Government of lndia for sharing of data of common rivers for flood\nmonitoring and flood forecasting in India.Under the bilateral arrangement, Central Water Commission\n(CWC)  receives data of rivers flowing from Bhutan to India during monsoon season.\nThe data so received is considered for formulation of flood forecast in these rivers and the same\nis shared with concerned Agencies of State & Central Government.\nFurther, the discharge releases from the Kurichhu project is shared by the Druk Green Power\nCorporation who is Project Authority and the Technical Maintenance Officer (Hydromet Operations &\nInfrastructures Division), National Center for Hydrology and Meteorology, Royal Government of Bhutan\nwith Central Water Commission offices in India at Barpeta and Guwahati in Assam. The same is further\nshared with the District Administrations of Chirang, Baksa and Barpeta districts as well as Water\nResources and Irrigation Departments of Bodoland Territorial Council, Water Resources Department of\nGovernment of Assam and Assam State Disaster Management Authority for action at their end.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |54", "t": "Gram Panchayat Development Plan", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.54 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \nTRANSMISSION AND DISTRIBUTION INFRASTRUCTURE \n \n*54. SHRI RAHUL GANDHI: \n \nWill the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether his Ministry has received proposals from the Kerala State Electricity \nBoard to improve the transmission and distribution infrastructure in Wayanad;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether the Ministry has approved the project proposal and sanction ed funds \nfor the same; \n \n(d)  whether the Ministry is currently executing any project to strengthen the \npower infrastructure and bridge the growing demand -supply gap for electricity in \nWayanad; \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(f)  the details of electricity related projects completed and being executed under \nthe aspirational districts programme in Wayanad? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER, NEW & \nRENEWABLE ENERGY AND THE MINISTER OF STATE FOR SKILL DEVE LOPMENT & \nENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n(SHRI  R.K. SINGH)  \n \n(a) to (f) : A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n******* \n  \n\nSTATEMENT \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO.54 TO BE ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 04.02.2021 \nREGARDING TRANSMISSION AND DISTRIBUTION INFRASTRUCTURE. \n********* \n \n \n(a) to (f) :  Government of India approved schemes worth  Rs. 2,02,266 crore \nunder Deen Dayal Upadhyaya Gram Jyoti Yojana (DDUGY); Integrated Power \nDevelopment Scheme (IPDS); Pradhan Mantri  Sahaj Bijli Har Ghar Yojana \n(SAUBHAGYA) (along with their subsumed components) to assist the State \nGovernments to strengthen their transmission and distribution systems and \nensure electricity  access to all households. Under the schemes, projects \nworth Rs . 2786 crore were sanctioned for Kerala based on the proposal \nreceived from the State Government. \n \nThe Ministry of Power had received a letter dated 10.09.2020 \nfrom the Member of Parliament of Wayanad, Kerala, enclosing a copy of  a \nDPR of Kerala State Electricity Board (KSEB) for improving the transmission \nand distribution infrastructure in Kalpetta Circle, Wayand district in Kerala. \nThe schemes of DDUGJY and IPDS were approved in the year 2014 and have \na sunset date of 31 st March,2022 and therefore it would not be possible to \nsanction new projects under these schemes. However, in the Union Budget \nof 2021 -22, the Government of India have announced the launching of a \nrevamped reforms-based and results -linked Distribution Sector scheme that \nwill provide assistan ce to State Governments for further strengthening and \nupgradation of their transmission and distribution infrastructure. The State \nGovernment of Kerala can propose projects under the above scheme.  \n \nProviding electricity connection to hous eholds is one of the \nbenchmark/aspirational targets which is monitored under Aspirational \nDistrict Programme and as per information available on NITI Aayog \ndashboard, the percentage of household s with electricity connection in \nWayanad district is 100%.  \n \n*********** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |540", "t": "Impact of GST on MSMEs", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED\n \nQUESTION NO.  No. 540 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04\n.\n02\n.\n2021\n \n \nIMPACT OF GST ON MSMEs\n \n \n540\n.\n \n \nSHRI VIJAY KUMAR:\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state: \n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the Goods and Service Tax (GST) has had an adverse impact over MSMEs thereby \naffecting this sector a lot;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nwhether the Government has devised any robust policy to protect and free the micro, small \nand medium entrepreneurs from adverse impact of GST as well as to encourage them; and  \n \n(\nd\n)\n \nif so, the details thereof ?\n \n      \n \nANSWE\nR\n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM\n \nENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NITIN GADKARI)\n \n \n(a)\n \n&\n \n(b)\n: \n \nNo such information have been received by this Ministry.\n \n \n(c) \n&\n \n(d) : \n \nThe Government has taken numerous measures with a view to encourage micro, \nsmall and medium entrepreneurs. These include the following:\n \n \n\n \nNo GST registration is required for inter\n-\nstate and intra\n-\nstate supply of services upto Rs.20 \nlakhs in a year (Rs.10 lakhs for the States of Manipur, Mizoram, Nagaland and Tripura).\n \n\n \nNo registration is required for intra\n-\nState supply of goods upto Rs. 40 la\nkh in a year (Rs. 20 \nlakh in the states of Arunachal Pradesh, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, \nPuducherry, Sikkim, Telangana, Tripura and Uttarakhand) w.e.f. 01.04.2019.\n \n\n \nComposition Scheme has been formulated for small businessmen being supplier of g\noods and \nsupplier of restaurant services. \n \n\n \nComposition Scheme has also been formulated for supplier of services from F.Y. 2019\n-\n2020. \n \n\n \nFiling of Annual Return in FORM GSTR\n-\n9 or FORM GSTRR\n-\n9A for F.Y. 2017\n-\n18, 2018\n-\n19 \nand 2019\n-\n20 has been made optional for t\naxpayers having aggregate annual turnover less than \n2 crore rupees. \n \n\n \nQRMP (Quarterly Return, Monthly Payment) Scheme:\n \nQRMP scheme has been launched with \neffect from 01.01.2021 under which registered persons having aggregate turnover up to five (5) \ncrore ru\npees would be allowed to furnish return on quarterly basis along with monthly payment \nof tax. \nThis would considerably reduce compliance under GST for such taxpayers.\n \nFlexibility \nhas also been provided for taxpayers to easily opt\n-\nin and opt\n-\nout of the schem\ne throughout the \nyear.  \n \n\n \nExtension of compliance deadlines and waiver of late fees to cope with COVID\n-\n19 pandemic\n: \nThe due date for compliance which fell during the period from 20.03.2020 to 30.08.2020 was \nextended till 31.08.2020. Conditional waiver of late fees has been granted for delayed filing of \nFORM GSTR\n-\n3B for the period from July, 2017 to July, 2020. Late fe\ne has also been waived \nfor delayed filing of monthly FORM GSTR\n-\n1 (from March, 2020 till June, 2020) and quarterly \nFORM GSTR\n-\n1 (from January, 2020 to June, 2020). \n \n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |541", "t": "Guidelines under PMAY", "head": " \n \nO.I.H  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 541 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nGUIDELINES UNDER PMAY \n \nNO. 541.  SHRI RAMCHARAN BOHRA: \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBANAFFAIRS be pl eased to \nstate: \n(a)  whether the guidelines of the housing scheme m eant for the \nurban areas under Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana (PMAY)  have \nbeen amended; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether the logic behind this amendment has be en cited as \nextending the coverage of the scheme; and \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n(a)&(b): Since the launch of the Pradhan Mantri Awa s Yojana \n(Urban) [PMAY(U)] in June, 2015 some amendments hav e been \nmade in the Mission Guidelines from time to time as  per \nrequirement of the scheme. These amendments include  – change in \nsize of carpet area, definition of beneficiary fami ly, inclusion of \nNotified Planning / Development Area under the Sche me, making \nAadhaar mandatory for PMAY(U) through Gazette Notif ication, \nlaunch of Credit Linked Subsidy Scheme (CLSS) for M iddle Income \nGroup (CLSS for MIG) and its extension from time to  time, inclusion \nof State Bank of India as Central Nodal Agency for CLSS,  etc.  \nContd…2/ \n \n \n\n \n \n-2- \n \n \n(c)&(d):Para 2.1 of the PMAY—U Guidelines was amend ed to \nenhance the coverage area under the scheme and now it reads as \nunder: \n“All the statutory towns as per Census 2011 and tow ns notified \nsubsequently including Notified Planning/ Developme nt Areas shall \nbe eligible for coverage in the Mission. \nAreas falling within Notified Planning/ Development  area under the \njurisdiction of an Industrial Development Authority / Special Area \nDevelopment Authority/ Urban Development Authority \nor any such Authority under State legislation which  is entrusted \nwith the functions of urban planning and regulation s shall also be \nincluded for the coverage under PMAY(U)”. \nLatest version of PMAY-U guidelines is available at  the following \nlink: https://pmay-urban.gov.in/uploads/guidelines/ 60180813309f2-\nUpdated%20guidelines%20of%20PMAY-U.pdf  \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |542", "t": "Technology Upgradation in MSME Sector", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 542\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021\n \n \nTECHNOLOGY UPGRADATION IN MSME SECTOR\n \n \n542.\n \n \nSHRI KANUMURU RAGHU RAMA KRISHNA RAJU:\n \n  \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to \nstate:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the Government has any proposal of taking up Technology \nUpgradation in the Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSME) Sector in the \ncountry; \n \n(\nb\n)\n \nif so, the details thereof,\n \nand \n \n(\nc\n)\n \nthe details of the initiatives taken to allow the MSMEs to access the global \nmarkets, improved research and development and investments proposed to indigenous \nMSMEs?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NITIN GADKARI)  \n \n \n(a)\n \n& (b)\n: \n \nYes, \nSir. Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSME) is \nimplementing various schemes and programmes to provide access to technology \nupgradation. These schemes include Credit Linked Capital Subsidy Scheme and \nTechnology Upgradation Sche\nme (CLCS\n-\nTUS), Micro and Small Enterprise \n–\n \nCluster \nDevelopment Programme (MSE\n-\nCDP), MSME Technology Centres (MSME\n-\nTCs), \nTechnology Centre Systems Programme (TCSP).\n \n \n(c):\n \n \n \nMinistry of MSME is implementing International Cooperation scheme to \nfacilitate the\n \nMSMEs business delegations to visit / participate in international \nexhibitions/ trade fairs etc., of other countries for exploring new areas of technology \ninfusion/ up\n-\ngradation, facilitating of joint ventures, improving market for MSMEs \nproducts, foreign\n \ncollaborations amongst others. \n \n \n*****\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |543", "t": "FASTag System at Toll Plazas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 543 \nANSWERED ON 4TH FEBRUARY, 2021 \nFASTAG SYSTEM AT TOLL PLAZAS \n543. SHRI DAYANIDHI MARAN: \n  Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nlM+d ifjogu vkSj jktekxZ ea=h \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government is aware of media reports of long queues at \nToll Plazas despite implementation of FASTags across the country and if \nso, the details thereof along  with  the  reaction  of  the  Government  \nthereto; \n(b)  the  comparison  of  time  taken  to  pass  through toll  points  \nincluding  waiting  time  before  and  after implementation of FASTag; \n(c)  whether complaints  have  been  raised  about failures in FASTag \nsystem and if so, the details thereof; and \n(d)  the  steps  being  taken  to  improve  efficiency  of FASTag  system? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) Cases of queues arise in ‘FASTag lanes’ at fee plazas due to vehicles \nnot fitted with “FASTag” or vehicle s without valid, functional “FASTag” \nentering into  it. In the hybrid lane , which accepts cash , queues arise \nbecause of non -FASTag vehicles . Sub-rule (3) of Rule 6 of the National \nHighways Fee (Determination of Rates and Collection) Rules, 2008, as \namended, provides that vehicles not fitted with “FASTag” or vehicle s \nwithout valid, functional “FASTag” have to pay double the applicable fee \nupon entering a FASTag lane at the fee plaza. \n\n(b) On arriving at the pay -axis of the toll booth, fee payment via FASTag \ncan be done within 5 seconds, whereas in cash payment , it usually takes \nmore than 40 seconds. Waiting time in FASTag lanes has significantly \nreduced after implementation of FASTag , which will further improve as \nthe system gets more operationalised.  However, the waiting time in the \nhybrid lane , which accepts cash , is relatively longer  because of non -\nFASTag vehicles.  \n(c) A r obust customer redressal mechanism has been put in place to \naddress various customer complaints. N ational Highways Auth ority of \nIndia (NHAI) has set up a dedicated Toll-free Helpline (1033) round the \nclock for complaints related to FASTag. Additionally, all member banks \nare mandatorily required to set up a dedicated Customer Care number to \naddress FASTag customers’ complaints or queries. \n(d) The Government has taken various progressive measures for the \nadoption of FASTag, increasing efficiency of the FASTag programme and \nproviding a smooth customer experience. In order to ensure easy \navailability of FASTag to vehicle owners, multiple channels like banks, \npetrol pumps, e -commerce websites, etc. have been engaged. The \nfacility of instant recharge  via UPI on various UPI -enabled Apps such as \nBHIM, Google Pay, Phone Pe, Paytm, etc.  has been provided.  My FASTag \nApp has been developed as a one -stop solution for FASTag customers, \nwith facility to check real time balance status of FASTag.  Further, NHAI \nis resorting to upgradation of  the fee plaza system to facilitate \nreal time transaction processing. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |544", "t": "Construction of New Dams", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA\nREJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 544\nANSWERED ON 04.02.2021\nCONSTRUCTION OF NEW DAMS\n544. SHRI RAMALINGAM S.\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Union Government has constructed any new dams in the country during the last three\nyears and if so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether the Union Government has any data with regard to co nstruction of new dams by any State\nGovernments across the country during the last three years;\n(c) if so, the details thereof; and\n(d) the details of total number of dams comes under the direct control of Union Government?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI & SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA)\n(a)  Dam Safety Organization (DSO) in the Central Water Commission compiles and maintains\nnation-wide register of Large Dams (NRLD) as per input/ information provided by dam owners. Latest\nedition of NRLD was published in June 2019. As per NRLD 2019, the country has 5334 completed\nlarge dams and 411 under-construction large dams. As per NRLD 2019, Union Government has\nnot constructed any new large dam in the country during the last 5 years.\nHowever, Union Government is providing assistance to State Governments for irrigation\nprojects through various schemes for harnessing desired outcome/ benefit i.e. Irrigation Potential\ncreation/utilization. Details are placed at Annexure-I.\n(b) & (c) As per NRLD 2019, 13 Large dams have been constructed since 2016. Details are as per\nAnnexureII.\n(d)   As water is a State Subject, ownership and operation of dams lies with the State Governments,\nState PSUs and a few Hydro power Generating Central PSUs. As per NRLD 2019, No large dam\ncomes under the direct and exclusive control of the Union Government.\n*****\n\nANNEXURE-I\nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.544 to be\nanswered on 04.02.2021 on ‘CONSTRUCTION OF NEW DAMS’.\nThe Union Government had launched Accelerated Irrigation Benefit Programme (AIBP) in 1996-\n97 to expedite the implementation of ongoing Major, Medium Irrigation Projects which are in\nadvance stage of construction and for which State Government is facing financial resource\ncrunch. Since the inception of Accelerated Irrigation Benefit Programme (AIBP), 297 MMI\nprojects have been benefitted. 143 projects have been completed, whereas 5 projects have been\ndeferred from the AIBP due to their inadequate performance and non-submission of subsequent\nproposals for seeking Central Assistance (CA) under AIBP. 149 projects were ongoing under\nAIBP as on March 2015. During 2015-16, Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY)\nwas conceived by the Central Government with an aim to enhance physical access of water on\nfarm and expand cultivable area under assured irrigation, improve on farm water use efficiency,\nintroduce sustainable water conservation practices etc to produce per drop more crop, thus bringing\nmuch desired rural prosperity. The programmes as being implemented by the Govt. of India, viz.\nAccelerated Irrigation Benefits Programmes (AIBP); Repair, Renovation and Restoration\n(RRR) and Command Area Development and Water Management (CADWM) have been\nsubsumed in PMKSY. With the vision of extending the coverage of irrigation ‘Har Khet ko\npani’ and improving water use efficiency ‘More crop per drop' the scheme has been\nformulated under PMKSY in a focused manner with end to end solution on source creation,\ndistribution, management, field application and extension activities. The schemes “Surface\nMinor Irrigation (SMI) & RRR of water bodies” are now part of PMKSY (Har Khet ko\npani, HKKP). Under the PMKSY-AIBP, 99 priority projects have been identified out of\nongoing 149 major / medium irrigation projects under AIBP with a dedicated funding\nmechanism to complete them in a time bound manner. 44 priority projects have been completed\nas on 31st March 2020 and the remaining are targeted to be completed soon. In addition,\nGovernment of India has sanctioned a special package for completion of Irrigation Projects to\naddress agrarian distress in Vidarbha and Marathwada and other chronically drought prone areas\nof Rest of Maharashtra during July, 2018. The package consists of 8 Major and Medium\nIrrigation (MMI) Projects approved by TAC of MoWR, RD & GR and 83 Surface Minor\nIrrigation (SMI) Projects.\n\nANNEXURE-II\nAnnexure referred to in reply to parts (b) & (c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.544 to be\nanswered on 04.02.2021 on ‘CONSTRUCTION OF NEW DAMS’.\nLarge Dams Constructed since 2016 (As per NRLD 2019)\nSr.\nNo PIC\nNam\ne of\nDam\nState Latitude Longitude\nYear of\nCompletio\nn\nRiver\nBasin River\nNear\neast\nCity\n1 GJ04VH0\n630\nSardar\nSarover Gujarat 21° 49 '\n47\"\n73° 45 '\n5\" 2017 Narmada Narmada Rajpipla\n2 JK30MH0\n016\nKISHANG\nANGA\nDAM\nJammu &\nKashmir\n34° 30 '\n51\"\n74° 45 '\n10\" 2018 Indus Up to\nBorder\nMod\nNadi\nBandip\nora\n3 MH09MH\n2417 Kinkhed Maharashtra 20°"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |545", "t": "Consumption of Electricity", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.545 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \nCONSUMPTION OF ELECTRICITY \n \n†545.  SHRI ARUN SAO: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the consumption of electricity has increased in the country during the last \nthree years and the current year and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  whether power generation in the country is sufficient to meet the demand and if so, \nthe details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c)  the steps being taken by the Government to meet the power demand; \n \n(d)  whether the Government has given approval to new power projects in the country; \nand \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof, State-wise including Chhattisgarh? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER, NEW & RENEWABLE \nENERGY AND THE MINISTER OF STATE FOR SKILL DEVELOPMENT & ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n(SHRI  R.K. SINGH)  \n \n(a) :  Yes, Sir. The consumption of electrical energy has increased from 12,04,697 \nMillion Units (MU) i n 2017-18 to 12,84,444 MUs in 2019 -20. The peak electricity demand has \nalso increased from 1,60,752 MW in 2017 -18 to 1,89,644 MW during the current year 2020 -21 \n(up to 31.01.2021). Inspite of COVID -19, the country has experienced the highest ever peak \ndemand of 1,89,644 MW on 30.01.2021 showing the revival of economic growth. \n \n(b) :  Yes, Sir. The installed generation capacity is around 3,75,323 MW which is \nsufficient to meet the demand of electricity in the country. \n \n(c) :  Following steps are being taken to increase power production to meet the \npower demand in the country: \n \n(i) Conventional power generation capacity to be commissioned by 2024 -25 is at various \nstages of construction in the country which includes Thermal 36,765 MW, Hydro \n10,164.50 MW and Nuclear 4,800 MW. \n \n(ii) Government of India ha ve set a target of 1,75,000 MW installed capacity from \nrenewable sources by the end of 2021 -22 which includes 1,00,000 MW from Solar, \n60,000 MW from Wind, 10,000 MW from Biomass and 5,000 MW from small Hydro. \n \n(d) & (e) : As per the Electricity Act, 2003, generation is a de -licensed activity and the \ninvestment decision for setting up of power projects is made by the concerned developers. \nHowever, the State -wise details of the new projects of the Central Public Sect or \nUndertakings (CPSUs) for which the Central Government has given investment approval are \nfurnished at Annexure. \n**********  \n\n \nANNEXURE  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (d) & (e) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n545 TO BE ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 04.02.2021. \n************* \n \nProjects for which investment approval given by Central Government  \nHydro Projects \n \nSl. \nNo. \nState/UT Name of the Project Capacity in MW \n1. UT of J&K Pakal Dul HE Project 1000 \n2. UT of J&K Kiru HE Project 624 \n3. Sikkim Teesta VI HE Project 500 \n4. Arunachal Pradesh Dibang Multipurpose Project 2880 \n5. UT of J&K Ratle HE Project 850 \n6. Uttarakhand Koteshwar HEP 400 \n7. Uttarakhand Naitwar Mori HEP 60 \n8. Himachal Pradesh Luhri HEP Stage-I 210 \n9. Himachal Pradesh Dhaulasidh HEP 66 \n \nThermal Projects \n \nSl. \nNo. \nState/UT Name of the Project Capacity in MW \n1. Bihar Buxar Thermal Power \nProject \n2x660 \n2. Uttar Pradesh Khurja Super Thermal Power \nPlant \n2x660 \n \n************* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |546", "t": "Road Accidents in Maharashtra", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 546  \nANSWERED ON 4th FEBRUARY, 2021 \n \nROAD ACCIDENTS IN MAHARASHTRA \n \n546. SHRIMATI POONAM MAHAJAN:  \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a)  the total number of accidents occurred, persons killed and injured \non National Highways (NH) due to damaged highways and traffic \ncongestion in Maharashtra during the last three years;  \n(b)  whether it is a fact that majority of these accidents were caused \nby heavy vehicles, mainly trucks and buses, passing through the \nmain cities;  \n(c)  if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the remedial measures taken by the Government to prevent \naccidents on NHs in Maharashtra? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a)    Ministry does not maintain the information regarding accidents \noccurring due to damaged highways and traffic congestion. However, \nas per the data received from police department total number of road \naccidents, persons killed & persons injured on National Highways in  \n \n \n\nMaharashtra during last three calendar years i. e. 2017 to 2019 is given \nin the table below:- \nYears Total \nNumber of \nRoad \naccidents on \nall roads \nTotal Number \nof Road \naccidents on \nNational \nHighways \nTotal Number \nof Road \naccidents in \nMaharashtra on \nNational \nHighways \nTotal \nNumber of \npersons \nkilled on all \nroads \nTotal Number \nof persons \nkilled on \nNational \nHighways \n1 2 3 4 5 6 \n2017 464,910 141466 9237(6.53) 147,913 53181 \n2018 467,044 140843 9355(6.64) 151,417 54046 \n2019 449,002 137191 8360(6.09) 151,113 53872 \n \nTotal Number of \npersons killed in \nMaharashtra on \nNational Highways \nTotal Number of \npersons injured \non all roads \nTotal Number of \npersons injured on \nNational Highways \nTotal Number of \npersons injured in \nMaharashtra on \nNational Highways \n7 8 9 10 \n3637(2.46) 470,975 142622 8351(5.86) \n4088(2.70) 469,418 140622 8457(6.01) \n3799(2.51) 451,361 137549 7263(5.28) \n(b) &   (c)    No Sir.   Road Accidents occuring due to multiple causes \nsuch as over speeding, drunken driving/consumption of alcohol, driving \non wrong side/lane indiscipline, fault of the driver of motor vehicle, use \nof mobile phones during driving, mechanical defect/fault of the vehicle \ndefect in road condition, weather condition and    lack of knowledge on \nroad signage etc. These various factors can broadly be categorized into \nhuman error, road condition/environment and vehicular condition that \nlead to accidents on roads. \n(d) The Motor Vehicle (Amendment) Act, 2019 which was recently \npassed by Parliament focuses on road safety and includes, inter -alia, \nrevision in penalties for traffic violations, electronic monitoring of  the \nsame, enhanced penalties for juvenile driving, computerization/automation \nof vehicle fitness and driving, tests, recall of defective vehicles, \nstreamlining the third party insurance and payment of increased \ncompensation for hit and run cases etc. The Amendment has strengthened \nthe Law to improve the road Safety scenario and reduce loss of lives.  \n \nThe Ministry issues regulations to improve vehicle safety standards, \nundertakes site specific interventions to address black spots, and also \nmobilises various activities to promote road safety such as road safety \nadvocacy and awareness programmes.  \n               The Ministry of Road Transport &  Highways has notified \n“Member of Parliaments’ Road Safety Committee” in each district of the \ncountry to audit promote awareness amongst road users under the \n\nchairmanship of Hon’ble Member of Parliament (Lok Sabha) from the \ndistrict.  \nThe Ministry has formulated a multi-pronged strategy to address the \nissue of road safety based on Education, Engineering (both of roads and \nvehicles), Enforcement and Emergency Care. Accordingly, various \ninitiatives have been taken by the Ministry as detailed below:- \n(i) Education:   \n The Ministry implements a scheme for undertaking publicity \nmeasures and awareness campaigns on road safety to create \nawareness among road users through the electronic media, print \nmedia, NGOs etc.  \n Observance of National Road Safety month every year fo r \nspreading awareness and strengthening road safety. \n A Certification Course for Road Safety Auditors has been \ncommenced in Indian Academy of Highway Engineers (IAHE). \n(ii) Engineering (both of Roads and vehicles) \n Road engineering \n High priority to identification and rectification of black spots \n(accident prone spots) on national highways. \n Road safety has been made an integral part of road design at \nplanning stage. \n The threshold for four laning of National Highways have been \nreduced from 15,000 Passenger Car Units  (PCUs) to 10,000 \nPCUs. \n Ministry has delegated powers to Regional Officers of MORTH \nfor technical approval of the detailed estimates for \nrectification of identified Road Accident black"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |547", "t": "Public Procurement Policy for MSEs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 547\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021\n \n \nPUBLIC PROCUREMENT POLICY FOR MSEs\n \n \n547.\n \n \nSHRI JAYANT SINHA:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \nbe please to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe objectives of the Public Procurement Policy for Micro and Small Enterprises \n(MSEs);\n \n(b) \n \nthe total annual procurement from such MSEs, sector\n-\nwise, category\n-\nwise (buyers), \nand year\n-\nwise during the last three years; and\n \n(c) \n \nth\ne total annual procurement from such MSEs in Jharkhand along the same \nparameters?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NITIN GADKARI)\n \n \n(a)\n:\n \nThe Public Procurement Policy for Micro and Small Enterprises (MSEs) Order, 2012 \nhas been notified by this Ministry, under Section 11 of MSMED  Act,  2006,  the same being \neffective  from 1st April, 2012, and has become mandatory w.e.f. April 2015. The pol\nicy was \namended vide Gazette Notification No. S.O. 5670(E), dated 9th November, 2018. The said \nPolicy, as amended, mandates 25% annual procurement from MSEs by Central Ministries/ \nDepartments/ Central Public Sector Enterprises (CPSEs) including 4% from MSE\ns owned by \nSC/ST and 3% from MSEs owned by Women entrepreneurs.\n \n \n(b)\n:\n \nAs per information reported by \nCentral Public Sector Enterprises (CPSEs) \non MSME \nSAMBANDH Portal, the details of the annual procurement of goods and services from \nMSEs, by CPSEs and \nyear\n-\nwise during the last three years (as on date 01.02.2021) is given as \nunder:\n \n \nFinancial \nYear\n \nBuyers\n \nTotal \nProcurement \n \n(Rs in Crores)\n \nProcurement from \nMSEs(including \nSC/ST owned MSEs)\n \n(Rs in Crores)\n \nProcurement from \nMSEs owned by \nSC/ST\n \n(Rs in Crores)\n \nP\nrocurement from \nMSEs owned by \nWomen\n \n(Rs in Crores)\n \n2017\n-\n18\n \n \n169 \nCPSEs\n \n114,042.05\n \n26357.46\n \n(23.11%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  89631)\n \n544.72\n \n(0.48%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  2255)\n \n-\n \n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n \n165 \nCPSEs\n \n153,477.05\n \n40,399.54\n \n(26.32%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  \n128124)\n \n824.71\n \n(0.54%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  4587)\n \n232.56\n \n(0.15%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  1410)\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n \n136 \nCPSEs\n \n123,717.50\n \n \n38,275.11\n \n(30.94%)\n \n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  156744)\n \n685.21\n \n(0.55%)\n \n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  6313)\n \n390.90\n \n(0.32%)\n \n \n(No. of MSEs\n \nbenefited  3613)\n \n\n \n \n \n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \nInformation are available CPSE\n-\nwise, and not Sector\n-\nwise or Category\n-\nwise.\n \n \n(c)\n:\n \nAs per available information, Sector\n-\nwise figures are not maintained\n.\n \nTotal annual \nprocurement under the said Policy in Jharkhand State from MSEs is as under:\n \n \nFinancial Year\n \nBuyers\n \nTotal Procurement from MSEs in Jharkhand\n \nRs.(In crores)\n \n2017\n-\n18\n \n9\n \n148.21\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n9\n \n139.89\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n \n9\n \n207.22\n \n \n \n******\n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |548", "t": "New Buildings under Central Vista Redevelopment Project", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 548 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nNEW BUILDINGS UNDER CENTRAL VISTA REDEVELOPMENT PROJECT \n \nNO. 548    SHRI SUBBARAYAN K : \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether all the Central Ministries are proposed to be housed \nat the new buildings to be constructed at the Rajpa th’s new \ncentral vista avenue under the Central Vista Redeve lopment \nProject;   \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and   \n(c)  the fate of the existing buildings such as North an d South \nBlocks , Krishi Bhavan, Nirman Bhavan etc. includin g the \nnewly built External Affairs Ministry office and so me other \noffices under construction at various parts of the Capital?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a)  & (b) Yes, all the Central Ministries are proposed to be housed in \nthe new buildings to be constructed at the Rajpath’ s central vista \navenue under the Central Vista Redevelopment Project.  \n \n(c)  As per Central Vista Development/ Redevelopmen t Plan evolved so \nfar, North and South Blocks, being heritage buildin gs will be retained \nand will be retrofitted and used for National Museu m. As regards other \nbuildings, projects are not yet finalized.   \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |549", "t": "Road Construction Work in Kerala", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.549 \nANSWERED ON 4TH FEBRUARY, 2021 \n  \nROAD CONSTRUCTION WORK IN KERALA \n  \n549.   SHRI V.K.SREEKANDAN: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has taken several initiatives to \nincrease the pace of road construction and plans to further \nspeed up the road works in the remaining period of the current \nfinancial year and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether the Government proposes to complete 11,000 km of \nroads before 31.03.2021 and if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government was able to construct about 29 km per \nday of road during the current financial year till date and if so, \nthe details thereof; and \n(d) whether the Government aims to complete the remaining road \nworks in the State of Kerala in the current financial year and if \nso, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a) & (b) During the current financial year 2020 -21, Ministry has set \nan overall target of 11,000 km for construction/development of \nNational Highways (NHs) against which 7767 km of NHs   have \nbeen constructed upto 31 December, 2020. \nSeveral initiatives have been taken to increase the pace of road \nconstruction to ensure timely completion of the projects, such as: \n\n  holding regular meetings with other Ministries, Project \nDevelopers, State Governments and contractors to sort out \nissues of  land acquisition, utility s hifting, forest permission \netc. \n all possible help extended to the Contractors, Concessionaires \nand developers in Road Sector by way of (i) additional  time \nextensions (3-6 months), (ii) relaxation in Contract provisions \nfor ensur ing cash flow, (iii) direct payment to sub -\ncontractors   and release of retention money to augment cash \nflow, (iv) waiver of penalty in case of delay in submission of \nPerformance Security (for new Contracts) as part of the recent \ninitiative of Atmanirbhar Bharat duly considering COVID \nsituation. \n(c) Average pace of National Highways constructed in the current \nfinancial year up to  December, 2020 is 28.24 km per day. \n(d)  Development and maintenance of National Highways is a \ncontinuous process. In Kerala 318  km road is targeted to be \ncompleted during current financial year and so far 171 km has been \ncompleted up to December, 2020. All out efforts are being made to \nachieve this target.   \n \n***** \n  \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |55", "t": "Death of Manual Scavengers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 55 \nANSWERED ON 04/02/2021 \n \nSOLAR PARKS \n \n*55.SHRI REBATI TRIPURA \n       SHRI VIJAY KUMAR DUBEY \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has sanctioned setting up  of  solar  parks  in  different  parts  of  \nNorth-Eastern States including Tripura and also Uttar Pradesh specially Kushinagar; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the funds allocated and spent for the purpose, State-\nwise, in the above  States; \n(c) whether the Government has also proposed to set up hydro power plants in the said States; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the  other  steps  taken  by  the  Government  for harnessing  and  utilizing  the  solar  \nenergy  with  special focus on North-Eastern States and Uttar Pradesh? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (I/C) FOR NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY, POWER and \nMoS for  SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION \nNO. 55 (15TH POSITION) FOR 04.02.2021 REGARDING SOLAR PARKS  \n \n(a)&(b) The details of  solar  parks sanctioned  alongwith the Central Financial Assistance \nreleased therefor, in  North -Eastern States and also in Uttar Pradesh under the Scheme for \nDevelopment of Solar Parks  and Ultra Mega Power Projects  are given at Annexure-I. No \nSolar Park has been sanctioned in Tripura for want of proposal from the State Government. \n(c) and (d) No new hydro projects are under consideration of the Government  in the States of \nUttar Pradesh and Tripura. \n \n(e) The Government has undertaken several measures for harnessing and utilizing the solar \nenergy in the country including North-Eastern States and Uttar Pradesh. These include: \n \n• permitting Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) up to 100 percent under the automatic route,  \n• waiver of Inter State Transmission System (ISTS) charges and losses for int er-state sale of   \n   solar and wind power for projects to be commissioned by 30th June 2023,  \n• declaration of trajectory for Renewable Purchase Obligation (RPO) up to the year 2022,  \n• setting up of Ultra Mega Renewable Energy Parks to provide land and transmission on plug   \n   and play basis,  \n• Schemes such as Pradhan Mantri Kisan Urja Suraksha evam Utthaan Mahabhiyan (PM -  \n    KUSUM), Solar Rooftop Phase II, 12000 MW CPSU Scheme Phase II, Solar Park Scheme   \n    etc., \n• laying of new transmission lines and creating new sub -station capacity under the Green   \n   Energy Corridor Scheme for evacuation of renewable power, \n• notification of standards for deployment of solar photovoltaic system/devises,  \n• setting up of Project Development Cell for attracting and facilitating investments, \n• Standard Bidding Guidelines for tariff based competitive bidding process for procurement   \n   of Power from Grid Connected Solar PV and Wind Projects, wherein adequate provisions  \n   have been made to protect Power Purchase Agreements.  \n• Government has issued orders that power shall be dispatched against Letter of Credit (LC)   \n   or advance payment to ensure timely payment by Distribution Licensees to RE generators. \n \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) and (b) OF LOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 55  FOR 04.02.2021 \n \nDETAILS OF SOLAR PARKS SANCTIONED IN NORTH-EASTERN STATES AND \nIN UTTAR PRADESH \n \nSl. \nNo. \nState Solar Park Capacity  \n(MW)  \nName of the \nSolar Power \nPark \nDeveloper \n(SPPD) \nCentral \nFinance \nAssistance \n(CFA) \ndisbursed  \n(in Lakh) \n1.  Arunachal \nPradesh \nLohit Solar \nPark 20 \nArunachal \nPradesh \nEnergy \nDevelopment \nAgency \n(APEDA) \n19.65 \n2.  Manipur Bukpi Solar \nPark 20 \nManipur Tribal \nDevelopment \nCorpn. Ltd. \n(MTDCL) \n10.00 \n3.  Meghalaya \nSolar park \nin \nMeghalaya \n20 \nMeghalaya \nPower \nGeneration \nCorporation \nLtd (MePGCL) \n3.07 \n4.  Mizoram Vankal \nSolar Park 20 \nPower & \nElectricity \nDepartment \n10.00 \n5.  \nUttar \nPradesh \nSolar Park \nin UP 440 \nLucknow \nSolar Power \nDevelopment \nCorporation \nLtd. (LSPDCL) \nJVC of \nUPNEDA & \nSECI \n3800.95 \n6.  Jalaun \nSolar Park 1200 BSUL \n0.00 7.  Lalitpur \nSolar Park 600 \nTUSCO Ltd \n(JV of THDCIL \nand UPNEDA) \n8.  Jhansi \nSolar Park 600 \nTUSCO Ltd \n(JV of THDCIL \nand UPNEDA) \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |550", "t": "Scheme for Character Development of the Young Students", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.550 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \nScheme for Character Development of the Young Students \n \n550. SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH CHOUDHARY: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any policy/scheme for the character \ndevelopment of the young students in the country and if so, the details \nthereof;  \n(b) whether the Government has any plan s to devise campaigns targeted \ntowards increasing leadership skills amongst the youth of the country; and  \n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n[SHRI KIREN RIJIJU] \n \n(a)  For young students Government has launched the National Service \nScheme (NSS) in the year 1969 with the overall objective of personality \ndevelopment of the students through community service. It is a Central \nSector Scheme under Government of India, Ministry of Youth Affairs and \nSports. It provides opportunity to the students/youth of 11 th & 12 th Class of \nschools at +2 Board level and student youth of Technical Institutions, \nGraduate &  Post Graduate at colleges and University level of India to take \npart in various government led community service activities & programmes.  \n \n(b) & (c): Yes, Sir. With a view to mobilize, motivate and organize the youth \nand enhance their capacities to devel op democratic institutional mechanism \nin the form of village based Youth Clubs, empower them to become \nproductive and responsible citizens, assume local leadership to act as active \npartner in the process of community development and nation building \nactivities with the spirit of equity, secularism and volunteerism, above \nmentioned programmes and activities are conducted by NYKS. \n***** \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |551", "t": "Integrated Rural Energy Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-551 \nANSWERED ON-04.02.2021 \n \nINTEGRATED RURAL ENERGY PROGRAMME \n \n+551. SHRI GOPAL CHINNAYA SHETTY \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the manner in which the Integrated Rural Energy Programme is being implemented across the \ncountry along with the salient features of the said programme;  \n(b) whether any survey has been conducted regarding the progress of implementation of the said \nprogramme in the said states;  \n(c) if so, the details thereof;  \n(d) whether the said programme is achieved the expected outcomes in the states;  \n(e) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(f) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (I/C) FOR NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY, POWER and MoS \nfor  SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n(a) to (f) Ministry of New and Renewable Energy is no  longer implementing Integrated Rural \nEnergy Programme in the country. The programme was implemented as a Centrally Sponsored \nscheme up to 31-03-2005. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |552", "t": "ARHC Beneficiaries", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 552 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nARHC BENEFICIARIES \n \nNO. 552. SHRI VISHNU DAYAL RAM: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)  the total number of anticipated beneficiaries under  the \nAffordable Rental Housing Scheme (ARHC) in Jharkhand;  \n(b)  the criteria used to determine the value of the ren tal \nagreement; and  \n(c)  whether any special agreement or compensation will be given \nto migrant labourers and if so, the details thereof  along with \nthe criteria fixed to determine the quantum of compensation? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a)       Scheme for Affordable Rental Housing Complexes  (ARHCs), a \nsub-scheme under Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana - Urban  (PMAY-U), for \nproviding accommodation at affordable rent to urban migrants/ poor has \nbeen launched on 31.07.2020. Government of Jharkhan d does not have \nany existing Government funded vacant housing compl exes available, \ntherefore no ARHCs are anticipated under Model-1 in the State.  \n     Under Model-2, Expression of Interest (EoI) ha s been issued to \ninvite applications from Public/Private Entities to  construct, operate \nand maintain ARHCs on their own available vacant la nd. The number of \nbeneficiaries will be finalised by the Private/Publ ic Entities as per the \nOperational Guidelines of the scheme, only after co nstruction of ARHCs \non their own available vacant land.   \nCont’d….p.2/ \n\n-2- \n \n(b)      Initial affordable rent of ARHCs will be fixed by Entity as per \nlocal survey. Subsequently, Entity can increase ren t biennially at 8% \nsubject to maximum increase of 20% in aggregate ove r a period of 5 \nyears effective from the date of signing contract. The same mechanism \nshall be followed over the entire concession period i.e. 25 years. \n(c)      As the scheme of ARHCs has been designed specif ically for the \nmigrant labourers from Economically Weaker Section (EWS)/Low \nIncome Group (LIG), no special agreement or compens ation is \nprovisioned in the scheme guidelines.  \n*****  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |553", "t": "Tap Water Connections", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 553 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n  \nTap Water Connections \n553. SHRI ANNASAHEB SHANKAR JOLLE:  \nSHRI SRIDHAR KOTAGIRI:  \nSHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY:  \nDR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI:  \nSHRI KURUVA GORANTLA MADHAV:  \nSHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a) the current status of implementation of the Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) across the country \nincluding Karnataka; and  \n \n(b) the total number of the households that have be en provided tap water connections \nunder JJM during the last two years, State-wise inc luding Andhra Pradesh along with the \nfunds allocated and utilized for the same? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA) \n(a) & (b)  At the time of announcement of Jal Jeeva n Mission – Har Ghar Jal on 15 th  \nAugust, 2019, out of 18.93 Crore rural households, 3.23 Crore (17%) were reported to \nhave tap water connections. Since then, 3.33 Crore rural households have been provided \ntap water connections. Thus, as on date, out of 19. 18 Crore rural households across the \ncountry, 6.56 Crore (34.20%) have tap water supply. \nAs on date, in Karnataka, out of 89.61 lakh rural h ouseholds, 26.11 lakh (28.63%) are \nreported to have tap water connections.State/ UT-wi se details of households with tap \nwater connections, including in Andhra Pradesh, is in public domain and available on the \nJJM dashboard @ https://ejalshakti.gov.in/jjmreport/JJMIndia.aspx. \nThe State/ UT-wise details of Central fund allocate d, released and utilized by States/ UTs \nin 2019-20 and 2020-21, is annexed . The amount released also include fund provided \nunder National Drinking Water Quality Sub-Mission f or providing potable water in \nArsenic and Fluoride affected habitations, performa nce incentive grants and funds for \nJapanese Encephalitis –Acute Encephalitis Syndrome affected districts.  \n*** \n  \n\nAnnex referred in the reply to Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 553 due for reply on 04.02.2021 \n \nState/ UT-wise details of central fund allocated, released and utilized by States/ UTs  \nin 2019-20 and 2020-21, under Jal Jeevan Mission \n(Amount in Rs. Crore) \nS. \nNo. \nState/ UT 2019-20 2020-21 (as on 02.02.2021) \nAllocation Released Utilization  Allocation  Released  Utilization  \n1. A & N Islands  1.78  0.5  NR  2.93  0.66  NR  \n2. Andhra Pradesh  372.64  372.64  121.62  790.48  162.59  250.44  \n3. Arunachal Pradesh 132.55  177.47  126.14  254.85  254.85  199.71  \n4. Assam  694.95  442.36  358.87  1,608.51  523.62  398.57  \n5. Bihar  787.31  417.35  473.33  1,839.16  353.6  441.12  \n6. Chhattisgarh 208.04  65.82  39.23  445.52  222.76  142.45  \n7. Goa 7.57  3.08  3.08  12.41  3.1  NR  \n8. Gujarat  390.31  390.31  384.61  883.08  662.31  387.66  \n9. Haryana 149.95  149.95  69.29  289.52  72.38  89.74  \n10. Himachal Pradesh 148.67  205.83  197.87  326.2  326.2  319.69  \n11 . Jammu & Kashmir  322.03  322.03  200.25  681.77  53.72  6.93  \n12. Jharkhand 267.69  291.19  114.58  572.24  143.06  207.26  \n13. Karnataka 546.06  546.06  492.24  1,189.40  296.29  208.9  \n14 . Kerala  248.76  101.29  62.69  404.24  101.06  86.65  \n15 . Ladakh  166.65  67.86  NR  352.09  ND  0.78  \n16. Madhya Pradesh 571.6  571.6  326.65  1,280.13  640.06  535.07  \n17 . Maharashtra  847.97  345.28  308.04  1,828.92  457.23  351.73  \n18 . Manipur  67.69  91.17  28.2  131.8  98.85  122.74  \n19. Meghalaya 86.02  43.01  26.35  174.92  87.46  67.33  \n20 . Mizoram  39.87  68.05  37.41  79.3  79.3  74.98  \n21 . Nagaland  56.49  56.49  23.54  114.09  57.05  61.47  \n22. Odisha 364.74  364.74  275.02  812.15  609.11  343.58  \n23. Puducherry 2.5  ND  0.97  4.64  1.06  0.2  \n24 . Punjab  227.46  227.46  73.27  362.79  ND  99.35  \n25. Rajasthan 1,301.71  1,301.71  620.31  2,522.03  630.51  529.77  \n26. Sikkim 15.41  26.15  14.71  31.36  15.68  18.07  \n27 . Tamil Nadu  373.87  373.1  114.58  921.99  458.72  431.25  \n28. Telangana 259.14  105.52  88.33  412.19  82.71  57.88  \n29. Tripura 107.64  145.37  59.45  156.61  39.15  96.99  \n30 . Uttar Pradesh  1,206.28  1,513.14  639.32  2,570.94  682.13  1,073.61  \n31 . Uttarakhand  170.53  170.53  110.04  362.58  90.64  84.32  \n32. West Bengal 995.33  994.75  609  1,614.18  778.52  1,040.79  \n  Source: IMIS, DDWS   ND: Not Drawn    NR: Not Rep orted "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |554", "t": "Allocation of Funds for Chhattisgarh", "head": "O.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOKSABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 554 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nALLOCATION OF FUNDS FOR CHHATTISGARH \n \nNO. 554.   SHRI VIJAY BAGHEL: \n   \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of various works as well as scheme s along with the \namount of funds allocated by the Union Government d uring the last \ntwo year 2018-2020 to Chhattisgarh;and \n \n(b) whether the Union Government has received any p roposal sent by \nthe Government of Chhattisgarh during the said period  and if so, the \ndetails thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n (SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a)  The Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs(MoHUA) fa cilitates and \nassists States/Union Territories (UTs) through its flagship \nMissions/Schemes  - Atal Mission for Rejuvenation a nd Urban \nTransformation (AMRUT), Smart Cities Mission (SCM),  Pradhan Mantri \nAwas Yojana (Urban)(PMAY-U), Swachh Bharat Mission- Urban(SBM-U), \nDeendayal Antyodaya Yojana-National Urban Livelihoo ds Mission (DAY-\nNULM) in urban development. The Mission/Schemes are  implemented by \nthe States/UTs in accordance with the Mission guide lines. MoHUA \nextends financial assistance for the schemes/projects as per guidelines. \nDetails of Mission/schemes, works and amount releas ed to Chhattisgarh \nare given in the Annex I and Annex II respectively. \n \n(b)  Proposal for sanction of 1, 35,192 houses under PMA Y-U was \nreceived during the period from the Government of C hhattisgarh which \nwas approved. \n \n \n***** \n \n\nAnnex I \nAnnexI referred to in reply to Part (a) of Lok Sabh a Unstarred Question No \n554 regarding ‘Allocation of funds for Chhattisgarh’ \n \n \n(i)  Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT) \n \n \nAtal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformat ion (AMRUT) was \nlaunched on 25 June, 2015 in 500 mission cities wit h focus on water \nsupply, sewerage & septage management, storm water drainage, green \nspaces & parks and non-motorised urban transport.Un der the Mission \nindividual projects are selected, appraised approve d and implemented by \nthe concerned States/Union Territories (UTs) themselves. The Government \nof India only approves State Annual Action Plans (S AAPs) and releases \nCentral Assistance (CA) as per Mission guidelines. For Chhattisgarh SAAP \nenvisaging investment of ₹2,192 crore with committe d CA of Rs. 1,009.74 \ncrore for its 09 Mission cities has been approved. Against this, contracts \nfor 292 projects costing Rs.2,299.81 crore have bee n awarded including  \n208 projects worth Rs.502.84 crore which have been completed.  \n(ii) Smart Cities Mission  \n \nThe Government of India launched Smart Cities Missi on (SCM) on 25 June \n2015 for developing 100 cities as Smart Cities. The  objective of the \nMission is to promote cities that provide core infr astructure and give a \ndecent quality of life to its citizens, a clean and  sustainable environment \nand application of ‘Smart Solutions’. A total numbe r of 100 cities have \nbeen selected to be developed as Smart Cities under  the Smart Cities \nMission.The implementation of the Smart Cities Mission at the city level is \ndone by a Special Purpose Vehicle (SPV) created for  the purpose. The \nSPVs plan, appraise, approve, implement, manage, op erate, monitor and \nevaluate their Smart City projects. \n \n Raipur, Bilaspur and Atal Nagar are the three citi es selected under \nthe Smart Cities Mission in the State of Chhattisga rh. The current status \nof projects being carried out by Smart Cities in Ch hattisgarh is given in \ntable below. \n \n \n \n \n \n\n(Amount in Rs. crore) \nCity Tender Issued Work Order Issued  Work Completed Total \nProje \ncts \nAmount  \nNo. of \nProjects  \nAmount  No. of \nProjects  \nAmount  No. of \nProjects  \nAmount  \nAtal \nNagar 3\n 43.2  2 15.9  1 158.0  6 217.2  \nBilaspur  19  259.2  22  1,133.1  14  109.7  55  1,502.0  \nRaipur 33  757.2  53  382.4  75  469.3  161  1,608.9  \nTotal 55  1,059.6  77  1,531.4  90  737.0  222  3,328.1  \n \n(iii) Swachh Bharat Mission (SBM-U)  \n \nSwachh Bharat Mission-Urban was launched on 02.10.2 014 with the \nobjective of improving the sanitation in the Urban Local Bodies (ULBs) by \nachieving 100 per cent Open Defecation Free (ODF) s tatus and by 100 \npercent scientific processing of Municipal Solid Wa ste (MSW) generated in \nULBs. The details of works executed in Chhattisgarh from the funds \nreleased to the State during the last two years 201 8-2020 is not \nmaintained in the Ministry since the work is execut ed by the concerned \nState Government/Union Territory (UT) in various Ur ban Local Bodies \n(ULBs) after release of funds to the State Government.  \n(ii)  Pradhan Mantri AwasYojana-Urban(PMAY-U)  \n \nPMAY(U) extends Central Assistance to States/Union Territories(UTs) \nincluding State of Chhattisgarh for providing all weather pucca houses to \nall eligib"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |555", "t": "Nai Roshni Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 555 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \n“NAI ROSHNI SCHEME” \n \n555. SHRI MALOOK NAGAR  \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n \n (a) the number of women belonging to minority communities benefitted under “Nai Roshni”  \nscheme  of the Government,  State/UT-wise; and   \n(b) the number of active Government organisations providing training under the said scheme, \nState/UT- wise? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n (SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a)   “Nai Roshni”, a scheme for Leadership Development of Minority Women \nis being implemented across India with an aim to empower and instill confidence in \nwomen by providing knowledge, tools and techniques for  interacting with \nGovernment systems, banks and other institutions  at all levels. This includes \nempowerment of the trainee women so that they become independent and confident \nmembers of the society. The overall objective of the scheme is to embolden the \nminority women to move out of the confines of their homes and community and \nassume leadership roles in society. The scheme provides for six days training \nprogramme followed by handholding for a period of one year. The training is provided \non various pre -designed Training modules covering issues relating to women viz. \nLeadership of Women through participation in decision making, Educational \nProgrammes for women, Health and Hygiene, Legal rights of women, Financial \nLiteracy, Digital Literacy, Swachh Bharat, Lif e Skills, and Advocacy for Social and \nBehaviourial change.  The number of minority women benefitted State wise /UT wise \nunder the scheme is given in Annexure. \n \n \n(b) The Scheme is being implemented through Non-Governmental Organisations \nempanelled under the  Nai Roshni Scheme as per scheme guidelines in force.  \n \nWhile Nai Roshni scheme targets women beneficiaries specifically, other schemes of \nthe Ministry also lay lot of emphasis on minority women. In Seekho Aur Kamao \n(Learn & Earn) Scheme, 33% of the t otal beneficiaries are women. Similarly in Nai \nManzil scheme , 30% of the total beneficiaries are women. These schemes help in \neconomic empowerment of the Minority women. \n \n*****    \n\n \nState wise no of trainees trained under the \nScheme – Leadership Development of \nMinority Women (Nai Roshni)  \nSr. No States \nTotal \ntrainees \ncovered \n1 Andhra Pradesh 6500 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 625 \n3 Assam 22175 \n4 Bihar 9775 \n5 Chhattisgarh 2700 \n6 Delhi 4800 \n7 Gujarat 3900 \n8 Haryana 1850 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 500 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 2525 \n11 Jharkhand 5375 \n12 Karnataka 9700 \n13 Kerala 5350 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 41775 \n15 Maharashtra 6975 \n16 Manipur 6975 \n17 Meghalaya 125 \n18 Nagaland 1700 \n19 Odisha 6250 \n20 Punjab 3275 \n21 Rajasthan 17500 \n22 Sikkim 400 \n23 Telangana 1025 \n24 Tripura 850 \n25 Tamil Nadu 5200 \n26 Uttar Pradesh 215275 \n27 Uttrakhand 10640 \n28 West Bengal 8475 \n  Total 402215 \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |556", "t": "Release of Funds for MSDP", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.556  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \nRelease of Funds for MSDP \n \n †556. SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV:  \n \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the release of Central fund due of the second installment of Anganwadi Centres \nunder MultiSectoral Development Programme (MSDP) is pending with various States for \nthe year 2019-20 especially with Buldhana district of Maharashtra; \n(b) if so, the det ails thereof for the said period along with the reasons therefor, State/district -\nwise;  \n(c) whether the Union Government has received requests from public representatives to \nrelease Central amount due of second installment under the said programme especial ly for \nthe Buldhana district of Maharashtra; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the action taken by the Union Government in this regard? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a) to (d):  The Ministry is implementing Pradhan Mantri Jan Vikas Karyakram (PMJVK) \nin 1300 identified Minority Concentration Areas (MCAs) of the country with the objective \nof developing socio -economic assets and basic amenities for improving quality of life of \nthe people in these areas.   Priority Sectors under the scheme are education, health, skill \ndevelopment and women centric projects.  Being a Centrally Sponsored Scheme, projects \nare funded on sharing b asis with the States/UTs.  The c entral share of funds are released \ngenerally in two instalments.   The first instalment is released along with the sanction of the \nproject.  Second and subsequent instalments are released after submission of utilization \ncertificate for the previous release , submission of quarterly progress reports, submission of \nphotographs of the asset  under construction and status of release & utilization of \ncorresponding State share etc.   \nThe Ministry on 5.11.2020 received a reference dated 27.10.2020 from Hon’ble Member \nfor release of second installment for  Anganwadi Centres in Buldhana district  of \nMaharashtra.  Second installment for the projects in Buldana including Anganwadi Centres \nhave been released to the State Government  of Maharashtra vide sanction l etter dated \n29.10.2020 & 06.11.2020.  \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |557", "t": "Work on Bypass on NH-57", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 557 \nANSWERED ON 04TH FEBRUARY, 2021 \n \nWORK ON BYPASS ON NH-57 \n \n557.  SHRI PINAKI MISRA: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a)  the details and the present status of work of the proposed \nbypass on National Highway (NH)-57 in Nayagarh, Odisha; \n(b) whether the Government has received a request from the \nNational Highways Authority of India to engage additional \nmanpower to expedite the land acquisition process for the \nproject; \n(c) if so, the steps taken by the Government thereon; and \n(d) the time by which the project is expected to be completed? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a)      The work of DPR preparation for the construction of Nayagarh \nBypass on NH-57 in the State of Odisha was taken up.  Alignment of \nthe bypass was approved and the DPR is in progress.  The land \nacquisition process has commenced.  \n(b) to (d)    A request for engagement of additional manpower to \nexpedite the land acquisition process for the p roject was received \nvide CE-NH letter dated 05/01/2021.  Instructions for meeting s uch \nrequirements from project contingencies on need based approach  \nhave already been issued .  The project would be taken up o n \ncompletion of the DPR and acquisition  of land in more than 90 \npercent of the length as per the extant guidelines of the Ministry. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |558", "t": "Achievements under DDUGJY", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.558 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \nACHIEVEMENTS UNDER DDUGJY \n \n558. SHRI DIBYENDU ADHIKARI: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of the achievements made u nder Deen dayal Upadhyaya Gram Jyoti \nYojana (DDUGJY)  along with the village infrastructure created and the number of \nvillages electrified thereunder in the country; \n \n(b)  whether any deficiencies in creation of infrastructure and the implementation of \nthe DDUGJY has come to the notice of the Union Government; and \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER, NEW & RENEWABLE \nENERGY AND THE MINISTER OF STATE FOR SKILL DEVELOPMENT & \nENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n(SHRI  R.K. SINGH)  \n \n(a) :  Government of India had launched Deendayal U padhyaya Gram Jyoti \nYojana (DDUGJY) in December, 2014 for rural electrification works across the country. \nAs reported by the States, all the inhabited un -electrified census villages stand \nelectrified on 28 th April, 2018 across the country under DDUGJY. A t otal of 18,374 \nvillages were electrified under the scheme. Details of infrastructure created under \n‘DDUGJY new projects’ category  (upto December, 2020) are as under: \n \nSl No item No/Ckm \n1 Sub-stations \n(including augmentation) \n3805 no \n2 Distribution Transformers 3,68,991  no \n3 Feeder Segregation 1,23,975 Ckm \n4 11 KV lines 1,14,877  Ckm \n5 LT lines 2,79,386 Ckm \n6 33 KV & 66 KV lines 21,401 Ckm \n7 Consumer Energy Meters 1,50,27,186 no \n8 Distribution Transformers Meters 2,12,153 no \n9 11kV feeder meters 13,246 no \n \n………2.  \n\n \n \n- 2 - \n \n(b) & (c) : Government of India ha ve appointed REC as the nodal agency for the \nscheme. As per the scheme guidelines, the nodal agency shall monitor physical and \nfinancial progress of the projects including quality of works throu gh a three tier \nmechanism of (i) quality control of the turnkey contractor, (ii) quality control of the PIA \nthrough Project Management Agency (PMA) and (iii) quality control exercised by the \nnodal agency i.e. REC. \n \nTill 31.12.2020, under DDUGJY 3,56,847 de fects have been reported by \nREC quality monitors to PIAs/DISCOMs out of which 2,84,595 defects  have been rectified \nby the PIA/DISCOMs. The State-wise details are at Annexure. \n \nIn order to conduct inspections and rectification in an efficient manner, an \nonline quality portal “Sakshya” has been devised by the nodal agency for uploading of \nobservations by quality monitoring agencies and submission of compliances by \nDISCOMs/PIAs.  \n \n \n********** \n \n  \n\n \n \nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS ( b) & (c) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  \n558 TO BE ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 04.02.2021. \n*************** \n \nState-wise details of defects observed and rectified under DDUGJY New  \nProjects   (status as on 31.12.2020)                                            \n   Sl. No. State/UT Name Defects Observed Defects rectified \n1 Andhra Pradesh 19481 19336 \n2 Arunachala Pradesh 7556 5578 \n3 Assam 2816 396 \n4 Bihar 3958 2401 \n5 Chhattisgarh 10043 9858 \n6 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 21 0 \n7 Gujarat 26414 26414 \n8 Haryana 2369 1588 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 3042 715 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 2433 537 \n11 Jharkhand 21453 8363 \n12 Karnataka 19897 16225 \n13 Kerala 1780 1780 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 57501 46366 \n15 Maharashtra 36524 36426 \n16 Meghalaya 5845 5836 \n17 Mizoram 448 108 \n18 Nagaland 1026 0 \n19 Odisha 8598 4720 \n20 Puducherry 23 0 \n21 Punjab 338 72 \n22 Rajasthan 81919 63615 \n23 Tamil Nadu 1793 1618 \n24 Telangana 9196 7432 \n25 Tripura 269 240 \n26 Uttar Pradesh 20832 18204 \n27 Uttarakhand 5422 3428 \n28 West Bengal 5850 3339 \nTotal 356847 284595 \n \n \n************ "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |559", "t": "Allocation of Funds under SFURTI", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.559\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021\n \n \nALLOCATION OF FUNDS UNDER SFURTI\n \n \n559.\n \nSHRI HEMANT TUKARAM GODSE:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND\n \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to \nstate:\n \n(\na\n)\n \nthe details about the allocation of funds under the Scheme of Fund for Regeneration \nof Traditional Industries (SFURTI) for Maharashtra during the current financial year for \nsetting up Common Facility Centres;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \nthe ac\ntion taken by the Government to facilitate cluster based development to make \nthe traditional industries more productive, profitable as envisaged under this scheme;\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nwhether the Government has also fixed target note through this scheme; and\n \n(\nd\n)\n \nif so, the detail\ns thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NITIN GADKARI)\n \n \n(a):\n \n \n \nUnder the Scheme of Fund for Regeneration of Traditional Industries (SFURTI), \nState\n-\nwise allocation of funds is not made. However, so far 20 proposals to set up SFURTI \nclusters have been approved in the State of Maharashtra with GoI assistance of about \nRs.\n39.26 crore to benefit about 10200 artisans.  9 of these clusters are functional.\n \n \n \n(b)\n:\n \nUnder the SFURTI scheme,\n \ntraditional artisans and industries are organized into \nclusters to add value to their production and enhance their income by supporting them \nin:\n \n \n(i)  \n \nUp\n-\ngradation of infrastructure by developing Common Facility Centers (CFCs) \nwith latest machineries and creation of Raw Material bank,\n \n(ii)\n \n  \nTraining\n \nand skill development of\n \nartisans, exposure visits, product development,\n \n(iii) \n \nDeveloping new\n \nmarket linkages, including e\n-\ncommerce tie ups,\n \n(iv) \n \nPartnerships with institutes of eminence for creation of new designs, etc.\n \n \n(c)\n \n& (d)\n:\n \nBetween 2014\n-\n17 it was proposed to set up 71 SFURTI clusters and during \n2018\n-\n20, it\n \nwas proposed to set up 100 SFUR\nTI clusters. \n   \nAs against this, 335 clusters \nhave been approved, of which 82 clusters are operational.\n \n \nRs.785 cr.\n \nis\n \ncommitted for \nthese 335 clusters which will benefit about 1.99 lakh artisans.\n \n \n***\n*\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |56", "t": "Barren Land", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 56\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 04.02.2021\n \n \nLOANS TO MSMEs\n \n \n*\n56\n.\n               \nSHRI KOMATI REDDY VENKAT REDDY:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether there has been some slowdown in loans sanctioned to struggling  businesses and \nMicro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) in the pre and post Covid\n-\n19 period;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \nif so,  t\nhe comparative details thereof and the reasons therefor;\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nthe corrective steps being taken/results yielded, loan\n-\nwise, during  the last three years and the \ncurrent year;\n \n(\nd\n)\n \nthe relief measures under the loan moratorium scheme extended to MSMEs in the country; \nand \n \n(\ne\n)\n \nthe details of demands raised by MSMEs and met by Government so far, State\n-\nwise?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NITIN GADKARI)\n \n \n(\na) to (e): \n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (e) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. *56 FOR ANSWER ON 04.02.2021.\n \n \n(a) & (b)\n:\n \nThe statement of Sector wise deployment of Gross Bank Credit released by Reserve \nBank of India indicates that as on 18\nth\n \nDecember, 2020, there is marginal decline of 1.5 percent in the \ncredit extended to Micro and Small Sector as compared to 27\nth\n \nMarch, 2020.  The details of credit \nextended to this sector since December 2018 is given in the table below:\n \n \nCredit to Micro & S\nmall Sector (Manufacturing & Service Sector)  \n \n(Rs.crore)\n \n \n \nVariation (Year\n-\non\n-\nYear) in \n%\n \nVariation (Financial Year) \nin %\n \nDec.21, \n2018\n \nMar.29, \n2019\n \nDec.20, \n2019\n \nMar.27, \n2020\n \nDec.18, \n2020\n \nDec.20, 2019 / \nDec.21, 2018\n \nDec.18, \n2020 / \nDec.20, \n2019\n \nDec.20, \n2019 /  \nMar.29, \n2019\n \nDec.18, 2020 \n/  Mar.27, \n2020\n \n1004744\n \n1067175\n \n1061953\n \n1149394\n \n1131858\n \n5.7\n \n6.6\n \n-\n0.5\n \n-\n1.5\n \nSource\n-\n \nRBI\n \n \n(c)\n:\n \n \nThe steps taken by the Government to increase availability of credit to the MSME sector \ninter\n-\nalia include Rs 20,000 crore Subordinate Debt for MSMEs under Credit Guarantee Scheme for \nSubordinate Debt (CGSSD), Rs. 3 lakh crores collateral free automatic lo\nans for business, including \nMSMEs under Emergency Credit Line Guarantee Scheme (ECLGS),  Rs. 30,000 crore Special \nLiquidity Scheme for NBFC/HFC/MFI,  Pradhan Mantri SvaNIDHI Scheme for collateral free loans \nto Street Vendors etc.  As on date 34 Banks \nare\n \nr\negistered under CGSSD. As informed by \nDepartment of Financial Services, as on 25.01.2021, guarantees to the tune of around Rs 2,01,364 \ncrore has been issued under ECLGS.\n \n \nIn addition to this, Ministry of MSME also provides \nguarantees in respect of credit f\nacilities \nextended by Lending Institutions to the borrowers in Micro and Small Enterprises (MSEs) sector.  \nThe \nperformance of the scheme during last three years and the current financial year is given in the \ntable below:\n \n \nFinancial Year\n \nNo. of Guarantee \n \nA\nmount of Guarantee\n \n(Rs. in Cr.)\n \n2017\n-\n18\n \n263195\n \n19065.90\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n435520\n \n30168.57\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n846650\n \n45851.22\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n(upto on 31.12.2020)\n \n542802\n \n25512.71\n \nSource\n-\n \nCGTMSE\n \n \n(d)\n:\n \n  \nReserve Bank of India (RBI), vide circulars dated 27.3.2020 and 23.5.2020 on COVID\n-\n19 \nRegulatory Package, permitted all lending institutions to grant a moratorium of six months on \npayment of all instalments (including equated monthly instalments, or EMIs) \nfalling due between \n1.3.2020 and 31.8.2020, in respect of all term loans outstanding as on 1.3.2020.\n \n \n(\ne)\n:\n \nMinistry of MSME receives representations from MSME Associations on issues such as \navailability of credit, capital subsidy etc. Such representations \nare considered on the basis of merit \nand administrative feasibility. \n \n \n****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |560", "t": "Online Booking of Government Accomodations", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 560 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nONLINE BOOKING OF GOVERNMENT ACCOMMODATION \n \nNO. 560.  SHRIMATI SUNITA DUGGAL: \nWill the MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased \nto state- \na. Whether the Government official or department ha s an \noption to book their accommodation online; \n  \nb.  if so, the details thereof including the steps taken by the \nGovernment to boost transparency and accountability  in \nproviding various estate services; \n  \nc.   whether the Government has any mobile applicat ion service \nto avail the various estate services and Government  \nofficers and Government departments;and \n  \nd.   if so, the details thereof along with the serv ices provided   \nthrough mobile application? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n (SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \na.  Yes. Government officials/departments have option t o \napply online for government accommodation one \nSampada portal i.e ''www.esampada.mohua.gov.in\" \nthrough automated system of allotment. \n \nContd…/2 \n\n-2- \n \nb. All services, including allotment of Government \naccommodation are available online on eSampada web portal i.e \n''www.esampada.mohua.gov.in\". All services can be a ccessed \nacross the country by all users through their respective login IDs. \nTo ensure transparency, all the services including allotment are \nautomated and no manual intervention is made. Offic ials in \nEstate Office are given login IDs for their roles i n the automated \nsystem to ensure accountability.  \nc & d. Yes. At present there are two mobile applica tion \nservices for Estates Services. “m-Awas” mobile App for \nautomated system of allotment and “m-Ashoka5” App i s available \nfor allotment of 5 Ashoka Road Bungalow for marriag e / social \npurposes. \n……  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |561", "t": "Negative Growth in MSME Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 561\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021\n \n \nNEGATIVE GROWTH IN MSME SECTOR\n \n \n561.\n \nSHRI P. RAVEENDRANATH:\n \n               \n \nWill\n \n \nthe \n \nMinister\n \n \nof \n \nMICRO,\n \n \nSMALL\n \n \nAND \n \nMEDIUM\n \n \nENTERPRISES\n \n \nbe\n \npleased\n \nto \nstate:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \n \nwhether the functions of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) sector in our \ncountry, including my State of Tamil Nadu has posted negative growth, followed by outbreak of \nCovid\n-\n19 pandemic and if so, the details thereof;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \n \nwhether those MSMEs can be \npermitted to place their own agricultural dry lands as \ncollateral security, enabling access to credit from commercial banks for their additional working \ncapital requirement and if so, the details thereof; and\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nthe steps taken or being taken to promote inclu\nsive growth in MSME sector at rural and hill \nareas, across State of Tamil Nadu including theni Parliamentary Constituency?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(\nSHRI \n \nNITIN GADKARI\n)\n \n \n(a) :\n \nCOVID\n-\n19\n \npandemic\n \nhas temporarily affected various sectors including Micro, Small and \nMedium Enterprises in the country. \nAs per the advance estimates received from Central Statistics \nOffice, Ministry of Statistics & PI, All India Gross Value Added growth of \nmanufacturing sector \n(including MSMEs) at constant prices for FY 2020\n-\n21 over FY 2019\n-\n20 is estimated at (\n-\n9.4) \npercent.\n \n \nHowever, as regard to the State of Tamilnadu, the positive growth rate has been recorded in \nrespect of sanction of projects under Prim\ne Minister’s Employment Generation \nProgramme(PMEGP) during the period of COVID\n-\n19 pandemic (April\n-\nSeptember 2020) as \ncompared to the same period last year (April\n-\nSeptember 2019). Progress of PMEGP Scheme in \nTamil Nadu State is as follows:\n \n \nYear\n \nNo. of Proj\nects assisted under \nPMEGP  in \n \nTamil Nadu\n \nEmployment opportunities \ngenerated under PMEGP in \n \nTamil Nadu\n \nFrom 01.04.2019 \n \nto \n \n30.09.2019\n \n1497\n \n11926\n \nFrom 01.04.2020 \n \nto \n \n30.09.2020\n \n1620\n \n12960\n \n \nAs per Udyog Aadhaar\n \nPortal, number of MSMEs registered (since September 2015 to \n30.06.2020) in All India and Tamilnadu was 102,32,468 and 11,06,628 respectively. \n \n \nAs per Udyam Registration Portal, number of MSMEs registered (from 01.07.2020 to \n01.02.2021) in All India and \nTamilnadu was 20,29,095 and 2,22,507 respectively .\n \n \n\n \n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \n \n(b) :\n \nAs part of Atmanirbhar Bharat Abhiyaan, the ECLGS was launched on 23rd May 2020. The \nscheme envisages fully guaranteed collateral free additional credit to eligible MSME units and \nbusine\nss enterprises either in the form of additional working capital term loans (in case of banks \nand FIs), or additional term loans (in case of NBFCs) up to 20% of their entire outstanding credit as \non 29.2.2020. All entities with outstanding credit of up to R\ns. 50 crore as on 29.2.2020 and annual \nturnover of up to Rs. 250 crore, which were less than or equal to 60 days past due as on 29.2.2020 \nare eligible under the Scheme. The Scheme seeks to help the eligible entities tide over the crisis \ncaused by the Covid\n-\n19 pandemic and meet their operational liabilities.\n \n \n(c):\n \nThe Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) implement various other \nschemes for the growth and development of\n \nMSME sector in the country including state of \nTamilnadu and district Th\neni\n. The total number of MSMEs registered on Udyam portal (from 1\nst\n \nJuly, 2020 to 1\nst\n \nFebruary, 2021) in Theni district was 2217. The brief achievements under PMEGP \nScheme in Tamil Nadu State are as follows:\n \n \nYear\n \nNo. of Projects assisted under \nPMEGP  in \n \nTamil Nadu\n \nEmployment opportunities \ngenerated under PMEGP in \n \nTamil Nadu\n \nFrom 01.04.2019 \n \nto \n \n30.09.2019\n \n1497\n \n11926\n \nFrom 01.04.2020 \n \nto \n \n30.09.2020\n \n1620\n \n12960\n \n \n \n \n***\n**\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |562", "t": "Slow Pace of Rooftop Solar Power Project", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-562 \nANSWERED ON-04.02.2021 \n \nSLOW PACE OF ROOFTOP SOLAR POWER PROJECT \n \n562. SHRI MANNE SRINIVAS REDDY \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether there is a slow pace of rooftop solar power project and if so, the details thereof \nand the reasons therefor along with the progress made thereunder; \n(b) the details of funds sanctioned/response received so far during the last three years and the \ncurrent year, State-wise; and \n(c) the steps being taken for better Power Purchase Agreements (PPAs) with Discoms along \nwith the steps being taken to address the issues in metering? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (I/C) FOR NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY, POWER and \nMoS for  SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n(a) During implementation of Phase -I of Rooftop Solar (RTS) programme in 2015 -2020 the \nachievement reported on MNRE SPIN po rtal till 31.1.2021 is 2071 MW against the target of \n4200 MW capacity. The factors affecting progress included lack of uniform regulations, \ninvolvement of multiple agencies, lack of awareness among the prospective beneficiaries , \napprehension of possible revenue loss by DISCOMs, etc.  \nThese issues have been addressed in Ph -II of RTS Programme, which included making \nDISCOMs nodal agencies for implementation, separate funding for awareness and capacity \nbuilding, incentivising DISCOMs for addition of RTS capaci ty above a certain percentage of \nbase capacity, etc.  \nOut of 4000 MW capacity targeted through central financial support in the residential sector \nunder Ph -II of RTS Programme, over 2600 MW capacity has been sanctioned to various \nDISCOMs. \nTill 31.12.2020, states have reported cumulative installation of 3.73 GW of grid connected \nrooftop solar capacity.  \n(b) Funds sanctioned for release under RTS Pogramme during the last three years and the \ncurrent year are given follows: \nFY 2017-18:Rs. 167.93 Crore \nFY 2018-19: Rs. 446.78 Crore \nFY 2019-20: Rs.  290.66 Crore \nFY 2020-21 ( as on 31.12.2020): Rs. 297.21 Crore \nState-wise breakup is given in Annexure. \n(c) The Ministry of New and renewable Energy has specified different business models like \nrent a roof, utility model, RESCO, etc., for implementation of RTS programme. In addition, \nthe Ministry has issued suggestive model o perating procedures and timelines for different \nactivities by DISCOMs. The model operating procedures includes timelines for installation of  \nmeters. \n \n***** \n\n \nANNEXURE \nAnnexure referred to part (b) of Lok sabha unstarred question no. 562 dated 04.02.2021 \non Slow Pace of Rooftop Solar Power Project by Shri MANNE SRINIVAS REDDY \nSl. No. State/UTs \nFunds Released (Rs. in Crore) \nFY-2017-18 FY- 2018-19 FY-2019-20 FY-2020-21 \n1.  Andhra Pradesh 4.82 44.95 2.07 27.81 \n2.  Assam 0.00 0.00 17.33  \n3.  Andaman & Nicobar 7.00 0.00   \n4.  Bihar   1.01 0.89 \n5.  Chhattisgarh 3.30 0.00 2.67  \n6.  Delhi 21.40 0.00 0.60 21.06 \n7.  Gujarat 23.18 169.19 77.53 0.78 \n8.  Haryana 0.00 11.74 13.07 4.12 \n9.  J& K 0.00  6.68 4.89 \n10.  Jharkhand 0.00  2.30  \n11.  Kerala 9.41    \n12.  Madhya Pradesh 3.14   4.45 \n13.  Maharashtra 0.00 77.13  55.06 \n14.  Odisha 1.61    \n15.  Puduchchery 0.00 0.10 0.79  \n16.  Punjab 0.00 10.71 5.50 6.87 \n17.  Rajasthan 11.68 21.36  11.60 \n18.  Tamil Nadu 0.00   4.41 \n19.  Telangana 2.72 18.25 20.23 20.66 \n20.  Tripura 0.70    \n21.  Uttarakhand 27.09  7.67 4.55 \n22.  Uttar Pradesh 2.32 7.52  14.68 \n23.  West Bengal 6.30 4.08 13.38  \n24.  Chandigarh 1.73 18.76 5.13 0.85 \n25.  Manipur 0.00  4.15 4.03 \n26.  Himachal Pradesh 0.00 9.76   \n27.  Meghalaya   3.47  \n28.  Mizoram 4.54    \n29.  Arunachal Pradesh 14.06    \n30.  PSU/Govt Department 16.75 19.67 21.34 57.52 \n31.  SECI 3.68 33.56 85.77 52.98 \n32.  NISE/TERI  2.51    \n  Total 167.93 446.78 290.66 297.21 \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |563", "t": "Composition of Population of the Country", "head": "O.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 563 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nCOMPOSITION OF POPULATION OF THE COUNTRY \nNO. 563.   DR. MANOJ RAJORIA: \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state : \n(a) the difference between urban and rural populati on of the country \naccording to the census 2001 and 2011; \n(b) the estimated percentage increase in the urban population according \nto the upcoming census 2021; and \n(c) the steps being taken by the Government to prov ide facilities to the \ncities in a planned way? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) As per Census 2001 and 2011, the difference between Rural and \nUrban Population is as under:  \n \n 2001 2011 \nUrban Population 28,61,19,689 37,71,06,125 \nRural Population 74,26,17,747 83,37,48,852 \nDifference between \nRural and Urban \npopulation \n45,64,98,058 45,66,42,727 \n \n(b) Estimates of Urban Population according to cens us 2021 are not yet \navailable. However, as per report of the Technical Group on population \nprojections for India and States, 2011-2036, based on Census 2011, \npublished by the National Commission on Population (NCP), Ministry of  \n\nContd…2/ \n \n \n \n-2- \n \nHealth and Family Welfare, Govt. of India, the projected urban population  \n for India as on 1st March 2021 will be 46,93,44,00 0 out of total \nprojected population of 1,36,30,06,000.  Thus, esti mated urban \npopulation in 2021 will mean an increase of 24.46 p ercent in comparison \nto urban population as per census 2011. \n (c) ‘Land’ and ‘Colonisation’ are State subjects a nd therefore, it is the \nresponsibility of the State/Union Territory (UT) Go vernments to frame \npolicies and implement schemes for providing facili ties to the cities. \nHowever, Government of India through various progra mmatic \ninterventions viz. Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana - Urb an (PMAY-U), \nAffordable Rental Housing Complexes (ARHCs) Scheme,   Atal Mission for \nRejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT), Smar t Cities Mission, \nSwachh Bharat Mission-Urban (SBM-U), Metro Rail Dev elopment Policy, \nDeendayal Antyodaya Yojana National Urban livelihoo d Mission (DAY-\nNULM) and PM Street Vendor’s AtmaNirbhar Nidhi  (PM  SVANidhi) have \nbeen making overarching efforts by assisting States /UTs both \ntechnically and financially to provide facilities v iz. housing shortage, \nurban infrastructure in the sectors of water supply , sewerage and \nseptage management, storm water drainage, green spa ces & parks, non-\nmotorised urban transport, sanitation services, met ro rail development \nand assisting urban migrants/poor to improve skills  and uplift \neconomically.  \n*****    "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |564", "t": "ASHA-India", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 564 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nASHA-INDIA \n \nNO. 564. SHRI SRIDHAR KOTAGIRI: \n  SHRI T.R.V.S. RAMESH: \n  SHRI POCHA BRAHMANANDA REDDY: \n  SHRI KURUVA GORANTLA MADHAV: \nSHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Government has set up five Incubation C enters under \nAffordable Sustainable Housing Accelerators – India  (ASHA-India) \ninitiative under PMAYU, for providing incubation su pport to identify \ninnovative materials, processes and technology for resource \nefficient, disaster resilient and sustainable construction; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether the Government has selected any Post-Protot ype \nTechnologies for Acceleration Support; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a)& (b): Yes, Sir. The five Incubation Centres set  up under Affordable \nSustainable Housing Accelerators – India (ASHA-Indi a) initiative are Indian \nInstitute of Technology (IIT), Roorkee; IIT, Kharagpur; IIT, Madras; IIT, Bombay \nand Council of Scientific & Industrial Research–North East Institute of Science \n& Technology (CSIR–NEIST), Jorhat. \n(c) & (d): Yes, Sir. The Government has selected Po st-Prototype Technologies \nfor Acceleration Support under ASHA-India initiativ e,the details of which are \nat Annexure.  \n*****  \n \n\n \nAnnexure referred in reply to LS USQ No. 564 due for answer on 04.02.2021 \nDetails of Post-Prototype Technologies selected for Acceleration Support under \nASHA-India initiative \n \nS.No. \n Name of Potential \nTechnology Providers \nName of Post-Prototype Technologies \n1. Saltech Design Labs Pvt. \nLtd \nComposite material made from plastic and \nindustrial waste \n2. Green Forms Private \nLimited \nPolymeric thermal insulation \n3. Gohemp Agroventures Pvt. \nLtd. \nBiocrete \n4. Gouda-Torgerson Humengi \nBldg. Sys. Ind. Pvt. Ltd \nHumengi mortarless interlocking blocks \n5. Prashak Techno \nEnterprises Pvt. Ltd. \nHabitech – Nivaratantra (housing and \nsanitation solutions) \n6. Bioman Technologies Pvt. \nLtd. \nBioman \n7. Hexpressionsmegatech \nPrivate Limited \nHexpressions – composite honeycomb \npaper panel \n8. Kaushal Bhaav Skill \nSolutions Pvt. Ltd. \nBuilding solutions based on lime masonry \nand skilling \n9. Taraltec Solutions Pvt. \nLtd. \nTaraltec reactor for treating water \n10. Nebeskie Labs Pvt. Ltd. Electricity management system \n11. Creative Group Sunlite HV LLP – honeycomb villa ge \n12. Caya Constructs Precast RCC technology – C chan nels \n13. Ideal Choice Homes Offsite production of buildi ng components \n– C channels \n14. ENXGE Structural \nConsultants (Krishna \nKumar) \nModular house with tunnel forms \n15. Anand Buildtech Pvt. Ltd. Interlocking blocks s ystems and helical \npiling technology for foundation \n16. Billion bricks Precast housing system with sola r roof \ntop \n17. Apna Ghar Precast load bearing clay hollow bloc ks \nwith precast columns and filler slabs \n18. Spur Dshelters Services \nPvt. Ltd. \nSpur domes and shells/shelters \n\nS.No.  Name of Potential \nTechnology Providers \nName of Post-Prototype Technologies \n19. Rincon Realty Formwork system \n20. Lavina Rodrigues \n(PyraBuildesign) \n3D olle pods \n21. Kumar Mridul (Ghost \nVision) \nAugmented reality for housing \n22. Parkhi Promoters & \ndevelopers Pvt. Ltd. \nFinancing solutions and land aggregation \n23. MailhemIkos Environment \nPvt.Ltd. \nSolid waste management \n24. Ashish Bhuvan Sustainable modular precast affor dable \nhousing system \n25. Credai-MCHI Online procurement portal  \n26. The Solar Labs Solar labs for renewable energy  \n27. WEGoT Utility Solutions \nPrivate Limited \nVenaqua (water efficient system)  \n28. Spartan Engineering \nIndustries Pvt. Ltd. \nFireman evacuation lifts \n29. Abhishek Gupta (Ceantra) Cost effective smart p ower solutions \n30. WhatsLoan Digital financial online platform for  loans \n31. Tapu Sustainable \nSolutions \nEco trap in waterless urinals \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |565", "t": "Implementation of PM 15 Point Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.565 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \nIMPLEMENTATION OF PM 15 POINT PROGRAMME \n565. DR. SUBHAS SARKAR: \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) the details for the welfare of minorities under Prime Minister’s new Fifteen Point \nProgramme which is under implementation in the Country, including the State of West \nBengal; \n(b) the details including the achievements made therein during the last three years, \nState-wise; and \n(c) whether the Government has any report of non -utilization of funds for minorities \nin the Country, if so, the details thereof and the action taken by Government against the \nconcerned Officers, State/UT-wise? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n(a) to (c) : The Prime Minister’s New 15 Point Programme for the Welfare of Minorities \n(PM’s New 15 PP) is under implementation in the country including West Bengal as an \noverarching programme.  The programme covers various schemes / initiatives of the \nGovernment being implemented by the various Ministries/Departments, either \nexclusively for or by earmarking of 15% of overall physical/financial targets for \nminorities, wherever feasible, or by monitoring the flow of funds in the minor ity \nconcentration areas, for the welfare of minorities in the country. \nThe programme aims to promote opportunities for education as well as social welfare by \nensuring equitable share for minorities in economic activities and employment through \n\nexisting and  new schemes, enhanced credit support for self -employment and \nrecruitment to State and Central Government jobs.  As per information received from \nthe participating Ministries/Departments , the details of targets and achievement \navailable, under various sche mes/initiatives of the programme, including funds \nsanctioned, release d and utilized during the last three years in various States/UT s, \nincluding West Bengal are on the website of the Ministry i.e. www.minorityaffairs.gov.in.  \nThe programmes/schemes under Prime Minister’s New 15 Point Programme are \nimplemented by various Ministries/Departments of Government and also through State \nGovernments/UT Administrations.  The schemes are monitored at different levels \nincluding the review done by the Ministry of Minority Affairs as a Nodal Ministry and \nshortfalls wherever noticed are pointed out to the concerned Ministries/ Departments so \nas to take remedial action to ensure that the targets are achieved and available funds \nare utilized opt imally.  The State -wise details of physical/financial targets fixed and \nachievements made under the seven schemes, implemented by the Ministry of Minority \nAffairs and th e details available of target f ixed and achievement s made under other \nschemes covered u nder PM’s New 15 PP during the least three years  are o n the \nwebsite of the Ministry. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |566", "t": "Disputes on projects of Krishna & Godavari River", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 566\nANSWERED ON 04.02.2021\nDISPUTES ON PROJECTS OF KRISHNA & GODAVARI RIVER\n566. SHRI BALASHOWRY VALLABHANENI\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of disputes identified by the Government between Andhra Pradesh and Telangana on\nvarious projects of Krishna and Godavari river;\n(b) the efforts being made by the Governm ent to resolve disputes amicably between Andhra Pradesh and\nTelangana;\n(c) the time by which Apex Council meeting is likely to be held to discuss on various projects of Krishna\nand Godavari river by Telangana and Andhra Pradesh; and\n(d) the reasons behind the Government asking States to stop projects on Krishna and Godavari rivers?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI & SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA)\n(a) Both the Governments of Telangana and Andhra Pradesh have mutually complained against each\nother about new projects on Krishna and Godavari rivers without submitting their Detailed Project\nReports (DPR) to Krishna River Management Board (KRMB) and Godavari River Management\nBoard (GRMB) respectively for appraisal and clearance.\n(b) & (c) An Apex Council was constituted under the AP Reorganization Act, 2014 for deciding on\nwater relared inter -state matters. The second meeting of the Apex Council was held on 06.10.2020\nunder the Chairmansh ip of Hon’ble Minister (Jal Shakti) with Hon’ble Chief Minister of Telangana\nand Hon’ble Chief Minister of Andhra Pradesh. In the meeting, it was decided that both the States\nshall submit the DPRs of new projects to KRMB/ GRMB for appraisal and subsequent sanction by the\nApex Council.\n(d) Taking up of new projects by Andhra Pradesh and Telangana in Krishna or Godavari rivers is in\nviolation of section 84(3)(ii), 85(8)(d) and para 7 of the Eleventh Schedule of Andhra Pradesh\nReorganization Act, 2014. Hence , both the States have been asked to not go ahead with new projects\nin Krishna or Godavari rivers till the projects are appraised by KRMB/GRMB and thereafter a\nsanction of Apex Council is obtained.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |568", "t": "Free Coaching and Allied Scheme", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.568 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n  \nFREE COACHING AND ALLIED SCHEME \n568.  SHRI B.B. PATIL: \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) the present status of the existing coaching centres identified in the State of Telangana \nunder Free Coaching and Allied Scheme for the candidates of Minority Communities; \n(b) the details of the funds allocated and released to these coaching centres under the s aid \nscheme during the last three years; \n(c) the details of the student s of minority community benefit ed under Scholarship schemes, \nFree Coaching and Allied Scheme during the last three years and the current year, \nState/UT and year-wise; \n(d) the details of schemes aimed at providing free coaching to minority students during the \nabove period; and \n(e) whether there exists any implementing agency to have a check on such coaching \ninstitutions/organizations and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OFMINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a) & (b): The Ministry of Minority Affairs implements the Free Coaching and Allied \nScheme (Naya Savera) under which free coaching is provided to students/candidates who \nbelong to the six notified minority communities and whose total  annual family income \nfrom all sources does not exceed Rs. 6.00 lakhs per annum. Eleven  Project Implementing \nAgencies (PIAs ) were empanelled under the scheme w.e.f. 2017 -18 from the State of \nTelangana and an amount of Rs. 7.71 crore  has been released to the PIAs in the State of \nTelangana during the financial years 2017-18 and 2018-19.  \n \n(c): In the last three years, 1,90,52,213 students have benefitted under the scholarship scheme.  \nIn the last three years and the current year 36839 students / candidates have benefitted / \nbeen allocated under the free coaching and allied scheme. The State-wise and Year –wise \ndetails of students of minority community benefitted under Scholarship schemes and Free \nCoaching and Allied Scheme during the last three years and current year are at Annexure-\nI and Annexure-II respectively.  \n \n \n \n\n \n-2- \n \n(d): The Ministry of Minority Affairs implements Naya  Savera– Free Coaching and Allied \nScheme under which free coaching is provided to students of six centrally notified \nminority communities namely Muslims, Christians, Sikhs, Buddhists, Jains, and \nZoroastrians (Parsis). The scheme provides coaching to students so as to enable them to \nqualify in entrance examinations for admission in technical/ professional courses , and \nvarious Competitive examinations for recruitment to Group ‘A’, ‘B’ and ‘C’ services. \nThe scheme is implemented through Project Implementing Agencies  (PIAs)/Non-\nGovernmental Organizations ( NGOs). Details are available on \nwww.minorityaffairs.gov.in. \n \n(e): Coaching institutions/organizations are monitored by State Government as per \nprovisions of the scheme including checking their infrastructure and mid -term inspection \nreport. In addition, National Productivity Council (NPC) which is an independent agency \nunder administrative control of Department for  Promotion of Industry & Internal Trade, \nMinistry of Commerce and Industry , Government of India  had been engaged to conduct \nconcurrent monitoring of the scheme w.e.f. 2016-17 to 2018-19.  Apart from this, in 2020 \nthe Indian Institute of Public Administration (IIPA), New Delhi has conducted the third \nparty evaluation study also of the scheme. \n \n*** \n \n \n \n\n \n * Provisional Data as on 15.01.2020. Disbursement of Scholarship for 2019-20 continues in 2020-21. \nNote- Arunachal Pradesh and Lakshadweep do not avail Scholarship under aforesaid schemes. \n \nAnnexure-I \nANNEXURE REFFERRED IN REPLY TO PART (C) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 568 TO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 ASKED BY SHRI \nB.B. PATIL REGARDING SCHOLARSHIP SCHEMES \nPre-Matric, Post-Matric and Merit-cum-Means Scholarship Scheme: State-wise and year-wise details of scholarships sanctioned  during the last three years (2017-18 \nto 2019-20) \nS. \nNo.  States/UTs \nPre-Matric Scholarship Scheme Post Matric Scholarship Scheme Merit-cum-Means based Scholarship \nScheme \n2017-18 2018-19 2019-20*  2017-18 2018-19 2019-20*  2017-18 2018-19 2019-20*  \nScholarshi\np \nSanctioned \nScholarship \nSanctioned \nScholarship \nSanctioned \nScholarship \nSanctioned \nScholarship \nSanctioned \nScholarship \nSanctioned \nScholarship \nSanctioned \nScholarship \nSanctioned \nScholars\nhip \nSanction\ned \n1.  Andhra \nPradesh 132983 129589 161555 14161 13292 15408 2618 2599 3476 \n2.  Telangana 176726 169146 171908 16275 15597 17393 2913 2854 3311 \n3.  Assam 225605 116564 290719 33071 27856 38531 5178 4437 6158 \n4.  Bihar 122582 222209 245662 50511 49333 48456 11094 11144 6985 \n5.  Chhattisgarh 7958 5595 4839 2189 2114 2202 499 421 432 \n6.  Goa 1460 349 497 291 140 218 119 76 76 \n7.  Gujarat 134673 128432 134907 20454 20652 23445 4058 3437 3037 \n8.  Haryana 4685 6259 8860 5247 3792 47"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |569", "t": "Corona Vaccination for Haj Pilgrimage", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 569   \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \nCORONA VACCINATION FOR HAJ PILGRIMAGE \n \n569. SHRI TALARI RANGAIAH: \n \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Union Government has made any arrangements to administer coronavirus \nvaccines to those going from the country for the Haj Pilgrimage;   \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the Government has reduced the Embarkation point -wise estimated cost per Haj \nPilgrimage; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof ? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a) & (b) The National Expert Group on Vaccine Administration for COVID -19 (NEGVAC) has \nbeen established, which provides guidance on all aspect of COVID-19 vaccination including \nprioritization of population groups, procurement and inventory management, vaccine selection, \nvaccine delivery and tracking mechanism, etc. The prioritized groups comprise of Health Care \nWorkers, Front Line Workers, persons above the age of 50 years and persons below 50 years with \nassociated co-morbidity, which are being covered in phased manner.  \nThere is no International Health regulation mandating COVID vaccination for international travel. \nFurther, till date no travel protocol for travelling to Saudi Arabia has been received mandating \nCOVID 19 vaccination.  \n(c) & (d)  The cost of Haj pilgrimage is decided by the Haj Committee of India, a st atutory body \nunder the administrative control of this Ministry, on actual basis. Based on the inputs received \nfrom Consulate General of India in Jeddah, HCoI has calculated the approximate cost for Haj \n2021, Embarkation Point-wise as under: \nS.No. Embarkation Point Approximate estimated cost \n1. Ahmedabad Rs. 3,28,168/- \n2. Bengaluru Rs. 3,42,994/- \n3. Cochin Rs. 3,56,433/- \n4. Delhi Rs. 3,44,809/- \n5. Guwahati Rs. 3,99,273/- \n6. Hyderabad Rs. 3,49,381/- \n7. Kolkata Rs. 3,69,050/- \n8. Lucknow Rs. 3,44,133/- \n9. Mumbai Rs. 3,29,279/- \n10. Srinagar Rs. 3,61,927/- \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |57", "t": "Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana", "head": "�\n������������������� �\n����������������������������������������������������� �\n����������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n���������������������� ����\n������������������ ��� ��������������� �\n�\n��������������������������������� �\n�\n�\n���� ������������������������ ���\n� �\n� ����� ���� ��������� ��� ����������� ������� ���������� ���� ���������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� �������� ���� ����������� ���� ��������� �������� �������������������������� ��������\n������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������� ����������������������� ������������������������\n������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������ �\n���������������������� ���������������������������������������� �\n����������������������������� �\n�\n������������������������ ����� ����������� ��� ����� ����� ����������������� ��������������\n������� ���������������� ����������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ���������� �������� ����� ���������� � ����� ����������� ���� ������������� �������� �����������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������� ������ ��������������������� ����������� ���� �������� ������������� ����� ���������\nILVKHUV\u0003DQG\u0003ILVK\u0003IDUPHUV¶\u0003LQFRPHV\u0003DQG\u0003\u0003JHQHUDWLRQ\u0003RI\u0003HPSOR\\PHQW\u000f\u0003\u000bY\f\u0003HQKDQFLQJ\u0003FRQWULEXWLRQ\u0003WR\u0003\n������������ ���� ���� ��������� ����� �������� ��������� ���� ��������� ��������� ���� �������� ���� �����\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n\n���� ��������� ��� �������������� ����������� ���� �������� ������ ����\n������� ������������ �������� ���� ������ ������ �� ����� ������� �� �\n���������������� ��������������������������� ����\n�\n����� ��"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |570", "t": "Implementation of DAY-NULM in Bihar", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 570 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nIMPLEMENTATION OF DAY-NULM IN BIHAR \n \n         NO. 570. SHRIMATI VEENA DEVI: \n \n         Will the Minister of Housing & Urban Affairs be pleased to state:- \n \n(a)  whether the Union Government has implemented D eendayal \nAntodaya Yojana - National Urban Livelihoods Missio n (DAY-\nNULM) in Bihar; \n(b)   if so, the details thereof including the numb er of the \nbeneficiaries under this scheme at present; \n(c)   whether the number of the beneficiaries in Bi har is lower than \nthe national average; \n(d)   if so, the reasons therefor along with the st eps taken by the \nUnion Government to bring reform in this regard; and \n(e)  the initiatives taken in this direction by the  Union Government \nin Bihar in order to achieve rapid progress in reforms? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n (a) & (b) Yes, Sir. The Ministry of Housing & Urba n Affairs is \nadministering DAY-NULM w.e.f. September, 2013 in th e country, \nincluding Bihar. The number of beneficiaries of DAY -NULM in Bihar \ninclude, 1,99,004 members of Self Help Groups, 24,7 53 skill-trained \ncandidates and 6,971 micro-entrepreneurs. Further, 1,15,884 urban  \n \nContd…2/ \n \n\n \n \n-2- \n \nstreet vendors and 10,253 urban homeless have been identified in \nsurvey, conducted by the Urban Local Bodies. \n(c) to (e) The Mission does not have a national ave rage for \nbeneficiaries’ coverage, \ninter-alia, because States/ UTs  notify the \nUrban Local Bodies, as per their capacity and requi rement, to \nimplement either the entire Mission or its specific  components. The \nStates/ UTs identify the beneficiaries as those liv ing below poverty \nline in urban areas. Further, the coverage may be b roadened by \nincluding families of disadvantaged groups like SCs , STs, women, \nminorities, disabled etc. subject to a maximum of 2 5 percent of the \nurban poor population. \n \n*****  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |571", "t": "Assitance to MSMEs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 571\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON\n \n04.02.2021\n \n \nASSISTANCE TO MSMEs\n \n \n571\n.\n \n \nSHRIMATI   MAHUA  MOITRA\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nthe status and efficacy of the steps already taken such as loan waivers, moratoriums, \netc. to assist MSMEs to recover from Covid\n-\n19 pandemic;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \nwhether the Government is taking \nsteps to assist MSMEs in recovering from the \npandemic and if so, the details thereof;\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nthe steps being taken the Government to make MSMEs go digitally (have website, \nemail, communication apps) as many services and needs have suddenly gone online; and\n \n(\nd\n)\n \nthe st\neps /incentive being given for technology transformation for MSME sector?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NITIN GADKARI)\n \n \n(a) & (b)\n:\n \n \nPost Covid\n-\n19, Government has taken a number of initiatives to support the \nMicro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSME) Sector in the country and to help them \nrecover. These steps inter\n-\nalia include Rs 20,000 crore Subordinate Debt for MSMEs under \nCredit Gua\nrantee Scheme for Subordinate Debt (CGSSD), Rs. 3 lakh crores collateral free \nautomatic loans for business, including MSMEs under Emergency Credit Line Guarantee \nScheme (ECLGS), Rs. 50,000 crore equity infusion in MSMEs through Fund of Funds, \nrevised crite\nria for classification of MSMEs, new registration of MSMEs through 'Udyam \nRegistration', doing away with  global tenders for procurement up to Rs. 200 crores etc. In \naddition to this, RBI has also announced several measures to Reduce Financial Stress of \nMS\nMEs. As on date 34 Banks have registered under CGSSD. As on 25.01.2021, guarantees \nto the tune of around Rs 2,01,364 crore has been issued under ECLGS. \n \n \n(c)\n:\n \n \nGovernment has launched an\n \nonline Portal “CHAMPIONS” which covers many \naspects of e\n-\ngovernance i\nncluding grievance redressal and handholding of MSMEs. \nRegistration of MSMEs has also been made completely online under ‘Udyam Registration’. \nMinistry of MSME has also launched \n‘MSME SAMBANDH’ portal for monitoring \nimplementation of public procurement poli\ncy for the MSEs and ‘MSME SAMADHAN’ \nportal for enabling MSMEs to directly register their cases relating to delayed payments\n.\n \n \n(d)\n:\n \n \nMinistry of MSME is implementing various schemes and programmes to provide \naccess to technology upgradation. These schemes i\nnclude Credit Linked Capital Subsidy \nScheme and Technology Upgradation Scheme (CLCS\n-\nTUS), Micro and Small Enterprise \n–\n \nCluster Development Programme (MSE\n-\nCDP), MSME Technology Centres (MSME\n-\nTCs).\n \n \n*******\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |573", "t": "Role of NI-MSME in Promotion of MSMEs in Rural Areas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.573\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021\n \n \nROLE OF \nNI\n-\nMSME IN PROMOTION OF MSMEs IN RURAL AREAS\n \n \n573.\n \nSHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL:\n \n \nWill the Minister of \nMICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nthe role of National Institute of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprise (NI\n-\nMSME) in \npromoting rural development and the details of commercial schemes and policies implemented for \nsetting up and pr\nomotion of MSMEs in rural areas; and\n \n \n(b)\n \nthe achievements of the institute in this regard during the last three years?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NITIN GADKARI)\n \n \n(a):\n \nThe National Institute of Micro, Small and Medium \nEnterprise (ni\n-\nmsme) has been set up \nfor providing consultancy services for entrepreneurship development and promotion of MSMEs, \nincluding enhancement of their competitiveness to enable enterprise creation, capacity building \nfor enterprise growth and susta\ninability, creation, development and dissemination of enterprise \nknowledge. The Institute organizes research, seminars/ workshops with financial institutions, \neducational institutions, industry associations, Central/State Governments. In line with objectiv\ne \nof the Institute, it has taken initiatives in Cluster development of SFURTI Programmes in the \nStates of Jharkhand, Kerala, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Punjab, and \nMaharashtra. ni\n-\nmsme has also conducted One District One Product (ODO\nP) study sponsored by \nDepartment of MSME & Export Promotion, ODOP Cell, Government of Uttar Pradesh, Prepared \nDiagnostic Study Reports(DSRs) for ten districts of Uttar Pradesh namely Gorakhpur, \nMaharajganj, Azamgarh, Prayagraj, Kalpi (Orai), Sultanpur, Ram\npur, Bulandshar and \nFarookhabad. More than 30000 artisans were contacted directly and indirectly to understand their \nsocio\n-\neconomic conditions and prepared strategic action plan for development initiatives. \n \n \n(b):\n \nThe achievements of ni\n-\nmsme for the past t\nhree years is as under:\n \n \nSr. No.\n \nYear \n \nNo. of Programmes\n \nNo. of Trainees\n \n1.\n \n2017\n-\n18\n \n858\n \n27960\n \n2.\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n270\n \n13239\n \n3.\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n191\n \n5087\n \nTotal\n \n1319\n \n46286\n \n \nThe Institute has also completed 19 clusters as Nodal Agency, supported 09 clusters as Technical \nAgency and involved as implementing agency in 7 clusters. Overall, the institute has completed \n70 clusters including newly identified clusters for development.\n \n \n \n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |574", "t": "PMAY in Odisha", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 574 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nPMAY IN ODISHA \n \nNO. 574. SHRI MAHESH SAHOO: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the number of houses build under the Pradhan Ma ntri Awas \nYojna in Odisha, district-wise for the last two fiscals;  \n(b) whether the scheme was able to achieve its targ ets in last two \nfinancial years in Odisha and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) whether the progress of the scheme is as per th e desired speed \nand if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a)  Under Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana - Urban [PMAY - U] , 60,659 \nhouses have been constructed during last two fiscal  years (2018-19 \n& 2019-20), district wise details of which are Annexed.  \n \n(b)  & (c) 1,73,476 houses have been sanctioned so far i n the State \nof Odisha since launch of PMAY (U) in June, 2015. O f the sanctioned \nhouses, 1,19,243 houses have been grounded for cons truction, of \nwhich 79,126 houses have been completed. All the ho uses \nsanctioned under the scheme are to be completed wit hin the \nmission period of PMAY – U i.e. by the year 2022. \n \n***** \n\nAnnexure referred to in reply to LSUQ No 574 due for 04-02-2021 \nDistrict wise details of Houses constructed during \nlast two fiscals (F Y 2018-19 & F Y 2019-20) under \nPMAY-U in the state of Odisha \n \n \nSr. No.  \n \n \nName of District \n \nHouses Constructed during last two \nfiscals (F Y 2018-19 & FY 2019-20) \n(Nos.) \n1 Angul 2,776  \n2 Balangir 960  \n3 Baleshwar 2,486  \n4 Bargarh 2,223  \n5 Baudh 415  \n6 Bhadrak 3,806  \n7 Cuttack 3,747  \n8 Debagarh 608  \n9 Dhenkanal 2,730  \n10 Gajapati 637  \n11 Ganjam 7,883  \n12 Jagatsinghapur 1,035  \n13 Jajapur 1,156  \n14 Jharsuguda 1,168  \n15 Kalahandi 1,313  \n16 Kandhamal 1,796  \n17 Kendrapara 1,581  \n18 Kendujhar 2,264  \n19 Khordha 4,320  \n20 Koraput 2,614  \n21 Malkangiri 253  \n22 Mayurbhanj 3,131  \n23 Nabarangapur 1,210  \n24 Nayagarh 1,579  \n25 Nuapada 1,203  \n26 Puri 1,282  \n27 Rayagada 774  \n28 Sambalpur 2,207  \n29 Subarnapur 1,181  \n30 Sundargarh 2,321  \n Grand Total  60,659  \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |575", "t": "Youth Convention Programmes", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF YOUTH AFFAIRS) \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 575 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \nYouth Convention Programmes \n \n575.     SHRI PARTHIBAN S.R: \n \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased \nto state: \na) the salient features of district youth convention programmes \nbeing implemented in the country; \nb) the number of youth convention programmes organised by \nthe Government across the country during the last three \nyears and the current year, State/UT -wise including Tamil \nNadu; \nc) the budgetary allocation for the purpose during the said \nperiod, State/UT-wise; and \nd) the other steps are taken/being taken by the Government in \nthis regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR \nYOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n \n(a) District Youth Convention Programme is one of the core \nprograms organized by Nehru Yuva Kendra Sangathan (NYKS). It \nis a one day programme in which 100 youth leaders from Youth \nClubs participate. Issues of s ocial and national importance are \nhighlighted. Resource Persons provide orientation to youth \nleaders on addressing local and contemporary issues. Youth \nleaders also share experience/  best practices. Youth Leaders in \nturn disseminate the acquired inputs amo ng members of their \nrespective youth clubs. \n  \n\n \n(b)&(c) Rs. 30,000/- per convention per district per year is allocated \nto each of the district Nehru Yuva Kendras for conduct of District \nYouth Convention Programme. Details of number of Youth \nConvention Programmes organized in States/UTs/Districts, \nincluding Tamil  Nadu, during the last three years i.e. 2017 -18, \n2018-19 and 2019 -20 are at Annexure. District youth Convention \ncould not be  organized during the  current year due to Corona \npandemic situation.  \n \n(d) This is an ongoing programme. Experience sharing amongst \nyouth leaders has been included in this program. \n***** \n  \n\n \nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE  REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (B)  & (C) OF THE LOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED Q.NO. 575 FOR 04.02.2021 ASKED BY SHRI PARTHIBAN S.R \nREGARDING YOUTH CONVENTION PROGRAMMES. \n \nYouth Conventions Organized  \nS N Name of State/UT Number of Conventions/Districts \n2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 14 13 13 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh  14 14 13 \n3 Assam 27 25 27 \n4 Bihar 36 37 24 \n5 Chhattisgarh 16 16 15 \n6 Delhi 9 9 9 \n7 Gujarat, Dadra Nagar Haveli Andaman & Diu 24 28 22 \n8 Haryana 19 19 13 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 12 12 12 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 14 14 12 \n11 Jharkhand 22 22 22 \n12 Karnataka 24 27 24 \n13 Kerala & Lakshadweep 16 16 15 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 44 42 34 \n15 Maharashtra and Goa 35 35 36 \n16 Manipur 10 10 10 \n17 Meghalaya 3 7 7 \n18 Mizoram 8 8 8 \n19 Nagaland 11 11 11 \n20 Odisha 30 29 26 \n21 Punjab & Chandigarh 20 21 16 \n22 Rajasthan 30 27 25 \n23 Sikkim 4 4 4 \n24 Tamil Nadu & Pondicherry 32 32 25 \n25 Telangana 9 9 8 \n26 Tripura 4 4 4 \n27 Uttar Pradesh 55 67 51 \n28 Uttarakhand 9 6 13 \n29 West Bengal & A&N Island 29 29 29 \n  Total 580 593 528 \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |576", "t": "SAI Centres", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 576 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04/02/2021 \n \nSAI Centres \n \n†576. SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARASWATI: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government is aware that SAI centres  have been \nestablished in various states to promote sports; \n(b) if so, the essential criteria to set up a SAI centre; and \n(c) the names of locations where SAI centres have  been established \nincluding Parliamentary Constituency Sikar, district-wise? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) to (c) Yes Sir. Sports Authority of India (SAI) Centres have been \nestablished in various States to promote  sports. These Centres are \nestablished based on various criteria including the potential and \nperformance of athletes in the catchment area along with availability of \ninfrastructure and funds. \n \nThe names and locations of SAI Centres in the country are placed \nat Annexure-I. \n***** \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART S (a) to (c)  OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 576 FOR 04/02/2021 REGARDING SAI \nCENTRES, ASKED BY SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARASWATI, HON’BLE MP. \n \nNATIONAL CENTRE OF EXCELLENCE (NCOEs) \nSN Centre Name \n Regional Centre (RC) Bhopal \n Madhya Pradesh \n1. Bhopal  \n RC Kolkata \n West Bengal \n2. Kolkata  \n Odisha \n3. Jagatpur \n Laxmi Bai National College of Physical Education (LNCPE) Thiruvananthapuram \n Kerala \n4. Trivandrum  \n5. Alleppey \n RC Bangalore \n Karnataka \n6. Bangalore  \n RC Guwahati \n Assam \n7. Guwahati  \n Arunachal Pradesh \n8. Sangey Lhaden Sports Academy (SLSA) Itanagar,  \n RC Sonepat \n Haryana \n9. Sonepat  \n10. Rohtak \n RC Chandigarh \n Chandigarh (UT) \n11. Zirakpur \n Himachal Pradesh \n12. Dharamshala \n RC Imphal \n Manipur \n13. Imphal  \n RC Gandhinagar \n Gujarat \n14. Gandhinagar  \n Para Sports \n Netaji Subhash National Institute of Sports (NSNIS) Patiala \n Punjab \n15. Patiala  \n RC Mumbai \n\nSN Centre Name \n Maharashtra \n16. Kandivali  \n17. Aurangabad \n RC Lucknow \n Uttar Pradesh \n18. Lucknow  \n Sports Authority of India (SAI) Head Quarter \n Delhi (UT) \n19. Jawahar Lal Nehru (JLN) Stadium \n20. Indira Gandhi (IG) Stadium \n21. Major Dhyan Chand National (MDCN) Stadium \n22. Dr. Shyama Prasad Mukherjee Swimming Pool Complex (SPMSPC) \n23. Dr.Karni Singh Shooting Range (KSSR) \n \nSAI TRAINING CENTERS (STC) SCHEME \nSN Region/State/Centre \n  \n RC Bangalore \n Karnataka \n1 Dharwad \n2 Medikeri \n3 Bangalore \n Andhra Pradesh \n4 Eluru \n5 Kurnool \n6 Visakhapatnam \n Telangana \n7 Hyderabad  \n LNCPE Trivandrum \n Tamil Nadu \n1 Chennai  \n2 Salem \n3 Mayiladuthurai \n Puducherry (UT) \n4 Puducherry  \n5 Yanam \n Kerala \n6 Trichur  \n7 Kollam \n8 Calicut \n9 Tellicherry  \n Lakshadweep (UT) \n10 Androth \n RC Bhopal \n Madhya Pradesh \n1 Jabalpur \n2 Tikamgarh \n3 Dhar  \n\nSN Region/State/Centre \n  \n Chhattisgarh \n4 Raipur \n5 Rajnandgaon \n RC LUCKNOW \n Uttar Pradesh  \n1 Raibareily  \n2 Safai Etawah \n3 Bareilly \n4 Varanasi \ni) Nivedita Girls Inter College, Varanasi \nii) Banaras Hindu University, Varanasi \n Uttrakhand \n5 Kashipur  \n RC CHANDIGARH \n Jammu & Kashmir (UT) \n1  Jammu \n Ladakh (UT) \n2 Kargil \n Himachal Pradesh \n3 Bilaspur  \n Punjab \n4 Mustana Sahib  \n5 Badal \n6 Ludhiana \n NSNIS PATIALA \n1 Patiala \n RC SONEPAT \n Haryana \n1  Kurukshetra  \n2  Bhiwani \n3  Hissar \n Delhi (UT) \n4 Bawana \n RC KOLKATA \n West Bengal \n1 Lebong \n2 Burdwan \n3 Jalpaiguri \n4 Bolpur  \n Andman & Nicobar Islands \n5 Port Blair  \n Tripura \n6 Agartala  \n Odisha \n7 Cuttack  \n8 Dhankenal \n9 Sundergarh \n\nSN Region/State/Centre \n  \n Bihar \n10 Patna \n11 Kishanganj \n12 Giddaur \n Jharkhand \n13 Hazaribagh \n14 Ranchi \n RC GANDHINAGAR \n Rajasthan \n1 Jodhpur  \n2 Alwar \n3 Jaipur \n RC MUMBAI \n Goa \n1 Ponda  \n Peddem \n RC IMPHAL \n Manipur \n1 Imphal  \n2 Utlou \n Mizoram \n3 Aizwal  \n Nagaland \n4 Dimapur  \n RC GUWAHATI \n Assam \n1 Golaghat  \n2 Tinsukia  \n3 Kokrajhar \n Arunachal Pradesh \n4 Naharlagun \n Meghalaya \n5 Shillong  \n Sikkim \n6 Namchi  \n \nEXTENSION CENTRE OF STC/ SAG CENTERS \nSN Name of the Schools & Colleges \n  \n RC Gandhinagar \n Gujarat \n1 Kendriya Vidyalaya No.3 Surat  \n Rajasthan \n2 Govt. College, Ajmer  \n3 Govt. Hr.Sec. School, Baori, Sikar \n4 University of Rajasthan, Jaipur  \n5 Mayo College, Ajmer \n\nSN Name of the Schools & Colleges \n  \n6 LBS Govt. Degree College, Kotputli \n7 Sri Kalyan Singh Rajkiya Sr. Sec. School, Jaipur \n8 Jawahar Navodaya Vidyala, Paota Jaipur \n RC Chandigarh \n JAMMU & KASHMIR \n1 Water Sports, Nehru Park \n HIMACHAL PRADESH \n2 Rainbow International School, Nagrota Bagwan, Kangra \n3 Govt. Sr.Sec. School, Dhawal, Mandi \n4 P.G. Govt. College, Hamirpur \n PUNJAB \n5 Khalsa Sr.Sec.School, Ropar \n6 Dashmesh Khalsa College, Zirakpur \n RC Bhopal \n Madhya Pradesh \n1 Ext. Centre Khandwa  \n2 Govt. Boys Multipurpose Hr. Sec. School, Malhar Ashram, Indore  \n RC, Lucknow \n UTTAR PRADESH \n1 Gurukul Archery Academy, Tikri, Meerut \n RC Kolkata \n Odish"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |577", "t": "Expanding SCM", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS   \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 577 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 4, 2021 \n \nEXPANDING SCM \n \nNO. 577.   MS. S. JOTHIMANI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n(a)  whether the Government is planning to expand t he \nnumber of cities to be developed under the Smart Ci ty \nMission (SCM); \n(b)  if so, the details thereof for the State of Ta mil Nadu; \n(c)  the criteria for selection of cities for SCM; \n(d)  whether the Government is considering Karur ci ty for \nSCM;      and \n(e)  if so, the details thereof and if not, reasons  thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a)       No, Sir. \n(b) \n Does not arise. \n \n \n(c)      The selection process of Smart Cities was conducted  through \na competition in two stages. In the first stage, St ates/Union \nTerritories(UTs) shortlisted potential Smart Cities  based on predefined \nscoring criteria. In the second stage, competing cities prepared their  \nContd…/2 \n\n \n-2- \nSmart City Proposals (SCPs) as per guidelines and s ubmitted them as \npart of the National competition. A total of 100 ci ties were selected as \nSmart Cities in Four Rounds of National Challenge f rom January, 2016 \nto June, 2018.  \n(d)    No, Sir. \n(e)   As per the first stage of the selection proce ss, the Government of \nTamil Nadu conducted the intra-State competition an d shortlisted \neleven Smart Cities, viz., Chennai, Coimbatore, Ero de, Madurai, Salem, \nThanjavur, Thoothukudi, Tiruchirappalli, Tirunelvel i, Tiruppur & \nVellore \n . The city of Karur was not part of the initial sho rtlist prepared \nby the Government of Tamil Nadu. All the eleven cit ies shortlisted by \nGovernment of Tamil Nadu have been selected for dev elopment as \nSmart Cities by the Central Government through a na tion-wide second \nround of challenge. As the Smart City Challenge has  been completed \nwith the selection of Shillong as the 100th city in  June, 2018, it is not \npossible to add any more cities under SCM. \n \n…… \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |578", "t": "Khelo India Centres of Excellence", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 578 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \nKhelo India Centres of Excellence \n \n578. DR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has inaugurated 8 Khelo  India Centres of \nExcellence in Various States recently; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the details of criteria for the eight States selected  for the location of \nthese Centre for Excellence; \n \n(d) whether the Government Proposes to provide a  centre of excellence in \nthe State of Andhra Pradesh; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS  \n{ SHRI KIREN RIJIJU } \n \n(a) and (b) Yes, Sir.  The Government has inaugurated eight Khelo India \nCentres of Excellence in various states recently . The details of the same \nare at Annexure. \n \n(c) These centres have been identified based on t he recommendation of the \nState Governments. Factors such as sports science setup, availability of \ninfrastructure and manpower, past achievements , etc. were taken into \nconsideration while identifying these centres. \n \n(d) and (e) Yes, Sir. Dr. YSR Sports Scho ol, YSR District, Kadapa, Andhra \nPradesh, has been approved as Khelo India State Centre of Excellence. \n  \n\nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART S (a) and (b) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 578 TO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \nREGARDING “ KHELO INDIA CENTRES  OF EXCELLENCE ” ASKED BY DR. \nBEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI, HON’BLE MEMBER OF LOK SABHA \n \nDetails of Khelo India Centres of Excellence inaugurated recently \n \nS. \nNo. Proposal State / Union \nTerritory \n1 Sangey Lhaden Sports Academy, Chimpu, \nItanagar \nArunachal \nPradesh \n2 Sh. Jaiprakash Narayan National Youth \nCentre, Bangalore \nKarnataka \n3 GV Raja Sr. Sec. Sports School, \nThiruvananthapuram \nKerala \n4 Khuman Lampak Sports Complex, Imphal Manipur \n5 Rajiv Gandhi Stadium, Aizawl Mizoram \n6 State Sports Academy, IG Stadium, Kohima Nagaland \n7 Kalinga Stadium, Bhubaneshwar Odisha \n8 Regional Sports School, Hakimpet Telangana \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |579", "t": "Implementation of JJM", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 579 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n  \nImplementation of JJM \n579. ADV. ADOOR PRAKASH:  \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Government has reviewed the implem entation of Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) \nin various States and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b)  whether the Government has set any target for achieving the goals;  \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(d)  the details of the fund released to the State of Kerala and the amount utilized so far? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA) \n(a) to (c)  Government of India, in partnership wit h State Governments, is implementing Jal \nJeevan Mission (JJM)-Har Ghar Jal to enable every r ural household in the country to have \nassured potable water through Functional Household Tap Connection (FHTC) by 2024. \nTo ensure the successful implementation, Government  of India has been regularly reviewing \nthe implementation with respective State government s. Regularreview meetings including \nconferences and workshops through video conferences  are held as well asfield visits taken \nupwherein States are advised to plan and expedite t he implementation to achieve the goal in a \ntime-bound manner. \nAs reported by States/ UTs, as of date, out of 19.1 8 Crore rural households in the country, \n6.55 Crore (34.20%)are having potable tap water supply.  \n(d)  Under JJM, allocation, fund drawn by the Keral a State and reported utilization of \nCentral grant is as under: \n(Amount in Rs. Crore) \nFinancial \nyear \nOpening \nBalance \nAllocation \n Amount drawn \nby the State \nReported \nutilization \nUnspent \namount \n2019-20 2.58 248.76 101.29 62.69 41.18 \n2020-21 41.18 404.24 101.06 86.65 55.59 \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |58", "t": "Survey List of BPL", "head": "������������������� �\n�������������������������� �\n��������� �\n�����������������������������������������������������\n���������������������� �\n�\n������������������ �\n�����������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������� �\n�\n���������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� �������� ������ ��� ���� ������� ��� �������� ������