ent to Bhokardan \nJalna Partur road of SH-225 \nkm 1/00 to 3/00 and 10/00 to \n16/00 of Bhokardan Tq and \nDistrict Jalna  \n7.60 8.00 \nWork \ncompleted \n13 SH-215 Improvement to SH-24 \nWalsawangi Paradh Danapur \nBhokardan road SH-215 km \n28/00 to 35/00 in Bhokardan \nTq and District Jalna  \n5.00 7.00 \nWork \ncompleted \n14 SH-214 Improvement to Golegaon-\nAnwa-Jalgaon-Sapkal -\nparadh-Dhamagaon road SH-\n214 km 374/00 to 376/00 in \nBhokardan Tq and District \nJalna  \n3.00 2.00 \nWork \ncompleted \n15 SH-228 Improvement to Amerpur \nChikali Jafrabad road SH-228 \nkm 47/00 to 54/00 \n3.00 7.00 \nWork \ncompleted \n16 \nSH-249 \nImprovement to Hatta Purna \nRoad SH-249, Km. 58/00 to 70/00 \nTq. Purna Dist. Parbhani   \n20.40 12.00 \nWork \ncompleted \n17 \nSH-145 \nLatur - CRF - 16 Improvement to \nLatur Barshi Road SH-145 Km. \n216/00 to 222/00 \n8.00 6.00 \nWork \ncompleted \n\n18 \nSH-236 \nOsmanabad CRF - 16 \nImprovement to NH-211 to Para \nDongrewadi Kallam Latur Road \nSH-236 Km. 0/00 to 25/200.    \n12.60 25.20 \nAt \nimplementa\ntion stage \n19 SH-26 Ahawa Malegaon Nandgaon \nShiur Aurangabad Road SH-\n26 Km- 168/00 to 177/00 . \n14.50 9.00 \nAt \nimplementa\ntion stage \n20 SH-178 Karmad Ladsavangi Road \nSH-178 Km 35/00 to 40. 3.00 5.00"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |685", "t": "LHPs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 685 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 04, 2021 \n \nLHPs \n \nNO. 685. SHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY:  \nSHRI T.R.V.S. RAMESH:  \nSHRI KOTHA PRABHAKAR REDDY:  \nSHRI KURUVA GORANTLA MADHAV:  \nSHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR BELLANA:  \nSHRI SRIDHAR KOTAGIRI:  \nSHRI HEMANT TUKARAM GODSE:  \nDR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI:  \nSHRIMATI VANGA GEETHA VISWANATH: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Government has laid the foundation of s ome Light \nHouse Projects (LHPs) as part of Global Housing Tec hnology \nChallenge - India (GHTC -India) initiative in six States; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof including the guidelines; \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to include the Stat es of \nAndhra Pradesh and Uttar Pradesh for the Light Hous e \nProjects; \n(d)  if so, the details thereof, and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(e)  the details of minimum amount of funds required for  the said \nprojects and the quantum of Central Grant-in aid in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a)  & (b):Yes, Sir. As part of Global Housing Technolog y Challenge-\nIndia (GHTC-India), the foundation stones for six L ight House \nProjects (LHPs) in Tripura, Jharkhand, Madhya Pradesh,  \n \nCont’d….p.2/ \n \n\n \n-2- \n \nUttar Pradesh,Gujarat and Tamil Nadu were laid by H on’ble Prime \nMinister on January 01, 2021, the details in this r egard including \nthe guidelinesare available at GHTC-India website i .e. www.ghtc-\nindia.gov.in .  \n \nLHPs are model housing projects which will demonstr ate best of \nnew age innovative global technologies, materials a nd processes \nand deliver ready to live houses with better qualit y of construction \nin a sustainable manner within 12 months. These LHP s will serve \nas Live Laboratories for facilitating transfer of t echnology to the \nfield, its further replication and to adapt in the country.  \n \n(c) & (d):In order to select six States across the country for construction of \nLHPs, the Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs (Mo HUA) had \ninstituted a challenge for States/Union Territories  (UTs), wherein \nthey were requested to submit their proposals based  on pre-\nspecified parameters. After evaluation of proposals  received from \nStates, six States as above have been selected for construction of \nLHPs including State of Uttar Pradesh. At present, there is no \nproposal to include the State of Andhra Pradesh for the LHPs.  \n(e):  The minimum amount of funds required for the said projectsand the \nquantum of Central Grant-in aid is at Annexure. \n***** \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure referred in reply to LS USQ No. 685 due for answer on 04.02.2021 \n \nCity-wise details of LHPs along with the estimated cost of the project \n \nS. \nNo. \n \nLocation \nof LHP \nName of \nTechnology \nNo. of \nDwelling \nUnits \nCentral Share                  \n(₹ in crore) State/ \nBeneficiary \nShare \nTotal \nCost \n(₹ in \ncrore) \nCentral \nAssistance \nTechnology \nInnovation \nGrant  \n1 Agartala, \nTripura  \nLight Gauge \nSteel \nStructural \nSystem & \nPre-\nengineered \nSteel \nStructural \nSystem \n \n1,000  15.00  50.00  97.50 162.50 \n2 \nIndore, \nMadhya \nPradesh \nPrefabricated \nSandwich \nPanel \nSystem \n \n1,024  15.36  40.96  71.68  128.00  \n3 \nLucknow, \nUttar \nPradesh \nPVC Stay In \nPlace \nFormwork \nSystem \n \n1,040  15.60 41.60 73.70 130.90 \n4 \nChennai, \nTamil \nNadu \nPrecast \nConcrete \nConstruction \nSystem – \nPrecast \nComponents \nAssembled \nat Site \n \n1,152  17.28  46.08  52.90 116.26  \n5 Rajkot, \nGujarat \nMonolithic \nConcrete \nConstruction \nusing Tunnel \nFormwork \n \n1,144  17.16  45.76  55.97  118.89  \n6 Ranchi, \nJharkhand \n \nPrecast \nConcrete \nConstruction \nSystem – 3D \nVolumetric \n \n1,008  15.12  40.32  78.56  134.00  \nTotal  6,368  95.52  264.72  430.31  790.55  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |686", "t": "Cruise and Ferry Services", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 686\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 4TH FEBRUARY, 2021\nCRUISE AND FERRY SERVICES\n686. SHRI SYED IMTIAZ JALEEL:\nSHRI ASADUDDIN OWAISI:\nWill the Minister of PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS be pleased to state:\nqd qfud-ad 3it{ d-{flT?i }i*\n(a) whether Sagarmala Development Corporation Limited has recently asked for Expression of\nlnterest(Eol) for cruise and ferry services on some routes;\n(b) if so, the number of Eols received by the Government so far;\n(c) the routes finalized by the Government for such services; and\n(d) the time by which such services are likely to be started for benefit of the people of the country\nand neighbouring countries?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\n(SHRI MANSUKH MANDAVIYA)\n(a) Yes, Sir. Sagarmala Development Corporation Limited has recently invited Expression of\nI nterest(Eol) for Ro-Ro/Ro-P axl'ferry services.\n(b) Total 82 proposals, including both domestic and international routes, have been received.\n(c) & (d) The routes and timelines will be finalized after scrutinizing the proposals."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |687", "t": "Objectives of NRDWP", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.687 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n \nObjectives of NRDWP \n†687. SHRI VINAYAK RAUT: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Government has achieved the target s and objectives of the National Rural Drinking \nWater Programme (NRDWP) and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  the details of the amount allocated and releas ed by the Government during each of the last three \nyears and the current year for Maharashtra; \n(c)  whether the Government has succeeded in achiev ing the goal of providing clean water to all \nacross the country, especially in the State of Maharashtra; \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; and \n(e)  if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA) \n(a) & (c) to (d) Under erstwhile National Rural Dri nking Water Programme (NRDWP), now \nsubsumed under the Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM), object ive was to make provision of potable water in \nrural habitations with minimum 40 litre per capita per day (lpcd) and drinking water source at a \nreasonable distance. As reported by States/ UTs, as  on 31.01.2021, 79.20% rural habitations having \n78.12% population have provision of minimum 40 litr e per capita per day (lpcd) and 17.93% rural \nhabitations having 19.33% population have service l evel of less than 40 lpcd potable water supply. \nFurther, 2.88% rural habitations having 2.54% popul ation are reported to have ground water sources \nwith quality issues. \nGovernment of India is implementing Jal Jeevan Miss ion in partnership with States to provide tap \nwater connection to every rural household in the country, including those of Maharashtra, by 2024. As \nreported by States/ UTs, as on date, about 6.56 Cro re (34.25%) rural households have tap water \nsupply. In Maharashtra, out of 142.36 lakh rural ho useholds, 85.50 lakh (60%) have piped water \nsupply.   \n(b)  The year-wise details of the fund allocated an d drawn by the State of Maharashtra during the last  \nthree years and current year (as on 31.1.2021) is as under: \n \n \n \n  \n   \n \n(e)  Doesn’t arise.   \n**** \n(amount in \u0001  Crore)  \nFinancial Year Fund allocated Funddrawn by the Stat e \n2017 -18  338.13  282.47  \n2018 -19  474.16  239.06  \n2019-20 847.97 345.28 \n2020 -21  1,828.92  457.23  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |688", "t": "Atal Bhujal Yojana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †688\nANSWERED ON 04.02.2021\nATAL BHUJAL YOJANA\n†688. SHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM SHRANGARE\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has launched Atal Bhujal Yojana in view of the water scarcity due to severe\ndrought in many parts of the country and if so, the details thereof;\n(b) the details of the house s provided with water connection in the country under the Nal Se Jal scheme\nfrom December 2019 to till date;\n(c) the funds spent so far under the said scheme, district and State-wise;\n(d) whether Latur district has also been covered under Nal Se Jal scheme;\n(e) if so, the number of villages and the houses covered thereunder during the above said period; and\n(f) the details of the funds spent in Latur district under this scheme?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI & SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA)\n(a) Atal Bhujal Yojana (ATAL JAL) is a Central Sector Scheme of the Government of India with World\nBank assistance, having an outlay of Rs.6000 crore, with focus on community participation and demand\nside interventions for sustainable ground water management in identified water stressed areas.  The scheme\nis being taken up for a period of 5 years from 1.4.2020 in  9000 Gram panchayats  of  222 blocks/Talukas\nin 80 districts of seven States, viz. Gujarat, Haryana, Karnataka, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Rajasthan\nand Uttar Pradesh.\n(b) to (f) Government of India, in partnership with States, is implementing Jal Jeevan Mission - Har Ghar\nJal, which aims at providing potable water through tap water connection to every rural household across\nthe country including those of Latur district of Maharashtra by 2024.  Since launch of Jal Jeevan Mission ,\nmore than 81 thousand houses of Latur District o f Maharashtra as well as about 3. 32 Crore rural\nhouseholds in the country have been provided with tap water connections (till 31.01.2021). Further, 163\nvillages of Latur district of Maharashtra have become ‘Har Ghar Jal Village’ i.e. every household is getting\nassured tap water supply as on 31.01.2021.\nThe total estimated outlay of the Mission is Rs. 3.60 lakh Crore, out of which Rs. 2.08 lakh Crore\nis central share. The details of State -wise fund release under JJM for Financial Year 2019 -20 & 2020 -21\n(as on 31.01.2021) is enclosed at Annex-I. Further, ‘water’ being a State subject, the power to plan,\nimplement and operations & maintenance of water supply schemes lies with State Government, as such,\ndistrict-wise allocation/ release is not done by this Department.\n*****\n\nANNEXURE-I\nAnnexure referred to in reply to part s (b) to (f) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. †688 to be\nanswered on 04.02.2021 on ‘ATAL BHUJAL YOJANA.\nState-wise fund release under JJM for Financial Year 2019-20 & 2020-21\nAmount in Rs. Crore\nS. No. State Central Release\n2019-20\nCentral Release  2020-21\n(as on 31.01.2021)\n1 Andaman & Nicobar\nIslands\n0.5 0.66\n2 Andhra Pradesh 372.64 162.59\n3 Arunachal Pradesh 177.47 254.85\n4 Assam 442.36 523.62\n5 Bihar 417.35 350.42\n6 Chhattisgarh 65.82 222.76\n7 Goa 3.08 3.1\n8 Gujarat 390.31 662.31\n9 Haryana 149.95 72.38\n10 Himachal Pradesh 205.83 326.2\n11 Jammu & Kashmir 322.03 53.72\n12 Jharkhand 291.19 143.06\n13 Karnataka 546.06 296.29\n14 Kerala 101.29 101.06\n15 Ladakh 67.86 0\n16 Madhya Pradesh 571.6 640.06\n17 Maharashtra 345.28 457.23\n18 Manipur 91.17 98.85\n19 Meghalaya 43.01 87.46\n20 Mizoram 68.05 79.3\n21 Nagaland 56.49 57.05\n22 Odisha 364.74 609.11\n23 Puducherry 0 1.06\n24 Punjab 227.46 0\n25 Rajasthan 1,301.71 630.51\n26 Sikkim 26.15 15.68\n27 Tamil Nadu 373.1 458.72\n28 Telangana 105.52 82.71\n29 Tripura 145.37 39.15\n30 Uttar Pradesh 1,513.14 682.13\n31 Uttarakhand 170.53 90.64\n32 West Bengal 994.75 778.52\nSource: IMIS, DWS\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |689", "t": "Hathnikund Link Channel-2 Project", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †689\nANSWERED ON 04.02.2021\nHATHNIKUND LINK CHANNEL-2 PROJECT\n†689. SHRI PRADEEP KUMAR CHAUDHARY\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of the schemes launched by the Government in every State/Union Territory in association\nwith the Department of Water Resources and Public Health Engineering Department under their domain;\n(b) whether the Government proposes to start Hathnikund Link Channel-2 Project which has been pending\nfor years in western region of Uttar Pradesh;\n(c) if so, the details thereof; and\n(d) if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI & SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA)\n(a) Water resources development & management are planned, funded, executed and maintained by the\nState Governments themselves as per their own resources and priorities. In order to supplement the efforts\nof the State Governments, Government of India provides technical and financial assistance to State\nGovernments to encourage sustainable development and efficient management of water resources through\nvarious schemes and programmes.\nDuring 2016 -17, ninety -nine (99) on -going Major/Medium irrigation projects spread in 18\nStates/UT under Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY) - Accelerated Irrigation Benefits\nProgramme (AIBP), having ultimate irrigation potential of 76.03 lakh ha. and balance estimated cost of\nRs.77,595 crore (Central Assistance Rs.31342.50 crore) have been prioritized in consultation with States,\nfor completion in phases along with their Command Area Development & Water Management (CADWM)\nworks.\nOut of these 99 prioritized projects, AIBP works of 44 prioritized projects have been reported to be\ncompleted/almost completed. Central Assistance (CA) of Rs.11489.31 crore has been released for these\nprojects during 2016 -17 to 2019 -20. Further, during 2020 -21(so far) CA of Rs.1082.71 crore has been\nreleased.\nCurrently, 88 projects from 18 States are under implementation for the CAD works under\nCADWM program - executed by the State Government Departments viz., Command Are a Development\nAuthority (CADA), Water Resources Department (WRD), Public Works Department (PWD), Public\nHealth Engineering (PHE) Department etc.). The Surface Minor Irrigation (SMI) and Repair, Renovation\nand Restoration (RRR) of Water Bodies schemes under PMKSY (Har Khet Ko Pani) have multiple\nobjectives like expanding cultivable area under assured irrigation, improving water use efficiency etc.\nContd…P/2\n\n//2//\nSpecial Package for completion of 8 Major and Medium Irrigation projects and 83 SMI projects\nwith a balance cost of Rs. 13,651.60 crore to address agrarian distress in Vidarbha, Marathwada and other\nchronically drought prone areas of Maharashtra has been approved. The targeted additional potential\nthrough these projects is 3.77 Lakh Ha. During 2018-19, 2019-20 & 2020-21 (so far) Rs.500 crore, Rs.300\ncrore and Rs.400 crore have been released respectively as CA.\nGovernment of India has approved “Relining of Sirhind Feeder and Rajasthan Feeder\" to address\nthe problem of water-logging with a total cost of Rs. 1976.74 crore. CA component is Rs. 826.17 crore in\naddition to Rs.156 crore released earlier. Government of India has approved “Implementation of\nShahpurkandi Dam (National Project) on River Ravi in Punjab State ” with the approved cost of\nRs.2715.70 crore. The project would be implemented by the Govt. of Punjab. The Cent ral Assistance\ncomponent is Rs.485.38 crore.\nDuring XI Plan, Government of India had launched Flood Management Programme (FMP) with an\noutlay of Rs 8,000 crore. Under the scheme, financial assistance was being provided to State Governments\nfor taking up works of river management, flood control, anti -erosion, drainage development, anti -sea\nerosion, flood proofing etc. in critical regions and restoration of damaged flood control/ ma nagement\nworks. The Flood Management Program (FMP) was continued durin g XII Plan with an outlay of\nRs10,000 crore. FMP and River Management Activities & Works related to Border Areas are merged and\nrenamed as “Flood Management and Border Areas Programme (F MBAP)” for the period 2017 -18 to\n2019-20 and further extended upto March, 2021.\nA Centrally Sponsored Scheme “Irrigation Census” impl ements in States/UTs with 100 % Central\nfunding to build a comprehensive and reliable database in the Minor Irrigation se ctor for effective\nplanning and policy formulation. This is a continuing scheme since 1987. Under the scheme, each\nState/UT has identified a nodal department which is responsible for implementation of the scheme in the\nentire state /UT. The details of Nodal Departments identified by States/UTs are given in Annexure.\nNational River Conservation Plan (NRCP) covers 34 rivers in 77 towns spread over 16 States with\ntotal sanctioned cost of pollution abatemen"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |69", "t": "Increase in Human Trafficking", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 69 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 02ND FEBRUARY, 2021/ MAGHA 13, 1942 (SAKA) \n \nINCREASE IN HUMAN TRAFFICKING \n \n69. DR. AMAR SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that the threat of human trafficking in \nthe wake of the ongoing pandemic will be exponentially high due to poor \neconomic conditions; \n \n(b) if so, the details of the Government’s plans to prevent human trafficking \nof economically backward individuals, if any; and \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n(a) to (c): ‘Police’ and ‘Public Order’ are State subjects as per the Seventh \nSchedule to the Constitution of India.  Prevention and combating the crime \nof human trafficking is the responsibility of State Governments who are \ncompetent to take appropriate action as per extant provisions of law.  \nHowever, taking cognizance of the risks of human trafficking during COVID-\n19 pandemic, the Ministry of Home Affairs issued a comprehensive advisory \non 6.7.2020 to all States and UTs for countering and combating human \ntrafficking.  In this context, some of the measures they were advised to \nundertake are as follows:  \n\n--2-- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 69 FOR 02.02.2021 \n(i) Anti Human Trafficking Units (AHTUs) be set up and upgraded in all \ndistricts of the country on urgent basis, for which around Rs. 100 crore was \nreleased to all States and UTs. \n \n(ii) Coordination mechanism amongst various Departments of States and \nUTs be evolved to handle issues of human trafficking and this should be \nmonitored/reviewed at the highest level in all States and UTs.  \n \n(iii) Special awareness generation campaigns be organized by the District \nAdministration at all levels to protect vulnerable sections of society, \nespecially those who are vulnerable to fall prey in the hands of \nunscrupulous persons who may dupe them with promises of better life and \nwages. \n \n(iv) Law enforcement agencies may engage with representatives of local \nPanchayats, community leaders, village watch and ward, Municipal \nCommittees, Resident Welfare Association etc. in identification, tracing and \nrecovery of trafficked persons. \n \n(v) Specific ‘intelligence’ and ‘surveillance’ mechanism to identify gangs \nand  their  modus operandi  to exploit  people  and link with such matters be  \n \n\n--3-- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 69 FOR 02.02.2021 \nlooked into by the Police Department and a close watch on known criminals \nand traffickers be kept by local police. \n \n(vi) Preventive steps such as identification of children in distress, \nwatching out for suspicious persons, keeping special vigil at transit points, \nviz. Railway Stations, Bus Depots, Airports, Seaports, Border areas etc. and \nidentify vulnerable population and susceptible pockets in the State and UTs \nbe undertaken by the Police officers. \n \n(vii) States and UTs should ensure that shelters for destitute women and \nchildren in need should remain open and additional facilities be made \navailable for women and girls and virtual/ telephone counseling services be \nprovided to them.  \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |690", "t": "Sustainability of Drinking Water Resources", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.690 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2021 \n  \nSustainability of Drinking Water Resources \n690. DR. ARVIND KUMAR SHARMA: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Government has taken any steps to ensure sustainability of depleting local \ndrinking water resources; and \n \n (b)  if so, the details thereof?  \n \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA) \n \n(a) & (b)  Government of India is implementing Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) in partnership \nwith States to enable every rural household to have tap water supply by 2024. Development of \nreliable drinking water sources and/ or augmentatio n of existing sources to provide long-term \nsustainability of water supply system in villages, is an integral part of Jal Jeevan Mission. \nProvision has been made for the preparation of Vill age Action Plan (VAP) by village \ncommunity, which inter alia  includes strengthening of drinking water sources i n convergence \nwith other schemes such as MGNREGS, Finance Commiss ion grants to rural local bodies/ \nPRIs, MP& MLA’s Local Area Development Fund, District Mineral Development Fund, CSR \nfund, etc. \nIn 2019, Government of India also launched Jal Shak ti Abhiyan (JSA) an intensive water \nconservation campaign with five targeted interventi ons i.e. water conservation and rainwater \nharvesting; renovation of traditional and other wat er bodies; reuse and recharge of borewells; \nwatershed development and intensive afforestation, to improve water availability including \nground water in water–stressed 1,592 blocks of 256 districts of the country. \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |691", "t": "Population Control", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 691\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH February, 2021\nPOPULATION CONTROL\n691. SHRI ARJUN LAL MEENA:\nSHRI ANIL FIROJIYA:\nSHRI RANJEETSINGH HINDURAO NAIK NIMBALKAR:\nDR. SANJAY JAISWAL:\nSHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV:\nSHRI ASHOK KUMAR RAWAT:\nSHRI ANUMULA REVANTH REDDY:\nSHRI DIPSINH SHANKARSINH RATHOD:\nSHRI MOHANBHAI KALYANJI KUNDARIYA:\nSHRI SUMEDHANAND SARASWATI:\nSHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM SHRANGARE:\nSHRI BALAK NATH:\nSHRI DEVJI M. PATEL:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has taken/proposes to take steps to check the increasing population\nin the country, if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise along with the funds allocated, disbursed\nand utilized for the purpose during the last five years;\n(b) whether a National Policy has been prepared to check and control the population explosion in\nthe country;\n(c) if so, the long te rm objectives of the said policy and the steps taken by the Government to\nachieve the policy objectives;\n(d) if not, the time by which any national population policy or scheme is likely to be formulated\nto contain and check population explosion in the country;\n(e) whether there is any agency to monitor the implementation of the said policy;\n(f) whether the Government proposes to reward States with financial incentives which have\nachieved a growth in the population of below replacement level and if so, the details thereof; and\n\n(g) whether it is proposed to involve private sector for dispensing family planning services in\npoor performing States, if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY)\n(a) to (c): The Government has been implementing the National Family Planning Program\nwhich provides voluntary and informed choices to the beneficiaries through a target free\napproach with the objective of checking population increase in the country.\nA National Population Policy has been formulated in the year 2000 with the long -term\nobjective of attaining population stabilisation by 2045\nVarious initiatives have been taken under the National Family Planning Program\nproviding broad range of services mentioned as follows:\n1. New Contraceptive Choices: The current contraceptive basket comprising of Condoms,\ncombined oral contraceptive pills, emergency contraceptive pills, Intrauterine\ncontraceptive uterine device (IUCD) and sterilization has been expanded with inclusion\nof two new contraceptives - Injectable contraceptive (Antara programme) and\nCentchroman (Chhaya)\n2. Post-partum Intrauterine contraceptive device (PPIUCD) incentive scheme under\nwhich PPIUCD services are provided post delivery.\n3. Compensation scheme for sterilization acceptors which provides c ompensation for\nloss of wages to the beneficiary and also to the service provider and team for conducting\nsterilisation.\n4. National Family Planning Indemnity Scheme (NFPIS) under which clients are\ncompensated in the eventualities of death, complication and failure following sterilization\noperations.\n5. Family Planning Logistics Management Information System (FP -LMIS): dedicated\nsoftware has been launched to ensure smooth forecasting, procurement and distribution of\nfamily planning commodities across all the levels of health facilities.\n6. Mission Parivar Vikas: Mission Parivar Vikas has been introduced for substantially\nincreasing access to contraceptives and family planning services in seven high\nfocus states having TFR of more than 3 namely Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Madhya Pradesh,\nRajasthan, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh and Assam..\n7. Scheme for Home Delivery of contraceptives by ASHAs at doorstep of beneficiaries\nhas been taken up.\n8. Scheme for provision of Pregnancy Testing Kits in the drug kits of ASHA for use in\ncommunities.\nState/UT wise details of funds allocated and utilized for the purpose during last five\nyears is annexed.\n\n(d): Does not arise\n(e): The National Family Planning Programme of the Ministry of Health and Family\nWelfare is working closely with the states and UTs to provide technical support and guidance\nunder overall tenets of the National Population Policy 2000 and oversees its implementation.\n(f): Presently the Government does not have any such proposal.\n(g): The following schemes provide for involvement of private partnerships for\nprovisioning of Family Planning services:\n1) Accreditation/ Empanelment of private facilities/providers: The private\nfacilities/providers are accredited/ empanelled through State/District Quality Indemnity\nsub committee for provision of free sterilization services. The accredited facilities are\nalso provided indemnity coverage.\n2) Clinical Outreach Teams (COT) Scheme provides for family planning services through\nmobile teams from accredited organizations in far -flung, underserved and geographically\ndifficult areas in the seven Mission Parivar Vikas States name"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |692", "t": "Procurement of Cotton", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 692 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2021 \n \nPROCUREMENT OF COTTON \n \n692.   SHRI G.S. BASAVARAJ:  \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंत्री \nbe pleased to state : \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that cotton farmers from Punjab have sold out record bumper crop \nof cotton at MSPs of Rs. 5,665/ - per quintal during the 2020 -21 season, if so, the details \nthereof; \n \n(b) whether the Cotton Corporation of India has stopped procuring cotton from Punjab \nwith market prices prevailing much above the MSP, if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(c) whether small and marginal cotton farmers from Punjab have already sold their entir e \nstocks to Cotton Corporation of India at MSP, if so, the details thereof? \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र मंत्री (श्रीमती स्मृतत ज़ूबिन इरानी) \nMINISTER OF TEXTILES \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a): In the state of Punjab, during the current cotton season 2020 -21 (from 1st October, \n2020 to 30 th September, 2021), Cotton Corporation of India  (CCI) started purchases of \nseed cotton (kapas) under Minimum Support Price (MSP) operations and has procured \naround 28.25 lakh quintal kapas equivalent to 5.38 lakh bales through its 22 procurement \ncentres. MSP purchases had been made by paying MSP rate of Rs. 5665/ - per quintal, \ndepending upon the quality parameters, as per the guidelines. \n \n(b): No, Sir. CCI has not stopped purchases under MSP in Punjab. However, due to \nsubstantial increase in kapas rates during last two weeks, the ruling price of kapas is \nmore than MSP rates and hence farmers are getting better price for their produce in the \nmarket. \n \n(c): Out of the total estimated production of around 63 lakh quintal kapas equivalent to \n12 lakh bales in the state of Punjab, around 52.50 lakh quintal equivalent to 10 lakh bales \ni.e. 83% of the production had already been arrived in the market. CCI has procured 5.38 \nlakh bales so far from 1.03 lakh farmers who are mostly small and marginal. \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |693", "t": "Technology Development Centre", "head": "Page \n1\n \nof \n2\n \n  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY\n \nDEPARTMENT OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. \n693\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05/02\n/202\n1\n \nTechnology Development Centre\n \n \n693\n.\n \n \n \n \nSHRIMATI VEENA DEVI\n:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY \n\u0000व\u0000ान\n \nऔर\n \n\u0000ौ\u0000यो\u0000गक\u0000\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)\n \nwhether Government is aware that Bihar has a \nhuge strength of \nstudents having scientific acumen/ approach;\n \n(b)\n \nwhether Government has any plan to set up \na Technology Development \nCentre in Bihar to facilitate such students; and\n \n(c)\n \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \n \nMINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE; MINISTER OF SCIENCE AND \nTECHNOLOGY; AND MINISTER OF EARTH SCIENCES\n \n(DR. HARSH VARDHAN)\n \n\u0000वा\u0000\u0000य\n \nऔर\n \nप\u0000रवार\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n, \n\u0000व\u0000ान\n \nऔर\n \n\u0000ौ\u0000यो\u0000गक\u0000\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nऔर\n \nपृ\u0000वी\n \n\u0000व\u0000ान\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n(\nडॉ\n .\nहष\u0000\n \nवध\u0000न\n)\n \n \n \n(a)\n \nYes, Sir. The Government of India, is aware of the significant strength of students \nwith scientific acumen in Bihar having knowledge & potential and is encouraging it \nappropriately through its schemes and programs. \n \n \nIn order to attract young talent toward\ns pursuing scientific career, .Department of \nScience and Technology (DST)\n \n \nis implementing an umbrella scheme, ‘Innovation in Science \nPursuit for Inspired Research (INSPIRE)’,\n \n \nto communicate excitement of creative pursuit of \nscience to students.The scheme\n \nalso aims to build the required critical human resource pool \nfor strengthening and expanding the Research & Development (R&D) base. \n \n \nUnder this umbrella, the scheme INSPIRE Awards MANAK (Million Minds \nAugmenting National Aspiration and Knowledge) attract\ns talented school students at an \nearly age of 10\n-\n15 years studying in Class 6\n-\n10 to pursue science. One lakh ideas from \nthese students are eligible for INSPIRE Award of Rs. 10000/\n-\n \neach.\n \n \nIn last three years, 801 \nstudents from Bihar were given INSPIRE Awar\nds MANAK, which reflects the innovation and \ncreative potential of school students from Bihar.\n \nAnother component under INSPIRE, Scholarship for Higher Education (SHE) aims to \nencourage meritorious students to study basic and natural sciences in Bachelor in \nScience \n(B.Sc) and Masters in Science (M.Sc) through attractive scholarship and mentorship \n\nPage \n2\n \nof \n2\n \n  \nopportunities. Selections under SHE is based on performance (top 1%) in Class XII \nexaminations of State/Central School Examination Boards and in selected national le\nvel \ncompetitive examinations like IIT\n-\nJEE, etc.\n \n \nDuring last year (2019\n-\n20), 229 students from \nBihar received SHE scholarship, which reflects the merit potential of science students from \nBihar.\n \nDuring 27\nth\n \nNational Children Science Congress (NCSC), 2019 or\nganized by National \nCouncil for Science and Technology Communication (NCSTC), DST at Kerala, 30 school \nstudents from Bihar participated to exhibit their creativity and innovation and particularly their \nability to solve a societal problem experienced locall\ny using the method of science.\n \nUnder State Science and Technology Programme (SSTP) o\nf DST, Bihar Council of \nScience and Technology (BCST) \nsupported,\n \n \n500\n \n \nprojects\n \n \nof\n \n \nUnder\n \n \nGraduate\n \n \nstudents\n \n \nunder its \nStudent\n \n \nProject\n \n \nProgramme (SPP) in 2019\n-\n20 to \nencourage their efforts in science and \ntechnology.\n \nIn order to encourage students for career development in the area of Biotechnology, \nDepartment of Biotechnology (DBT), is supporting Postgraduate Teaching Program to \nensure high standard of teaching and to\n \ngenerate critical mass of trained manpower in \ncountry. A total of 72 courses are being supported under this programme across the country \nand one M.Sc. course in Agriculture Biotechnology is being supported at Dr. Rajendra \nPrasad Central Agricultural Unive\nrsity, Pusa, Samastipur (Bihar). Total 84 students were \nsupported in last 5 years at Dr. Rajendra Prasad Central Agricultural University, Pusa, \nSamastipur. Apart from this, DBT is also providing fellowships to biotechnology students for \npursuing doctoral r\nesearch in universities and / or research institutions in the country.\n \n \nTotal \n23 students supported in last 5 years under DBT\n-\nJRF Program in Bihar. \n \n \nUnder DBT Star College Scheme, envisaged for encouraging more students to take \nup higher education in scie\nnce, 3 colleges from Bihar are being supported a) Patna \nWomen's College b) Patna Science College c) Anugrah Narayan College, Patna\n \n \nIn addition to above, the Government of Bihar has taken several initiatives to nurture \nthe knowledge and potential of scienc\ne students of Bihar such as establishment of \nEngineering College, Polytechnic, facilitating proposal to set up Indian Institute of Science \nEducation and Research (IISER) and School of Planning and Architecture. BCST has \nsubmitted several proposals to Depar\ntment of Biotechnology (DBT) under "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |694", "t": "Shortage of Staff in CDSCO", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 694\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH February, 2021\nSHORTAGE OF STAFF IN CDSCO\n694. SHRI MANICKAM TAGORE B.:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that for about 20000 pharmaceutical manufacturers, the Central Drugs\nStandards Control Organization (CDSCO) has less than 300 staff to grant drug approvals;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether the Government had already agreed to place 1200 more regular staff and 1200 staff\non contract basis to strengthen the capacity of CDSCO ; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and the necessary steps taken by the Government in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY)\n(a) & (b): As on date, 492 regulatory posts are sanctioned for Central Drugs Standards\nControl Organization (CDSCO), Ministr y of Health & Family Welfare.  Out of these, currently\n395 posts are filled up.  In addition to regulatory posts of CDSCO, the State/UT Drug Control\nauthorities also have regulatory posts in their respective State/UT Drug Control Divisions to\ncater to the pharmaceutical manufacturers in their States/UTs.\n(c) & (d): The Government had approved a proposal for strengthening the drug regulatory\nsystem in the country, both under the Central and State Governments at a total expenditure of\nRs.1750 crore.  The Scheme had two components viz (i) Strengthening of the Central Drugs\nStandard Control Organisation (CDSCO) at a cost of Rs .900 crore, and (ii) Strengthening of\nStates’ Drug Regulatory System at a cost of Rs.850 crore.  This proposal inter -alia includes\naddition to the regular and contractual manpower strength of CDSCO and States.\nThe centrally sponsored scheme of Strengthen ing the States’ Drug Regulatory System\nwas implemented during the three financial years 2015-16, 2016-17 and 2017-18. Thereafter, the\nscheme was further extended upto March, 2021."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |695", "t": "Spread of Corona Virus", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 695\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH February, 2021\nSPREAD OF CORONA VIRUS\n695. SHRI NATARAJAN P.R.:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is aware of the date on which COVID-19 virus was first noticed in\nIndia;\n(b) if so, the details of preventive measures taken to contain the entry or delay the spread of the\nvirus across the country; and\n(c) the details of the budget provisions made and actual utilisation thereof to meet the expenses\nof preventive management and also mitigate adverse social impact of COVID-19?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY)\n(a)India’s first case of COVID -19 was reported on 30 th January 2020. The case was a student\nstudying in Wuhan, China.\n(b): A brief note on actions taken by Government of India to contain the entry and delay the\nspread of virus across the country is attached as Annexure.\n(c) Under the ‘India COVID19 Emergency Response and Health System Preparedness package’\nof Rs 15,000 crores has been approved by the Cabinet on 22 nd April 2020 with an objective to\nprevent, detect and respond to the threat posed by COVID-19.\nAs up to 15th January 2021, Rs 11, 361.67 Crores have been utilized for the same.\n\nAnnexure\nBrief note on action taken by Government of India to manage COVID-19\nGovernment of India has taken a series of actions to prevent, control and mitigate the impact of\nCOVID-19. India followed a whole of Government and whole of society approach. Hon’ble\nPrime Minister, a High -level Group of Ministers (GoM), Cabinet Secretary, Committee of\nSecretaries and senior officers in MoHFW continue to monitor public health response to\nCOVID-19 in the country.\nThe public health actions were taken in a pre -emptive, pro-active, graded manner based on the\nevolving scenario. A number of travel advisories were issued restricting inflow of international\ntravelers, till such time, commercial flights were suspended on 23 rd March, 2020. Till then, a\ntotal of 14,154 flights w ith 15,24,266 passengers have been screened at these airports. In\naddition, point of entry screening was also initiated at 12 major seaports, 65 minor seaports and\ninternational land border crossings.\nInternational travelers traveling to India under Vande Bharat Mission and Transport Bubble\nscheme are being screened at the airport.   As on 1 st February 2021, a total of 40.4 lakh\npassengers and crew members have been screened at the airports. It is a pre -requisite for such\ntravelers to bring negative COVID -19 test reports on get themselves at the airport, failing they\nhave to undergo institutional quarantine.\nIn view of detection of mutant variant of SARS -CoV-2 in United Kingdom, all flights\noriginating from U.K into India were suspended temporarily from 22 nd December to 7 th January\n2021. Regulated resumption of limited flights originating from United Kingdom (UK) to India\nhas been allowed from 8 th January 2021. Government of India has taken a number of steps to\nprevent spread of mutant SARS-CoV-2 variant spread in the country:\ni. All flights to UK were banned till from December 23, 2020 to 7th January 2021.\nii. Resumption of limited international flight services to/from UK has been allowed with\neffect from 8th January 2021 in a calibrated manner by initially allowing flight\nmovement to/from UK to five international airports i.e. Delhi, Mumbai, Bengaluru,\nHyderabad and Chennai only.\niii. All the passengers arriving from U.K. should be carrying Negative RT -PCR Test Report\nfor which test should have been conducted within 72 hours prior to undertaking the\njourney.\niv. All international passengers who arrived in India from UK or transit through UK are\nagain mandatorily tested for COVID-19 by RT-PCR at the airport.\nv. All passengers and their contacts who tested positive for RT -PCR are isolated, their\nsamples are sent for whole genome sequencing to detect UK variant.\nvi. All international passengers who test negative at Airports are quarantined for 14 days.\nvii. Detailed guidelines are available at:\nhttps://www.mohfw.gov.in/pdf/ExtensionofvalidityofStandardOperatingProcedureforEpi\ndemiologicalSurveillance&ResponseforthenewvariantofSARSCoV2.pdf\nCommunity surveillance through Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme is ongoing in\ncontainment zones in accordance with COVID-19 containment plan.\nThe laboratory network is continuously being strengthened. As of 1 st February 2021, total\noperational (initiated independent testing) government laboratories reporting to ICMR is 1214. In\naddition, 1147 Private Laboratories have been approved for COVID -19 Testing. A total of\n19,77,52,057 samples have been tested as on 1 st February 2021. At present India is testing about\na 7-8 lakh samples a day.\nAs on 2nd February 2021, a total of 15,288 CO VID treatment facilities with 12,54,128 dedi"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |696", "t": "Adoption of New Emission Norms by Coalfields", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 696. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2021 \n \nAdoption of New Emission Norms by Coalfields \n \n696.  SHRIMATI VANGA GEETHA VISWANATH: \n         SHRI KOTHA PRABHAKAR REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government has proposed pushing back deadlines for adoption of new \nemission norms by coalfields; \n(b) if so, the details and present status thereof; \n(c) whether the Union Government has received any requests from the State Government \nin this regard; and \n(d) if so, the State/UT-wise details thereof along with the action taken by the Government \nthereon? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE \n(SHRI BABUL SUPRIYO) \n \n(a) & (b):  The emission norms for coalfields are notified vide notification no. GSR \n742(E),dated 25thSeptember 2000. The Ministry has no proposal for pushing \nback deadlines for adoption of emission norms by coalfields. \n \n(c) & (d)        No such request has been received from State Governments.  \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |697", "t": "Crop Damage by Wild Animals", "head": " \nOIH \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 697 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2021 \n \nCrop Damage by Wild Animals \n \n†697. DR. DHAL SINGH BISEN:  \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n (a) the details of the crop damage by wild animals;  \n(b)  the details of the compensation given to the farmers by the Union Government to the \nrespective States between the year 2014 to 2019; \n(c)  whether the Government has formulated any scheme to prevent/check the damage to \ncrops by wild animals and if so, the details thereof;  \n(d)  if not, whether the Government proposes to provide relief to the farmers by formulating a \nCentral scheme or grant subsidy to the farmers who suffer losses and if so, the details \nthereof;  \n(e)  the funds available with the Compensatory Afforestation Fund Management and Planning \nAuthority (CAMPA) along with the fund provided to Madhya Pradesh and the respective \nwork on which the fund was utilised; and  \n(f)  whether any guidelines have also been issued to the States with regard to the nature of \nworks on which the said fund is utilized and if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n \n(SHRI BABUL SUPRIYO) \n \n(a)&(b) The management of forest and wildlife is the responsibility of concerned \nState/UT Governments and the details of the crop damage by wild animals are \nnot collated in the Ministry. However, the Ministry provides financial \nassistance to State/Union Territory Governments as per sanctioned annual plan \nof operations (APO) under the Centrally Sponsored Schemes of 'Development \nof Wildlife Habitats' and ‘Project Elephant’ for management of wildlife and its \nhabitats, including for activities like habitat enrichment, solar fences, animal \nproof trenches around forest, water augmentation, etc.  \n \n\n \n \n \n:2: \n \n \nThe financial support to the States/Union Territories  also include component \nof compensation due to damage by wild animals. The details of financial \nassistance released to the State/UT under these schemes during 2014-19 are \ngiven in the Annexure-I& II. \n \n \n(c) &(d) The responsibility to formulate the scheme to prevent/check the damage to \ncrops by wild animals lies with the concerned State Governments. However, \nthe Ministry provides financial assistance to State/Union Territory \nGovernments under the Centrally Sponsored Schemes of ‘Development of \nWildlife Habitats’, and ’Project Elephant’ for management of wildlife and its \nhabitats in the country. It includes compensation for depredation by wild \nanimals including cattle lifting, crop damage, loss of life and property. \n \nMinistry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare has modified operational \nguidelines for Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojna (PMFBY), under the revised \nguidelines states have been given liberty to consider providing add-on \ncoverage for crop loss due to attack by wild animals.  \n \n(e)  An amount of ₹60,047.47/- crore was transferred from the state specific bank \naccounts maintained by the Ad-hoc CAMPA to the Public Account of India \ncreated as per Compensatory Afforestation Fund (CAF) Act, 2016 during the \nperiod 08.02.2019 to 31.03.2020 and funds to the tune of ₹47,872.31 crores \nwere been disbursed to 30 States/Uts, which created the State Fund under the \npublic accounts of such States and reconciled the amount project-wise and \ncomponent-wise. This included a transfer of 5196.69/- to State Fund, Madhya \nPradesh on 29.08.2019.  The State/UTs wise information is attached at \nAnnexure-III. State wise accumulation of compensatory levies, its \nreconciliation and disbursement is a continuous process. In the year 2020-21, ₹ \n500.22 crore has been provided by the Government of Madhya Pradesh for \nimplementation activities as per their approved Annual Plans of Operation \n(APO). The details of the respective activities, on which the funds have been \nutilized in the year 2020-21 is attached at Annexure-IV. \n  \n(f) Yes, Sir. The Compensatory Afforestation Fund (CAF) Act, 2016 and CAF \nRules, 2018 have come into force with effect from 30.09.2019, which inter alia \nprovide the manner of utilization of compensatory afforestation fund.  \nAccordingly, the respective State CAMPAs submit their Annual Plans of \nOperations (APOs) after the approval of their State Steering Committees, \nwhich is finally approved by the Executive Committee in the National \nAuthority.  \n \n*** \n \n\n \nAnnexure-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 697 DUE FOR REPLY ON 05.02.2021 REGARDING “CROP \nDAMAGE BY WILD ANIMALS”. \nDetails of fund released to State Government/UTs under CSS-‘ Development of Wildlife Habitats \nduring the year 2014-19 \n(Rs. In lakhs) \nS. \nNo. \nName of  \nStates/UTs \n2014-15 2015-16 \n \n2016-17 \n \n2017-18  2018-19  \n \n1 A& N Islands 00 100.00 118.49 141.93486 191.00 \n2 Andhra Prades"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |698", "t": "Outbreak of COVID-19", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 698\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH February, 2021\nOUTBREAK OF COVID-19\n698. SHRI THIRUNAVUKKARASAR SU:\nSHRI Y.S. AVINASH REDDY:\nSHRI KANUMURU RAGHU RAMA KRISHNA RAJU:\nSHRI P.C. GADDIGOUDAR:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether a large number of people in the country have been traced, tested, treated, recovered\nand died with the outbreak of COVID -19 pandemic including mutant strain from United\nKingdom;\n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise;\n(c) the details of financial, medical and other assistance provided by the Government to the\nStates/ UTs in order to overcome this viral disease;\n(d) whether India has also rolled out new vaccine for COVID-19 pandemic;\n(e) if so, the details thereof and the plan of action initiated for its distribution to the States/UTs to\ncontrol the disease;\n(f) whether the Government has taken note th at new Strain of Corona Virus is spreading in the\ncountry, if so, the details thereof; and\n(g) the precautionary measures being taken by the Government to avoid its spread in the\ncountry?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY)\n(a) & (b): Total number of people tested and found positive in India as on 3 rd February 2021\nis 1,07,77,284. The State/UT -wise details of persons total cases tested positive, recovered and\ndied is at Annexure I.\n\nTill 28th January 2021, 136 travelers and their contacts have been tested positive for UK\nstrain in India. State-wise details are:\nAndhra Pradesh 1\nDelhi 27\nGoa 4\nGujarat 11\nHaryana 6\nKarnataka 14\nKerala 10\nLadakh (UT) 1\nMadhya Pradesh 2\nMaharashtra 11\nOdisha 3\nPunjab 4\nRajasthan 4\nTamil Nadu 9\nTelangana 7\nUttar Pradesh 17\nUttarakhand 1\nWest Bengal 1\nCase details awaited 3\n(c): Under the ‘India COVID19 Emergency Response and Health System Preparedness\npackage’ of Rs 15,000 crores has been approved by the Cabinet on 22 nd April 2020 with an\nobjective to prevent, detect and respond to the threat posed by COVID -19. As up to 15 th January\n2021, Rs 11, 361.67 Crores have been utilized for the same. Of this, Rs.6309.90 crore has been\nreleased to the States/UTs, through Agency - National Health Mission (NHM). The details are\ngiven at Annexure-II.\nIn addition, States are being supported in terms of supply of logistics. So far 1.69 crore of\nPPE Kits, 4.08 crores N-95 masks, 11.16 crore tablets of Hydroxychloroquine, 36,948 ventilators\nand 1,02,400 oxygen cylinders have been supplied to States/UTs/ Central Government hospitals\nso far (as reported on 2nd February 2021).  In addition, oxygen concentrators are also being\nsupplied to States.\n(d): Yes, COVID-19 vaccination has been launched in India on 16th January 2021.\n(e): The National Expert Group on Vaccine Administration for COVID -19 (NEGVAC) has\nbeen established, which provides guidance on all aspects of COVID -19 vaccination including\nprioritization of population groups, procurement and inventory management, vaccine selection,\nvaccine delivery and tracking mechanism etc.\n\nThe supply of COVID -19 vaccine is based on the number of beneficiaries registered\nunder priority group for vaccination in Co-WIN portal.\n(f) & (g): Yes, Government of India has taken note of mutant strain of SARS -CoV-2\nreported from United Kingdom.\nGovernment of India has taken a number of steps to prevent spread of mutant SARS -\nCoV-2 variant spread in the country:\ni. All flights to UK were banned till from December 23, 2020 to 7th January 2021.\nii. A surveillance system has been put in place to identify new UK variant of COVID 19\namong those with travel history to UK from 23 rd December 2020. States / UTs have been\nadvised to maintain strict surveillance of Whole Genome Sequencing of cases.\niii. Resumption of limited international flight services to/from UK has been allowed with\neffect from 8th January 2021 in a calibrated manner by initially allowing flight\nmovement to/from UK t o five international airports i.e. Delhi, Mumbai, Bengaluru,\nHyderabad and Chennai only.\niv. All the passengers arriving from U.K. should be carrying Negative RT -PCR Test Report\nfor which test should have been conducted within 72 hours prior to undertaking th e\njourney.\nv. All international passengers who arrived in India from UK or transit through UK are\nagain mandatorily tested for COVID-19 by RT-PCR at the airport.\nvi. All passengers and their contacts who tested positive for RT -PCR are isolated, their\nsamples are sent for whole genome sequencing to detect UK variant.\nvii. All international passengers who test negative at Airports are quarantined for 14 days.\nviii. Detailed guidelines are available at: https://www.mohfw.gov.in/pdf/Extensionofvalidity\nofStandardOperatingProcedureforEpidemiological\nSurveillance&ResponseforthenewvariantofSARSCoV2.pdf\n\nAnnexure I\nState/UT-wise details of persons total COVID-19 cases tested posit"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |699", "t": "AYUSH hospitals and dispensaries under NAM", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY,\nUNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY\n(AYUSH)\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO.699\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH February, 2021\nAYUSH HOSPITALS AND DISPENSARIES UNDER NAM\n699. DR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE:\nSHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE:\nDR. SUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL:\nSHRI UNMESH BHAIYYASAHEB PATIL:\nSHRI HEMANT SRIRAM PATIL:\nDR. HEENA GAVIT:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA,YOGA AND NATUROPATHY, UNANI,\nSIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of AYUSH hospitals and dispensaries upgraded during the last three years\nunder the National Ayush Mission (NAM) in the Country, State/UT-wise, Stream-wise;\n(b) the details of Primary Health Centres (PHCs), Community Health Centr es (CHCs) and\nDistrict Hospitals (DHs) that have been upgraded with AYUSH facilities, State/UT-wise;\n(c) the total number of seats offered to students at various AYUSH educational institutions\nunder NAM, since its inception;\n(d) the increase, if any, in the number of seats in the past three years; and\n(e) the estimated amount spent by the Government on NAM during the last three years?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND\nSPORTS AND ADDITIONAL CHARGE OF MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA,\nYOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY\n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU)\n(a): The number of AYUSH hospitals and dispensaries upgraded during the last three\nyears under the National AYUSH Mission (NAM) is furnished at Annexure-I.\n(b): The number of Primary Health Centres (PHCs), Community Health Centres (CHCs)\nand District Hospitals (DHs) upgraded with AYUSH facilities, State -wise under the National\nAYUSH Mission (NAM) is furnished at Annexure-II.\n(c) & (d): Under NAM, there is no provision to offer seats to students at various AYUSH\nEducational Institutions.\n(e): Under NAM, Grant -in-aid of Rs. 1440.47 Crore has been released to States/UTs\nduring the last three years for diffe rent activities as per proposal received through State\nAnnual Action Plan (SAAP).\n\nAnnexure-I\nThe number of AYUSH hospitals and dispensaries upgraded in the past three years under the\nNational AYUSH Mission (NAM)\nSl.\nNo.\nName of\nStates/U\nTs\nAYUSH Hospitals AYUSH Dispensaries\n2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 2017-18 2018-19 2019-20\nNo.\nof\nunits\nsupp\norted\nfor\nRecu\nrring\nGran\nts-in -\naid\