ISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n \n(a): In order to provide a universal social security system for all \nIndians, especially the poor, the under -privileged and the workers in \nthe unorganised sector, the Government of India had introduced the \nAtal Pension Yojana (APY), with effect from 1st June, 2015. \n \n(b): As on 31.12.2020, the total number of enrolments under APY in \nOdisha are 10,22,751 out of which 20,143 enrolments are in Kalahandi \ndistrict and 10,121 enrolments are in Nuapada district. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |959", "t": "Atmanirbhar Bharat Rojgar Yojana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 959 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON  08TH  FEBRUARY, 2021 \n \n ATMANIRBHAR BHARAT ROJGAR YOJANA \n \n959. SHRI PARBATBHAI SAVABHAI PATEL: \n        SHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state:   \n \n(a)  whether the Government has approved Atmanirbhar Bharat Rojgar \nYojana(ABRY); \n \n(b)  if so, the time by which this scheme is likely to be helpful in promoting \nemployment opportunities in India specially in Gujarat; and \n \n(c)  the expenditure (in crores) approved by the Government for the total period \nof scheme implementation i.e. from 2020 to 2023? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a) to (c): Aatmanirbhar Bharat Rozgar Yojana (ABRY) has been launched to \nincentivize  employers for creation of new employment along with social \nsecurity benefits and restoration of loss of employment during COVID -19 \npandemic. Under the scheme; \n An employee drawing mo nthly wage of less than Rs. 15000/ - who \nwas not working in any establishment registered with the Employees’ \nProvident Fund Organization (EPFO) before 1 st October, 2020 and did not \nhave a Universal Account Number or EPF Member account number prior \nto 1st October 2020 is eligible for the benefit.  \n Any EPF member possessing Universal Account Number (UAN) \ndrawing monthly wage of less than Rs. 15000/ - who made exit from \nemployment during Covid pandemic from 01.03.2020 to 30.09.2020 and \ndid not join employment in  any EPF covered establishment up to \n30.09.2020 is also eligible to avail benefit. \n \n\n \nThis scheme being implemented through the Employees’ Provident Fund \nOrganisation (EPFO), reduces the financial burden of the employers of various \nsectors/industries includ ing MSME and will encourages them to hire more \nworkers. Under ABRY, the Government of India is bearing both the employees’ \nshare (12% of wages) and employers’ share (12% of wages) of contribution \npayable or only the employees’ share, depending on employmen t strength of \nthe EPFO registered establishments. The scheme has commenced from 1 st \nOctober 2020 and shall remain open for registration of eligible employers and \nnew employees upto 30 th June 2021. Government will pay the subsidy for two \nyears from the date of registration.  \n \nThe outlay approved by the Government for the total period of scheme \nimplementation i.e. from 2020 to 2023 is Rs. 22810 Crore. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |96", "t": "State of Land Reforms Act in Kerla", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *96 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 8.2.2021 \n \nEXPERT COMMITTEE ON INDIAN CULTURE \n \n*96.   DR. KALANIDHI VEERASWAMY: \n SHRI RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU \n \nWill the Minister of Culture be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has set up an Expert Committee to conduct \nholistic study of the origin and evolution of Indian Culture since 12,000 years ago and \nits interface with other cultures of the world within the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with its composition; \n(c) whether the Committee has no representative from the Southern States including Tamil \nNadu which has a glorious past and is home to one of the oldest civilisations and if so, \nthe reasons therefor; \n(d) whether the Committee does not consist of any woman, person from \ndalit community or person from religious minority and if so, the reasons therefor; \n \n(e) whether the Government has received any proposal from Tamil Nadu to reconstitute \nthis Committee; and \n \n(f) if so, the details thereof along with the action taken by the  \nGovernment in this regard? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR CULTURE & TOURISM \n(Prahlad Singh Patel) \n \n(a) \nto \n(f) \nA statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY PART (a) TO (f) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *96 FOR 8.2.2021 \n \n \n(a) \n \n \nA Committee for Holistic Study of Origin and Evolution of Indian Culture since \n12,000 years before present and its interface with other Cultures of the World was \nset up in 2016. Currently, there exists no such committee to conduct holistic study \nof the origin and evolution of Indian Culture since 12,000 years.  \n \n(b) \n  \nDoes not arise. \n(c) & (d) Does not arise. \n \n(e) & (f) Yes, Sir. In this regard, inter-alia the following representations are received on the \nsubject: \n \n1. Shri Edappadi K. Palaniswami, Hon’ble Chief Minister, Tamil Nadu  to \nreconstitute the expert committee by inducting eminent scholars from Tamil \nNadu. \n2. Shri V. Narayanasamy, Hon’ble Chief Minister, Puducherry requested to \nreconstitute the expert panel having an expert from all the states and UTs. \n3. Shri Dr. Kalanidhi Veeraswamy, Hon’ble MP (Lok Sabha) requested to \nreconstitute the expert committee by inducting eminent scholars from Tamil \nNadu. \n \n         While reconstituting the Committee, due consideration would be given to all \nthese suggestions. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |960", "t": "Funds to Skill Development Training", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 960 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08.02.2021 \n  \nFUNDS TO SKILL DEVELOPMENT TRAINING \n \n† 960.  SHRI RAVI KISHAN:   SHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE: \nSHRI MANOJ TIWARI:   SHRI SUBRAT PATHAK: \nSHRI UPENDRA SINGH RAWAT: SHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO:  \nSHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR SAHU: SHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO M ANDLIK: \nSHRI SUDHEER GUPTA: \n  \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be pleased to \nstate: \n  \n(a) whether the Government has allocated funds for imparting of skill development training \nto the unemployed youth in the country; \n   \n(b)      if so, the details thereof during each of the last three years along with fund allotted to \nMaharashtra, Uttar Pradesh and Madhya Pradesh under  various schemes including number of \npersons benefitted in the State; \n   \n(c)        the details of budgetary allocation made  for skill development training during the last \nthree years, year/ State-wise; \n \n(d)   the total number of unemployed youth trained for skill development during the said \nperiod, State/UT-wise; and \n \n(e)     whether the Government has set any target f or achievement in imparting of skill \ndevelopment and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR THE MINISTRY OF \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R. K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (c) Under Skill India Mission, Ministry of S kill Development and Entrepreneurship \n(MSDE) is implementing Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas  Yojana (PMKVY), Scheme of Jan \nShikshan Sansthan (JSS) for Skill Development and N ational Apprenticeship \nPromotion Scheme (NAPS). \n \n  \nUnder PMKVY 2.0 (2016-20), since inception, funds a mounting to Rs. 7115 Cr. \n(approx.) have been sanctioned, till Jan’21, to imp lementing agencies for the implementation \nof the scheme. PMKVY 2.0 (2016-20) has two componen t namely Centrally Sponsored \nCentrally Managed (CSCM) and Centrally Sponsored St ate Managed (CSSM). There is a \nprovision of State-wise funds allocation under CSSM  component of the PMKVY 2.0 (2016-20). \nThe scheme of Jan Shikshan Sansthan (JSS) is being implemented through its institutional \nframework in the country with the objective to impr ove the occupational skills and technical \nknowledge of the non/neo literate. Further, governm ent launched the National Apprenticeship \n\nPromotion Scheme (NAPS) which makes special efforts  to reach maximum number of \nestablishments including MSMEs with an objective to  promote apprenticeship training and to \nincrease the engagement of apprentices. The State-w ise details of funds disbursed under \nCSSM-PMKVY 2.0 (2016-20), JSS and NAPS is given at column (a) of Annexure . \n  \n(d)       The State-wise number of candidates benefitted under PMKVY 2.0 (2016-20) (CSCM + \nCSSM), JSS and NAPS, is given at column (b) of Annexure.  \n  \n(e)       Under PMKVY 2.0 (2016-20), there is a tar get to train one crore youth of the country. \nAs on 19.01.2021, 1.07 Cr. candidates have been tra ined / oriented across the country. \nFurther, modified demand driven scheme PMKVY i.e. P MKVY 3.0 (2020-21) has been \nlaunched on 15.01.2021 with an objective to train 8 lakh candidates across the country.  \n \n  \n\nAnnexure \n \nThe State-wise details of funds disbursed and benef iciaries candidates under PMKVY \n2.0 (2016-20), JSS and NAPS is given below: \n \nStates/UTs \nFunds disbursed  \n(a) \nCandidates trained  \n(b) \nCSSM- PMKVY \n2.0 (till Jan’21) \n(In Rs.) \nNAPS  \n(2016-20 to \n20-21 till \nJan’21) \n(In Cr.) \nJSS  \n(2018-19 to \n20-21 till \nAug’20) \n(In Rs.) \nCSCM and \nCSSM - \nPMKVY 2.0 \nApprentice \nCount \nJSS \n(2018-19 \nto 20-21 \n(jan21) \nAndaman &  Nicobar \nIslands 2,10,94,164 \n 0  2,462  0  \nAndhra Pradesh 54,92,66,464  11.532  6,27,80,766  2,88,370  33,324  16,683  \nArunachal Pradesh 14,42,49,768  0 65,25,000  67,839  0 726  \nAssam 36,95,32,800  0 5,39,22,411  6,21,930  7,664  13,659  \nBihar 36,81,62,449  0.157  11,44,19,724  4,52,919  4,201  35,701  \nChandigarh 6,15,88,800  0.104  90,15,773  18,197  1,407  2,694  \nChhattisgarh 35,57,76,000  0.071  6,46,22,934  1,10,433  12,303  19,567  \nDelhi 15,39,72,000  0.43  2,92,69,765  3,58,277  15,541  8,225  \nGoa 10,70,25,937  0.022  1,06,33,720  6,845  1,932  2,874  \nGujarat 46,94,93,826  30.912  10,23,75,336  3,28,356  1,98,675  27,786  \nHaryana 36,56,99,375  15.479  4,93,85,867  5,23,106  1,06,092  13,745  \nHimachal Pradesh 21,55,60,800  0.24  59,25,000  1,06,608  11,737  3,124  \nJammu And Kashmir 22,94,18,280  0.1  1,61,47,336  2,54,382  2,243  5,047  \nJharkhand 29,59,64,978  1.744  2,43,85,697  2,62,045  26,688  7,992  \nKarnataka 21,43,95,135  4.082  8,33,29,296  4,13,738  65,479  26,812  \nKerala 22,00,25,988  1.298  8,78,78,194  2,26,789  23,716  23,387  \nLadakh 0  0 0 2,267  0 0 \nLakshadweep 1,23,17,760  0 0 150  0 0 \nMadhya Pradesh 27,32,80,576  4.734  27,38,01,656  6,51,665  33,297  78,951  \nMaharashtra 85,77,62,615  19.768  20,52,82,890  9,88,847  2,19,201  59,878  \nManipur 32,59,88,939  0 3,"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |961", "t": "National Child Labour Project", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 961 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08.02.2021 \n \nNATIONAL CHILD LABOUR PROJECT \n \n961.  SHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY:  \nSHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE: \n  SHRI ARJUN LAL MEENA: \n \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)the number of Project Institutions which are provided support under \nthe National Child Labour Project (NCLP) in the State of Rajatshan, \nincluding Pali and Uttar Pradesh; \n(b)the details of support including fin ancial support provided to the \nInstitutions ; \n(c)whether the Government has an outstanding payment towards the \nProjects Institutions working in National Child Labour Project as of \nnow; and  \n(d)if so, the time by which people of Rajasthan can expect the release of \nthe funds along with the reason for delay? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a) & (b): The details of District Project Society    which were provided \nsupport under NCLP Scheme in the State of Rajasthan and Uttar Pradesh \nis   annexed.  \n \n(c) & (d): The outstanding payment of the District Project Society for \nrunning Special Training Centers is calculated on t he basis of  Annual \nProgress Report, Quarterly Progress Report, receipt of Utilization \nCertificate etc. of the Financial Year. Only on receipt of the reports, the \nactual outstanding payment figures are calculated and payment effected. \nNo such case with all  requisite documents is pending for payment in the \nStates of Rajasthan and Uttar Pradesh. \n*                                                   ******* \n\nANNEXURE- I \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) &  (b) of Lok Sabha Un -Starred Question No. 961 for \n08.02.2021 by SHRI P.P. Chaudhary, Shri Kaushal Kishore & Shri Arjun Lal Meena \nregarding National Child Labour Project. \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of State No. of Project \nSociety \noperational \nduring 2019-20 \nNo. of Project \nSociety \noperational \nduring 2020-21 \nAmount \nreleased during \n2019-20  \nAmount released \nduring 2020-21 \n1.  Rajasthan 5 4 28140363 6733328 \n2.  Uttar Pradesh 10 3 66739086 NIL \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |962", "t": "Setting up of Migration Commission", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 962 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08.02.2021 \n \nSETTING UP OF MIGRATION COMMISSION \n \n962.  SHRI M. SELVARAJ: \nSHRI MOHAMMED FAIZAL P.P.:  \nSHRI ANTO ANTONY: \nSHRI SAPTAGIRI SANKAR ULAKA: \n \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)whether the Government is considering setting up a Migration Commission for \nmigrant workers‘ employment; and \n(b)if so, the details thereof, including the time by which the propos ed Commission \nwill be established including its composition? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a) & (b): NITI Aayog has constituted a sub-group to prepare a National Action Plan \nfor Migrant Workers. The sub -group comprises the members from various Ministries \nof Govt. of India, subject experts, NGOs and civil society organisations, to prepare a \ntangible action plan to address issues related to migrant workers. \n \n        In order to safeguard the interest of the migrant workers, the Central \nGovernment had enacted the Inter -state Migrant Workmen (Regulation of \nEmployment and Conditions of Service) Act, 1979. This Act has no w been subsumed \nin the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 and the Code \nhas been notified on 29 .09.2020.  The above mentioned code, commonly known as \nOSH Code, provides for decent working conditions, minimum wages, grievances \nredressal mechanisms, protection from abuse and exploitation, enhancement of the \nskills and social security to all category of organised and unorganised workers \nincluding Migrant workers.  \n         \nThe Code is applicable to every establishment  in which 10 or more inter-\nstate migrant workers are employed or were employed on any day of the preceding \n12 months. \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |963", "t": "Infrastructure Facilities for Primary Schools", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION\nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 963\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08TH FEBRUARY, 2021\nInfrastructure Facilities for Primary Schools\n†963. SHRI VIJAY KUMAR:\nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of primary schools in the country having no permanent building and\nfacility of water and toilets, State-wise;\n(b) whether the number of primary schools in the country is adequate in proportion\nto its population and if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) if not, the action plan formulated/ proposed to be formulated by the Government\nin this regard?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF EDUCATION\n(SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL 'NISHANK')\n(a) State/UT-wise number of Government primary schools/ Government schools with\nprimary sections in the country without drinking water facility, toilets and buildings is at\nAnnexure.\n(b) and (c) The Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education (RTE) Act, 2009,\nmandates the appropriate Government to provide free and compulsory elementary\neducation to every child of the age 6 to 14 years in a neighbourhood school. In\npursuance of Section 6 of the Act, the Central RTE Rules has notified the area or limits\nof neighbourhood as one kilometre, within which a primary school has to be established.\nThe States have notified area or limits of their neighbourhood norms in their state RTE\nRules, factoring in their State specific condition, for opening of schools.\nAs reported by States/UTs in their respective Annual Work Plan and Budget,\n2020-21, 97.49% of habitations in the country are covered by primary schools. The\n\nhabitations that remain uncovered are mostly small or sparsely populated in difficult\nareas where opening of school is not feasible.\nThe Department of School Education and Literacy has launched an Integrated\nCentrally Sponsored Scheme for School Education – Samagra Shiksha w.e.f. 2018-19,\nwhich subsumes the three erstwhile Schemes of Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA),\nRashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan (RMSA) and Teacher Education (TE). Under\nthe erstwhile scheme of SSA and Samagra Shiksha, 2.04 lakh primary schools have\nbeen approved for ensuring universal availability of schooling facilities. For the\nremaining habitations, there is a provision of giving transport and escort facility, and\nopening of residential schools and hostels under the Samagra Shiksha scheme.\n*****\n\nANNEXURE\nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED\nQUESTION NO. 963 FOR 08.02.2021 ASKED BY HON’BLE MP SHRI VIJAY KUMAR\nREGARDING INFRASTRUCTURE FACILITIES FOR PRIMARY SCHOOLS\nStatement showing State/UT-wise number of Government primary schools/\nGovernment schools with primary sections without drinking water facility, toilets and\nbuildings\nS.\nNo.\nState/UT Number of primary\nschools/schools\nwith primary\nsections without\nDrinking water\nfacility\nNumber of primary\nschools/schools with\nprimary sections\nwithout Toilet\nNumber of primary\nschools/schools with\nprimary sections\nwithout Building\n1 Andaman & Nicobar\nIslands 1 0 4\n2 Andhra Pradesh 6575 865 610\n3 Arunachal Pradesh 1327 621 15\n4 Assam 2427 805 664\n5 Bihar 261 376 2065\n6 Chandigarh 0 0 2\n7 Chhattisgarh 299 300 372\n8\nDadra & Nagar\nHaveli 0 0 1\n9 Daman & Diu 0 0 0\n10 Delhi 0 0 28\n11 Goa 0 0 24\n12 Gujarat 6 47 431\n13 Haryana 30 39 64\n14 Himachal Pradesh 11 83 54\n15 Jammu & Kashmir 1167 1053 2529\n16 Jharkhand 1017 202 147\n17 Karnataka 595 712 420\n18 Kerala 50 37 146\n19 Lakshadweep 0 0 4\n20 Madhya Pradesh 2712 1021 991\n21 Maharashtra 1238 741 1356\n22 Manipur 398 323 60\n23 Meghalaya 3642 680 555\n24 Mizoram 106 70 5\n25 Nagaland 713 127 3\n26 Odisha 0 0 226\n27 Puducherry 1 0 4\n28 Punjab 18 8 118\n29 Rajasthan 4694 1458 1674\n30 Sikkim 2 2 15\n31 Tamil Nadu 1749 5 402\n32 Telangana 2341 685 749\n33 Tripura 910 128 13\n34 Uttar Pradesh 2702 1708 1756\n35 Uttarakhand 904 171 142\n36 West Bengal 634 48 1889\nTotal 36530 12315 17538\nSource: UDISE+ 2018-19 (Provisional)"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |964", "t": "Setting up of National Committee to Commemorate Birth Anniversary of Netaji", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.964  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 8TH FEBRUARY, 2021 \n \n SETTING UP OF NATIONAL COMMITTEE TO COMMEMORATE  \nBIRTH ANNIVERSARY OF NETAJI \n \n964. SHRI NISITH PRAMANIK: \n SHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR: \n SHRI RAJA AMRESHWARA NAIK: \n SHRIMATI SANGEETA KUMARI SINGH DEO: \n DR. JAYANTA KUMAR ROY: \n SHRI BHOLA SINGH: \n DR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR: \nWILL the MINISTER OF CULTURE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) Whether the Government has set up a National Committee to \n commemorate the 125th birth anniversary of Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose ; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) Whether the National Implementation Committee has approved proposals  for \n programmes/projects; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof including funds sanctioned for \n programmes/projects;  and  \n \n(e) the other steps taken/being taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMinister of State (I/C) for Culture & Tourism \n(Shri Prahlad Singh Patel) \n \n(a) Yes Sir, Government has set up a High Level Committee to commemorate the \n125th birth anniversary of Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose. \n \n(b) Government has set up a 85 member High Level Committee under the \nChairmanship of Hon’ble Prime Minister for commemorating the 125th birth anniversary \nof Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose in befitting manner at national and international level. \nA copy of Gazette Notification issued on 8 January 2021 is enclosed. \n  \n(c) ; (d) and (e):   In the very first meeting of National Implementation Committee \nheld on 25 November, 2020, a presentation was made to the NIC regarding the themes \nand activities proposed on 75 years of independence by the Committee of Secretaries \nconstituted for this purpose. Commemoration of 125th anniversary of Netaji was part of \nthe proposed commemorations. NIC asked Ministries to dedicate/launch projects during \ncommemoration and to communicate their action plans. Meanwhile, a preparatory \nmeeting under the Chairmanship of Hon’ble Culture Minister was held on 15th \nDecember 2020 to discuss modalities for celebration of 125th birth anniversary of Netaji \nSubhash Chandra Bose beginning from 23rd January, 2021. The decisions/ suggestions \ngiven during preparatory meeting are to be placed before the NIC for consideration as \nand when next meeting of NIC takes place. In the meantime, the following projects and \nprograms were organized by Ministry of Culture on 23rd January, 2021, to launch the \ncommemoration (Annexure).” \n\n \n \n \nANNEXURE \nActivities undertaken at the inaugural event of commemoration of 125th birth \nanniversary of Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose on 23rd January, 2021 at Kolkata: \n \n1. (A) Victoria Memorial Hall & Museum: \n Cultural program (based on INA songs/songs associated with Netaji) \n Exhibition on Netaji \n Projection mapping (Sound & Light show on Netaji) \n Book release \n Release of commemorative stamps \n(B) Belvedere (National Library) \n International conference on Netaji \n 4 day artists’camp \n2.  Cultural program at Cuttack, (Netaji’s birth place) on 23rd January, 2021 by South \nZone Cultural Centre  \n \n3.  Exhibition on Nand Lal Bose’s iconic “Haripura Panels”at Haripura near Surat on \n23rd January, 2021 (where Netaji was first nominated as Congress president). \n \n4.  Declaration of 23rd January as Parakram Diwas. Gazette Notification for this \npublished on 19th January 2021.  \n \n \n\n \nREGD.No.D.L.330004/99 \nThe Gazette of India \nCG-DL-E-09012021-224300 \n \nEXTRAODINAY \nPART-1- Section-1 \n \nPUBLISHED BY AUTHORITY \n \nNo.9] NEW DELHI, FRIDAY, JANUARY8, 2021/PAUSHA 18,1942 \n \nMinistry of Culture \n(Special Cell) \n \nNotification \n \nNew Delhi the 8th January, 2021 \nF.No. 21037/1/2020 -Special Cell: To commemorate the 125th Birth Anniversary of \nNetaji Subhas  Chandra Bose, the Competent Authority has approved  the constitution \nof a  High Level Committee. The composition of the High Level Committee is as under:- \n  \nS. No. Name Designation \n  \nChairman \n  \n1.   Shri Narendra Modi  \n  \n Prime Minister of India \nMembers \n   \n2.   Shri Amit Shah  Minister of Home Affairs \n3.   Shri Rajnath  Singh   Minister of Defence \n4.   Ms. Nirmala Sitharaman  Minister  of Finance \n  \n5.   Shri Narendra Singh Tomar   Minister of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare,   \nRural Development, Panchayati Raj, Food  \nProcessing Industries  \n  \n6.   Ms. Smriti  Irani  Minister of Woman and Child Development  \nand Textiles \n7.   Shri Prakash Javadekar  Minister of Information & Broadcasting,  \nEnvironment, Forests and Climate Change,  \nHeavy Industries and Public Enterprises \n8.   Shri Jitendra Singh MoS(IC) DONER, Prime Minister’s Office \n9.   Shri Prahlad Singh Patel Minister of State(IC)- Culture & Tourism \n10.   Shri Babul Supriyo Minister of State- Environment, Forest and \n Climate Change \n11.   Ms. Debasree Chaudhuri Minister of State – Woman and Child  \nDevelopment \n12.   Shri H D Deve Gowda Former Prime Minister of India \n13.   Shri Manmohan Singh Former Prime Minister of India \n14.   Shri Om "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |965", "t": "Skill Development in Haryana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 965 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08.02.2021 \n  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT IN HARYANA \n \n†965.   SHRI DHARAMBIR SINGH: \n  \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state: \n  \n(a)  whether the Government has assessed that the t rainers taking training from \nSkill Development Centres are getting employment according to their skill expertise; \n \n(b)   if so, the details thereof and the number of trainers getting employment \nduring the last three years in Haryana district-wise; \n \n(c)  the number of Skill Development Centres set up  in the Country including \nHaryana; \n \n(d)  whether there is any proposal to set up new Sk ill development centres; and \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof?   \n      \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R. K. SINGH) \n \n(a) and (b)      Under short term skilling eco-syst em, trainers are being trained \nthrough Training of Trainers (ToT) programme which is being conducted by Sector \nSkill Councils (SSCs) and facilitated through Natio nal Skill Development Corporation \n(NSDC). Under ToT, 50,689 trainers have been certif ied across 37 sectors. ToT \nprogramme is for the development of professionals, who are already associated / \nemployed with training providers. The state-wise nu mber of trainers certified under \nTraining of Trainers (ToT) Programme is given at Annexure . \n  \n(c) to (e)             Till date, 6 Trainer and Ass essor academies have been set up as \ntrainer development centre  up for conducting ToT p rogrammes; one each in Andhra \nPradesh, Haryana, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu, Telangan a and Uttar Pradesh. The \nNational Skill Development Corporation (NSDC) in co nsultation with Sector Skill \nCouncil (SSC) is facilitating the setting up of mor e such academics by private \nplayers. \n  \n \n \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure  \n  \nThe state-wise number of trainers certified under T raining of Trainers (ToT) \nProgramme is given below:  \n  \nS. No.  State/UT  Trainer Certification  \n1.   Andaman And Nicobar Islands 7 \n2.   Andhra Pradesh 1805 \n3.   Arunachal Pradesh 145 \n4.   Assam 2902 \n5.   Bihar 2809 \n6.   Chandigarh 99 \n7.   Chhattisgarh 889 \n8.   Dadra And Nagar Haveli 9 \n9.   Daman And Diu 8 \n10.   Delhi 1685 \n11.   Goa 51 \n12.   Gujarat 1025 \n13.   Haryana 2128 \n14.   Himachal Pradesh 753 \n15.   Jammu And Kashmir 708 \n16.   Jharkhand 2199 \n17.   Karnataka 1406 \n18.   Kerala 2535 \n19.   Lakshadweep 8 \n20.   Madhya Pradesh 4294 \n21.   Maharashtra 4589 \n22.   Manipur 303 \n23.   Meghalaya 105 \n24.   Mizoram 122 \n25.   Nagaland 104 \n26.   Odisha 1867 \n27.   Puducherry 136 \n28.   Punjab 1724 \n29.   Rajasthan 2317 \n30.   Sikkim 45 \n31.   Tamil Nadu 3174 \n32.   Telangana 1373 \n33.   Tripura 459 \n34.   Uttar Pradesh 5379 \n35.   Uttarakhand 714 \n36.   West Bengal 2813 \nTotal  50689  \n  \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |966", "t": "Skill Development Centres", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 966 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08.02.2021 \n \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT CENTRES \n \n966.  DR. SUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL: \nSHRI ANUMULA REVANTH REDDY: \nSHRI UNMESH BHAIYYASAHEB PATIL: \nSHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE: \nDR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE: \nSHRI HEMANT SRIRAM PATIL: \nDR. HEENA GAVIT: \n \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state: \n  \n(a)  the details of operational Skill Development C entres opened in the country during \nthe last three years, State/ UT-wise; \n \n(b)  the details of persons who have been provided employment by these Centres, \nState/UT-wise; \n \n(c)  the details of the persons who have been provi ded certification by these Centres in \nMaharashtra, district wise data; \n \n(d)  the details of the efforts made to enhance emp loyment opportunities for those who \nhave not received certification; \n \n(e)  whether the persons who undertook training hav e been placed in alignment with \nthe target set by the Government and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(f)  if not, the corrective steps taken by the Gove rnment to improve the placement \nscenario?      \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR THE \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R. K. SINGH) \n \n(a)       PMKVY does not mandate the establishment of training centres. Under PMKVY \n(2016-20) training is being imparted in empanelled training centres (TCs). As on \n19.01.2021, 15,124 training centres have been empan elled and presently 1,595 TCs are \noperational across the country. The state-wise no. of empanelled and operational TCs \nincluding Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Kendra (PMKK) is given at column (a) of Annexure I .\n \n \n(c)        The district-wise details of the certifi ed candidates in State of Maharashtra is give \nat Annexure II . \n \n(b), (d) to (f)    Under placement linked Short Ter m Training (STT) component of PMKVY \n(2016-20), as on 19.01.2021, 36.04 lakh candidates have been certified; out of which, \n18.95 lakh candidates have been placed. The State-w ise number of placed candidates \nunder PMKVY (2016-20) is given at column (b) of Annexure I . \n  \n            Under PMKVY 2.0 (2016-20), placement op portunities are being provided to \nsuccessfully certified candidates. There is a provi sion of one-time re-assessment for the \ncandidates who have either failed or did not appear for assessment.  \n  \n\nFurther, under PMKVY, various initiatives are taken  to enhance placement of the \ncertified candidates such as organization of Rozgar  Melas with the support of Sector Skill \nCouncils; placement linked release of last tranche funds to the Training Centres \n(TCs); requirement to have dedicated mentorship-cum -placement cells at TCs / Training \nProviders (TPs) for industry linkage and placement of candidates; incentive to TCs @ Rs. \n3000 per trainee if he or she is retained in employ ment for 12 months; post placement \nsupport of Rs. 1,500 per month per trainee for spec ial areas/special groups;  one time \nplacement travel cost upto maximum of Rs. 4,500 per  candidate; implementation of \nemployer-led training model; reallocation of target  to TCs based on placement \nperformance;   etc.  \n \n \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure I  \n  \nThe state-wise no. of empanelled and operational TC s including Pradhan Mantri \nKaushal Kendra (PMKK) and candidates placed under P MKVY 2.0, as on 19.01.2021, \nis given below: \n \nS. No. STATE \nTotal \nempanelled \nTCs \nOperational \nTCs Candidates Placed \n(a)  (b)  \n1.  Andaman And Nicobar \nIslands 12 4 124 \n \n2.  Andhra Pradesh 530 46 82,213  \n3.  Arunachal Pradesh 103 30 4,390  \n4.  Assam 396 103 41,238  \n5.  Bihar 485 77 91,237  \n6.  Chandigarh 55 6 4,868  \n7.  Chhattisgarh 172 1 25,064  \n8.  Delhi 383 45 66,391  \n9.  Goa 25 3 835  \n10.  Gujarat 423 55 59,405  \n11.  Haryana 1,080 59 1,43,439  \n12.  Himachal Pradesh 323 18 21,498  \n13.  Jammu And Kashmir 378 32 48,050  \n14.  Jharkhand 224 18 24,151  \n15.  Karnataka 279 29 51,620  \n16.  Kerala 328 99 20,229  \n17.  Ladakh 8 0 943  \n18.  Lakshadweep 1 0 0  \n19.  Madhya Pradesh 1,116 108 1,77,086  \n20.  Maharashtra 889 180 57,985  \n21.  Manipur 131 30 11,017  \n22.  Meghalaya 77 30 5,928  \n23.  Mizoram 78 5 6,704  \n24.  Nagaland 72 31 4,199  \n25.  Odisha 391 24 54,125  \n26.  Puducherry 50 12 8,177  \n27.  Punjab 811 98 98,340  \n28.  Rajasthan 1,406 66 1,51,113  \n29.  Sikkim 35 3 1,719  \n30.  Tamil Nadu 1,081 30 1,19,874  \n31.  Telangana 485 29 83,526  \n32.  The Dadra & Nagar \nHaveli And Daman & Diu \n 18 0 2,378  \n33.  Tripura 174 25 9,111  \n34.  Uttar Pradesh 2,265 190 2,83,205  \n35.  Uttarakhand 377 16 41,617  \n36.  West Bengal 463 93 93,408  \nTotal 15,124 1,595 18,95,207  \n \n  \n\nAnnexure II \n \nThe district-wise details of the certified candidat es in State of Maharashtra, under \nPMKVY 2.0 (2016-20), is give below: \n \nS. No. District \nShort Term Training  Recognition of \nPrior Learning \n(RPL) Certified \nTotal \nCertified Empanelled \nTCs \nCerti"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |967", "t": "Tourism Development Projects for Karnataka", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.967 \nANSWERED ON 08.02.2021 \n \nTOURISM DEVELOPMENT PROJECTS FOR KARNATAKA \n \n967.  SHRI Y. DEVENDRAPPA:  \nDR. UMESH G. JADHAV:  \nSHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the details of the tourism development projects sanctioned for \nKarnataka and Northern Karnataka during the last three years and \ntill date and the funds sanctioned and released for each of these \nprojects, district-wise;  \n(b)  the number and details of the proposals received from the State \nGovernments for tourism development indicating the status of each \nproject and reasons for delay, if any, in sanctioning these proposals; \nand  \n(c)  the time by which the pending proposals of the State are  likely to be \ngiven clearance? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) to (c): The Ministry of Tourism under its schemes of Swadesh Darshan \nand National Mission on Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual, He ritage \nAugmentation Drive (PRASHAD) provides financial assistance to State \nGovernments/Union Territory (UT) Administrations/Central Agencies for \ndevelopment of tourism infrastructure in the country.   The projects are \nsanctioned subject to availability of f unds, submission of suitable Detailed \nProject Reports, adherence to scheme guidelines and utilization of funds \nreleased earlier etc. \n \nSanctioning of projects under these schemes is a continuous process \nhowever sanctioning of new projects under Swadesh Dars han scheme is \ncurrently under  review.  No project has been sanctioned to the state of \nKarnataka. However, ‘Chamundeshwari Devi’ (Mysuru District, Karnataka) \nis one of the identified site for development under the PRASHAD scheme of \nthe Ministry. \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |968", "t": "Demand for Steel", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF STEEL \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.968 \nFOR ANSWER ON 08/02/2021 \n \nDEMAND FOR STEEL \n \n968. SHRI PASUNOORI DAYAKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the demand for steel in the country is  likely to increase in 2021, under \nthe Government’s efforts  of improving infrastructure, coupled with the ‘Atma  Nirbhar \nBharat’ policy and the Production Linked  Incentive Scheme is bound to further boost the \nmoney inflow into the system; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and progress made in this regard; \n \n(c) the situation of pre and post COVID-19 estimated for steel demand and supply? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STEEL            (SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n (a)&(b): Yes , Sir. The announcements in Budget for the Financial Year , 2021-22 viz. \nCapex allotment of Rs. 1,07,100 crore for Railways, 2.87 lakh crore outlay for Jal Jeevan \nMission, Construction of roads under Bharatmala Pariyojana project, Swachh Bharat \nMission 2.0, Scheme for Mega Textile Parks etc. will lead to increased demand for steel \nin the country. The Vision for Atmanirbhar Bharat and the Production Linked Incentive \n(PLI) Scheme will also   help attract capital investments and thereby enhance demand \nand production of  steel in the country. The Government has also approved inclusion of \n'Speciality Steel' under the PLI Scheme. \n \n(c) The production and consumption of total finished steel (Non -alloy, alloy/stainless) \nin India during the period from April to June,  2020  had declined substantially as \ncompared to the corresponding period last year.  However, the production and \nconsumption have shown sharp recovery subsequently and substantial increase in \nrecent months. In December, 2020, production and consumption were 9.32 MT and 9.68 \nMT, showing growth of 1.08% and 11.91% on month -on-month basis  respectively \ncompared to the corresponding period last year. The details of month wise production \nand consumption during April, 2019-20 and 2020-21 are given below:- \n \nMonth Production (MT) % Change \n(MoM basis) \nConsumption (MT) % Change \n(MoM basis)   2019-20 2020-21* 2019-20 2020-21 \nApril 8.67 1.57 - 81.89 7.33 1.09 -85.13 \nMay 9.04 5.52 - 38.94 8.85 4.79 -45.88 \nJune 8.83 6.94 -21.40 8.59 6.35 -26.08 \nJuly 8.61 8.13 -5.57 8.57 7.69 -10.27 \nAugust 8.36 8.56 2.39 9.19 8.16 -11.21 \nSeptember 8.11 8.52 5.06 8.45 8.45 0 \nOctober 8.32 9.08 9.13 8.83 9.33 5.66 \nNovember 8.57 8.90 3.85 7.77 9.08 16.86 \nDecember 9.22 9.32 1.08 8.65 9.68 11.91 \nJanuary 9.11 - - 9.17 - - \nFebruary 8.57 - - 8.55 - - \nMarch 7.20 - - 6.21 - - \nSource: JPC, *provisional data for 2020-21(September, 20-December, 20) \n \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |969", "t": "Education Cess", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO 969 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY THE 08TH FEBRUARY, 2021 \nMAGHA 19, 1942 (SAKA) \n \nEDUCATION CESS \n969. DR. A. CHALLAKUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:- \n \n(a) the details of education cess levied, modus operandi of its allocation and \nthe amount collected on income tax during the last three years across the \ncountry;  \n(b) whether the Government has utilized the education cess collected so f ar \nand if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c) the details of the schemes/programmes identified so far for utilization of \neducation cess; \n(d) whether the Government has conducted any study for utilisation of \neducation cess before imposing it on income tax; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor along with the \nother steps taken/being taken by the Government for effective use of the \ncorpus collected by the Government through education cess?  \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n \n(a) (i) Initially, education cess was levied @ 2% from Financial Year 2004 -\n05 to make available additional resources for basic education and Mid -Day \nMeal scheme. Subsequently, from Financial Year 2007 -08, an additional cess \n@1% was levied to fund secondary and  higher education and the expansion \nof capacity by 54% for reservation for socially and educationally backward \nclasses. Further, to take care of the needs of education and health of Below \nPoverty Line (BPL) and rural families, ‘Education Cess’ @3% was replaced by \n‘Health and Education Cess’ @4% from Financial Year 2018-19. \n \n\n \n (ii) The allocation of the Cess is as under:  \n \n The total proceeds of Health and Education Cess are distributed as \nunder:- \n    \n  Primary & Secondary Education  -   60%  \n  Higher Education                         -    15%  \n  Health                                          -    25%  \n \n(iii) Details of Education Cess/ Health and Education Cess collected on \nIncome Tax during the last three  years across the country are as \nunder: \n                                                                                          (In Rs. Crores) \nS.No. Financial Year Total Amount of Education Cess \ncollected  \n1 2017-18 28,174.09* \n2 2018-19   40,590.57** \n3 2019-20   38,430.21** \n \n* Education cess includes Primary Educat ion cess and Secondary \n& Higher Education cess \n** From F.Y. 2018 -19 Primary and Secondary Education Cess  has \nbeen changed as Health and Education cess \n \n \n(b) Details of utilization of education cess in last three financial years are as \nunder: \n                                                                                     (In Rs. crores) \nFinancial \nYear \nDepartment of \nHigher Education \nDepartment of \nSchool \nEducation \nTotal \n2017-18 0 19,088.01 19,088.01 \n2018-19 7,935.72 28,506.54 36,442.26 \n2019-20 9,088.75 30,656.81 39,745.56 \n \n (c) The details of the schemes/programmes identified so far for utilization of \neducation cess are as under: \n1. Schemes implemented by Department of Higher Education:- \n(i) Rashtriya Uchhatar Shiksha Abhiyan (RUSA) \n(ii) Interest Subsidy and contribution for Guarantee Funds \n(iii) Scholarship for College and University students \n(iv) Pandit Madan Mohan Malviya National Mi ssion on Teachers \nand  Teaching \n(v) Support to Indian Institutes of Technology \n(vi)  University Grants Commission (UGC)  \n\n(vii) All India Council for Technical Education (AICTE)  \n(viii)Grants to Central Universities (CUs), and  \n(ix) Support to National Institutes of Technology. \n  \n2. Schemes implemented by Department of School Education:- \n(i)  Mid Day Meal Scheme \n(ii)  Samagra Shiksha \n(iii)  Scholarships, and \n(iv)  Incentives  \n(d)  and (e) The work related to levy of direct taxes and cess on direct taxes is \npart of continuous process of study of various factor s of economy undertaken \nin course  of legislative and budgetary exercise . Proposal regarding levy of \neducation cess was part of Finance (No.2) Bill,  2004 which was enacted as \nFinance (No.2) Act, 2004 after debate and discussion in the Parliament. The \nbudget exercise is carried year after year and proposals of allocation of \neducation cess for expenditure on education and health are put before the \nParliament for effective utilization of amount collected from the cess. \n****** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |97", "t": "Changes in PMFBY", "head": "O.I.H\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n97\n                     \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n2\nND \nFEBRUARY\n,\n \n20\n21\n \n \nCHANGES IN PMFBY\n \n \n97.\n \nSHRI\n \nASHOK KUMAR RAWAT:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether major changes have been enacted by\n \nremoving discrepancies in the old fasal \nbima yojana\n \nbeing replaced by Pradhan \nMantri Fasal Bima Yojana\n \n(PMFBY)so that the farmers \nc\nan \navail maximum benefits\n \nand if so, the details thereof; and\n \n \n(b)\n \nwhether the premium money being paid by the\n \nfarmers under the Pradhan Mantri Fasal \nBima Yojana has\n \nbeen reduced considerably and capping\n \non payment\n \npremium has also been \nremoved and if so, the details\n \nthereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ी\n  \n   \n       \n( SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n  \n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \nThe revisions/improvements in the crop \ninsurance schemes is a continuous \nprocess and decisions on suggstions/representations are taken from time to time after \nconsultation with various stakeholders.    Accordingly, Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana \n(PMFBY) has been introduced from Kharif 2016 to\n \nprovide a simple and affordable crop \ninsurance product to ensure comprehensive risk cover for crops of farmers against all non\n-\npreventable natural risks from pre\n-\nsowing to post\n-\nharvest, to provide adequate claim amount \nand timely settlement of claims.   B\nased on the discussions with stakeholders, Government has \nintroduced various changes over the erstwhile crop insurance schemes.  Major changes made \ninteralia include very low premium rates i.e. maximum 1.5% for Rabi, 2% for Kharif food and \noilseed crops an\nd 5% for commercial/horticultural crops and remaining part of the \nactuarial/bidded/premium is paid by the Government; removal of capping on premium, equating \nof sum insured with scale of finance/notional average yield to provide maximum risk coverage \nand a\ndequate claim amount etc. to increase the benefit under PMFBY to farmers.\n \n \n \nBased on the experience of implementation of PMFBY and views of stakeholders, the \nscheme and its Operational Guidelines were further revised/revamped twice  w.e.f. Rabi 2018\n-\n19 \nseason and Kha\nrif 2020 season respectively. \nMajor features of revamped PMFBY are \nannexed\n.   \n   \n \n \n\n \nAnenxure\n \n \nChanges/Improvments made in the provisions/parameters of PMFBY w.e.f. Kharif 2020 season\n \n \n\n \nTo address the demand of farmers, the scheme has been made\n \nvoluntary for all farmers. \nHowever, there is no change in farmers’ share of premium.\n \n \n\n \nThe premium subsidy sharing pattern between Centre and North Eastern States has been \nchanged from 50 : 50 to 90:10.  This has been done to allow more States to notify th\ne scheme \nand existing States to notify more crops and areas to facilitate greater coverage of farmers under \nthe scheme.  For remaining States, subsidy sharing pattern will continue as 50 : 50. \n \n \n\n \nTo address the issue of high premium rate for few crops/areas\n \ndue to adverse selection, the \nrequisite central share of premium subsidy (90 : 10 for North Eastern States and 50 : 50 for \nremaining States) will be provided for areas/crops upto gross premium rate of 25% for irrigated \nand 30% for un\n-\nirrigated areas/crops\n. Besides, alternate risk mitigation measures to be explored \nfor these areas/crops.\n \n \n\n \nInsurance companies will now be selected by the States for 3 years in a go instead of one year \nthereby increasing their commitment and accountability to the farmers.\n \n \n\n \nOpti\non has been given to the States to choose the notional value of average yield or the Scale of \nFinance as sum insured in the interest of the farming community.\n \n \n\n \nIn view of the demand of many states, option has been given to states to choose additional risk \ncovers besides shortfall in yield\n-\nbased cover depending upon the local weather challenges and \nrequirements of the farmers.\n \n \n\n \nThe states delaying the release of subsidy beyond stipulated timelines can not participate in \nupcoming seasons.\n \n \n\n \nTwo\n-\nstep process of\n \ncrop yield estimation using weather and satellite indicators etc. is adopted, \nwhich will help in early assessment of loss \n \n \n\n \nUse of smart sampling technique through satellite data for crop cutting experiments by some \nstates has shown increased efficiency i\nn implementation. This will now be universalized.\n \n \n\n \nThe delay by some States in submission of crop yield data will now be suitably addressed using \ntechnological solutions. \n \n \n\n \nProvision has been made for earmarked administrative expenses @ 3% for \nstrengthening the \ninfrastructure and technology usage for better delivery of the Scheme.\n \n \n\n \nInsurance companies have been asked to mandatorily utilize 0.5% of"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |970", "t": "JNU", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION\nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 970\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08.02.2021\nJNU\n970. ADV. ADOOR PRAKASH:\nWill the Minister of Education be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has any proposal for changing the name of Jawaharlal\nNehru University (JNU) Delhi?\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefore; and\n(c) whether the Government has considered the same and taken any decision?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF EDUCATION\n(Shri Ramesh Pokhriyal ‘Nishank’)\n(a) No sir,\n(b) & (c) does not arise.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|5|UNSTARRED |971", "t": "Special Package for Tourism Industry", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†971 \nANSWERED ON 08.02.2021 \n \nSPECIAL PACKAGE FOR TOURISM INDUSTRY \n \n†971. SHRIMATI MALA RAJYA LAXMI SHAH:  \nSHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to announce special \npackage for tourism industry which has been affected by the \ncoronavirus crisis;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c)  the State -wise details of the reduction in the number of \ntourists in the country including Uttarakhand and Bundelkhand \nduring the last year? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) & (b):   The Government announced various fiscal and relief \nmeasures under the Atma N irbhar Bharat Package which is \nexpected to benefit the Tourism Industry.  The stimulus package \nannounced by the Government of India is outlined as under: \n The G overnment has further announced Atmanirbhar Bharat \nPackage vide which Rs .3 lakh crore collateral f ree automatic \nloan has been made available for MSMEs. The loan will have 4 -\nyear tenure and will have 12-month moratorium. \n The Government waived off PF contribution for three months \nfor organisations less than 100 pax and 90% employees earn \nbelow Rs.15000. Under Atmanirbhar Bharat package, PF \ncontribution of both employer and employee has been reduced \nto 10% each from existing 12% each for all establishments \ncovered by EPFO for the next three months i.e. up to \nSeptember 2020. \n\n Deferment of TCS upto October 2020 \n Return filing deferred for three months with no penal interest \nfor companies upto Rs.5 Crore, rest @ 9% penal interest. \nThe Central government has also given relief from various \nregulatory compliances under Income Tax A ct, companies Act \nand GST Act fo r varying period in the wake of COVID -19 crisis to \nensure business continuity and survival.  Further, RBI has \nextended moratorium on term loans till 31st December 2020. \n \n \n(c):  The state-wise details are at Annexure. \n \n******** \n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO  PART (c) OF LOK SABHA  UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. †971 ANSWERED ON 08.02.2021 REGARDING SPECIAL \nPACKAGE FOR TOURISM INDUSTRY \n \nMinistry of Tourism, Government of India compiles State/UT wise yearly \ndata on domestic tourists visits  (DTV) and Foreign Tourists Visi ts (FTV) \nwith a time lag of one year. Hence data on DTV and FTV for year 2020 is \nnot yet compiled. Further region wise data on DTV and FTV is not centrally \nmaintained by  Ministry. However, details of State/UT wise DTV and  FTV \nduring year2019 are given below: \n  \nStates/UTs DTV FTV \n2018 2019 Growth 2018 2019 Growth \nAndaman & \nNicobar \nIslands \n498279 505398 1.43% 15242 16206 6.32% \nAndhra \nPradesh \n194767874 237051508 21.71% 281083 280356 -0.26% \nArunachal \nPradesh \n512436 555639 8.43% 7653 7825 2.25% \nAssam 4710617 5447805 15.65% 15592 26878 72.38% \nBihar 33621613 33990038 1.10% 1087971 1093141 0.48% \nChandigarh 1538796 1563795 1.62% 39681 44132 11.22% \nChhattisgarh 19329501 17304506 -10.